CT.searchIndex = [{"f":"01-basics.html","n":"C++ Basics and Pointers","id":"s1","h":"How a C++ program is built","x":"How a C++ program is built main.cpp Preprocess #include pasted Compile one .o per .cpp Link join .o + libs main Compile error: a problem inside one file. Linker error: declared and called but never defined. Three stages. Know which one an error comes from. C++ is compiled to machine code before it runs: fast, but it trusts you not to make memory mistakes. #include // paste in the declarations for std::cout int main() { // execution starts here std::cout Preprocess : #include literally pastes header files in; #define does text substitution. Compile : each .cpp file becomes an object file on its own. Link : the linker joins object files and libraries into one executable and resolves calls between files. g++ -std=c++17 -Wall -Wextra main.cpp -o main ./main Habit to build now Always compile with -Wall -Wextra . The compiler warns about many of the bugs this lesson describes (uninitialized variables, unused values, signed/unsigned comparisons). Key point \"Compile error vs linker error?\" A compile error is a syntax or type problem inside one file. A linker error (\"undefined reference\") means a function was declared and called but never defined anywhere you linked. Read more C++ Primer (Lippman, Lajoie, Moo) 2.6 Defining our own data structures (PDF pages 101 to 109) 3.1 Namespace using declarations (PDF pages 111 to 112) 18.2 Namespaces (PDF pages 814 to 830)"},{"f":"01-basics.html","n":"C++ Basics and Pointers","id":"s2","h":"Variables and types","x":"Variables and types Every variable has a type that fixes how much memory it uses and what you may do with it. Type Holds Typical size bool true / false 1 byte char a character / small integer 1 byte (always, by definition) int whole number 4 bytes long long bigger whole number 8 bytes float / double decimal number 4 / 8 bytes Only char has a fixed size. When size matters use <cstdint> : std::int32_t , std::uint64_t . Initialization: always give a variable a value int a; // BAD: indeterminate value, reading it is undefined behavior int b = 5; // copy initialization int c(5); // direct initialization int d{5}; // brace (uniform) initialization: preferred int e{}; // empty braces: zero-initialized, e == 0 int f{3.7}; // ERROR: braces forbid narrowing double -> int auto g = 5; // g is int: the compiler deduces the type const int h = 9;// cannot be changed after initialization Undefined behavior An uninitialized local holds garbage. Reading it is UB : junk, a crash, or \"works\" until it does not. The signed/unsigned trap unsigned wraps instead of going negative, and a mixed comparison converts the signed side to unsigned. Predict What does this print? std::cout It prints 0 : -1 becomes 4294967295. Beware comparing an int index with vector::size() . Read more Programming: Principles and Practice (Stroustrup) 2.5 Assignment and initialization (PDF pages 49 to 52) 2.8 to 2.10 Type safety, conversions, type deduction with auto (PDF pages 56 to 60) Effective Modern C++ (Meyers) Item 7, Distinguish () and {} when creating objects (PDF pages 67 to 76)"},{"f":"01-basics.html","n":"C++ Basics and Pointers","id":"s3","h":"Operators and control flow","x":"Operators and control flow 7 / 2 // 3 integer division truncates 7 % 2 // 1 remainder 7 / 2.0 // 3.5 one double operand makes the whole thing floating point if (x > 0 && y > 0) { } // && and || short-circuit: right side is skipped if not needed for (int i = 0; i Predict Prefix vs postfix int x = 5; int a = x++; // use x (5), then increment int b = ++x; // increment first, then use std::cout 5 7 7 x++ yields the old value and then increments, so a is 5 and x becomes 6. ++x increments first (x becomes 7) and yields the new value, so b is 7. Classic bug if (x = 5) assigns 5 to x and is always true. You meant == . The compiler warns with -Wall . Read more C++ Primer (Lippman, Lajoie, Moo) 4.4 to 4.5 Assignment, increment and decrement (PDF pages 173 to 178) 5.3 to 5.4 Conditional and iterative statements (PDF pages 203 to 218)"},{"f":"01-basics.html","n":"C++ Basics and Pointers","id":"s4","h":"Functions and passing arguments","x":"Functions and passing arguments Declare before use; the header holds declarations, the .cpp the definitions. int add(int a, int b); // declaration int add(int a, int b) { return a + b; }// definition double add(double a, double b); // overload: same name, different parameter types void greet(std::string who = \"world\"); // default argument Three ways to pass a parameter void byValue(int n) { n++; } // works on a COPY void byRef(int& n) { n++; } // works on the ORIGINAL void byConstRef(const std::string& s) { } // original, read-only, no copy You want to... Use read a small value (int, double, char, pointer) by value: int n read a large object without copying it by const reference: const std::string& s modify the caller's variable by reference: int& n accept \"no object\" as a valid argument by pointer: Widget* w , and test it for nullptr pass a run of elements std::span<int> (C++20) or const std::vector<int>& , not a pointer plus a count get a result back return it by value; big objects such as std::vector are moved, not copied, so no out-parameter is needed Predict What does this print? int a = 1; byValue(a); byRef(a); std::cout 2 . byValue incremented its own copy and threw it away. Only byRef changed a . Step through what a call does to the argument: Read more C++ Primer (Lippman, Lajoie, Moo) 6.1 Function basics (PDF pages 231 to 236) 6.2 Argument passing (PDF pages 237 to 250) 6.3 Return types and the return statement (PDF pages 251 to 258) 6.4 Overloaded functions (PDF pages 259 to 264) 6.6 Function matching (PDF pages 271 to 275) Programming: Principles and Practice (Stroustrup) 3.5 to 3.6 Functions; vector introduced (PDF pages 81 to 89) 7.2 to 7.3 Declarations and definitions; scope (PDF pages 194 to 202) 7.4 Function call and return (pass by value, by reference, by const reference) (PDF pages 203 to 218)"},{"f":"01-basics.html","n":"C++ Basics and Pointers","id":"s5","h":"Where variables live: stack, heap, static","x":"Where variables live: stack, heap, static Stack locals, parameters freed at the closing } very fast, about 1 to 8 MB int local = 1; Heap new / malloc lives until you delete it slower, huge int* p = new int(5); Static / global globals, static variables whole program lifetime fixed at startup static int calls; The lifetime rule decides where a variable should live. Region What goes there Lifetime Speed / size Stack local variables, function parameters automatic: freed when the scope { } ends very fast, small (about 1 to 8 MB) Heap (free store) objects created with new / malloc manual: lives until you delete it slower, huge Static / global globals, static variables whole program fixed at startup int counter = 0; // static storage: exists for the whole program void f() { int local = 1; // stack: created on entry, destroyed on exit static int calls = 0; // static storage, but only visible inside f ++calls; int* p = new int(5); // the int lives on the HEAP; p itself is on the stack delete p; } Mental model Stack: a pile of plates, popped automatically. Heap: a storage unit you rent until you hand the key back. Read more Programming: Principles and Practice (Stroustrup) 7.2 to 7.3 Declarations and definitions; scope (PDF pages 194 to 202)"},{"f":"01-basics.html","n":"C++ Basics and Pointers","id":"s6","h":"Pointers from scratch","x":"Pointers from scratch A pointer is a variable whose value is an address . Syntax Meaning int* p declare p as \"pointer to int\" &x address-of: the address of x *p dereference: the thing p points to nullptr the pointer that points to nothing Step through it (addresses are made up): Pointers and arrays An array name decays to a pointer to its first element; p + 1 moves one element . int a[3] = {10, 20, 30}; int* p = a; // same as &a[0] std::cout Careful Never dereference nullptr and never read past the end of an array. Both are undefined behavior. C++ will not stop you. What a pointer does not know Only an address and a type: not how many elements, nor who frees them. Root of most pointer bugs. Key point \"What is the difference between NULL and nullptr ?\" NULL is a macro for a null pointer constant, usually plain 0 (an integer), so f(NULL) can call f(int) instead of f(char*) . nullptr has its own type and only converts to pointers. Read more C++ Primer (Lippman, Lajoie, Moo) 2.3 Compound types (PDF pages 79 to 87) Programming: Principles and Practice (Stroustrup) 15.3 to 15.5 Memory, addresses and pointers; free store; destructors (PDF pages 452 to 463)"},{"f":"01-basics.html","n":"C++ Basics and Pointers","id":"s7","h":"References","x":"References A reference is an alias : another name for an existing variable, not a copy. int x = 1; int& r = x; // r is another name for x r = 5; // x is now 5 Pointer int* Reference int& Can be null yes no (must refer to something real) Must be initialized no (but should be) yes, at declaration Can be re-pointed yes no, bound for life Syntax to use *p , p->member just use the name Use for optional things, arrays, ownership, C APIs function parameters, aliases Predict The reference trap int a = 1, b = 2; int& r = a; r = b; std::cout 22 r = b does not make r refer to b . References cannot be re-bound. It assigns the value of b into the variable r aliases, so a becomes 2. Step through the reference trap: Read more C++ Primer (Lippman, Lajoie, Moo) 2.3 Compound types (PDF pages 79 to 87) Programming: Principles and Practice (Stroustrup) 16.1 to 16.2 Arrays, pointers and references (PDF pages 477 to 483)"},{"f":"01-basics.html","n":"C++ Basics and Pointers","id":"s8","h":"const and pointers","x":"const and pointers const int* p data locked p p can move int (read-only) *p = 5 is an error int* const p pointer locked p p cannot move int (changeable) *p = 5 is fine Read right to left. Red = locked, green = free. const before the star protects the data; after the star it protects the pointer. const = \"I will not modify this\". With pointers, either the data or the pointer can be const. Read right to left. int x = 1, y = 2; const int* p1 = // \"p1 is a pointer to const int\" // *p1 = 5; // ERROR: cannot change the int through p1 p1 = // OK: the pointer itself may move int* const p2 = // \"p2 is a const pointer to int\" *p2 = 5; // OK: the int may change // p2 = // ERROR: the pointer may not move const int* const p3 = // neither can change Make everything const that can be. Read more C++ Primer (Lippman, Lajoie, Moo) 2.4 const qualifier (PDF pages 88 to 95)"},{"f":"01-basics.html","n":"C++ Basics and Pointers","id":"s9","h":"Dynamic memory: new and delete","x":"Dynamic memory: new and delete new allocates on the heap and constructs; you must delete it or it leaks . int* one = new int(42); delete one; // single object: new / delete int* arr = new int[100]; delete[] arr; // array: new[] / delete[] (must match!) Bug What happened Memory leak you lost the pointer (or forgot delete ) so the memory is never freed Dangling pointer you used a pointer after the memory was freed (see the step-through above) Double delete you freed the same memory twice: undefined behavior, often a crash Mismatch new[] freed with delete , or the reverse Modern C++ Avoid raw new / delete : use std::vector and std::unique_ptr ( Smart Pointers ). Key point \"What does new do beyond malloc ?\" It calls the constructor, throws std::bad_alloc on failure (instead of returning null), and returns a correctly typed pointer. delete calls the destructor and then frees the memory. Read more C++ Primer (Lippman, Lajoie, Moo) 12.2 Dynamic arrays (PDF pages 505 to 512) Programming: Principles and Practice (Stroustrup) 15.3 to 15.5 Memory, addresses and pointers; free store; destructors (PDF pages 452 to 463)"},{"f":"01-basics.html","n":"C++ Basics and Pointers","id":"s10","h":"Arrays, C-strings and std::string","x":"Arrays, C-strings and std::string int a[5] = {1, 2, 3}; // fixed size, remaining elements are 0, NO bounds checking a[10] = 7; // compiles, undefined behavior at runtime void f(int arr[]) { // this is really `int* arr`: the size is lost! sizeof(arr); // size of a POINTER (8), not of the array } char s[] = \"hi\"; // 3 chars: 'h', 'i', and a terminating '\\0' Prefer: Instead of Use Why int a[N] std::array<int, N> knows its size, no decay, same speed new int[n] std::vector<int> grows, frees itself, .at(i) bounds-checks char* strings std::string owns its memory, safe copy, + , .size() , .find() pointer plus a count std::span<int> (C++20) a non-owning view that remembers its size; accepts an array or a vector without copying std::string s = \"hello\"; s += \" world\"; // grows automatically std::cout v = {1, 2, 3}; v.push_back(4); // size is now 4 Read more C++ Primer (Lippman, Lajoie, Moo) 3.2 Library string type (PDF pages 113 to 124) 3.5 Arrays (PDF pages 142 to 153) 3.6 Multidimensional arrays (PDF pages 154 to 161) Programming: Principles and Practice (Stroustrup) 16.3 to 16.4 C-style strings; alternatives to pointer use (PDF pages 484 to 487)"},{"f":"01-basics.html","n":"C++ Basics and Pointers","id":"s11","h":"The classic memory bugs","x":"The classic memory bugs Most \"mysterious\" crashes are one of these. Bug Example Uninitialized read int n; use(n); Out-of-bounds int a[3]; a[3] = 1; (valid indices are 0 to 2) Null dereference int* p = nullptr; *p = 1; Use after free delete p; *p = 1; Signed overflow INT_MAX + 1 (UB for signed; wraps for unsigned) Returning a reference to a local int& bad() { int local = 42; return local; // BUG: local is destroyed when bad() returns } // the caller gets a dangling reference int good() { int local = 42; return local; // fine: returns a copy of the value } Tooling Test with -fsanitize=address,undefined : it reports leaks, out-of-bounds, use-after-free and overflow with the exact line."},{"f":"01-basics.html","n":"C++ Basics and Pointers","id":"tooling","h":"Tooling: warnings, sanitizers, static analysis","x":"Tooling: warnings, sanitizers, static analysis C++ trusts you, so let tools check what the language will not. Use them in layers, cheapest first. Layer What it catches How Compiler warnings many beginner mistakes at once: uninitialized variables, signed/unsigned comparisons, missing returns -Wall -Wextra , and -Werror in CI Sanitizers what only shows at run time: out-of-bounds, use after free, leaks, undefined behavior, data races -fsanitize=address,undefined (and -fsanitize=thread for threads) Static analyzer rule violations, null dereferences, leaks and dead code found by reading the source, without running it clang-tidy , cppcheck Formatter style, so reviews are about logic clang-format g++ -std=c++17 -Wall -Wextra -g -fsanitize=address,undefined main.cpp -o main ./main // a bug now stops with the exact line, not with a mystery crash later A clean report is not a proof Analyzers give false positives and miss things, and a sanitizer only checks the paths your test actually ran. They reduce risk; they do not replace design, review and tests. Coding standards Safety-critical projects (cars, medical devices) add written rules such as MISRA C++ or the AUTOSAR C++14 guidelines, checked automatically. The common themes are the ones in this course: initialize everything, no C-style casts, no naked new / delete , no silent narrowing, and no using namespace in headers."},{"f":"01-basics.html","n":"C++ Basics and Pointers","id":"s12","h":"Cheat sheet","x":"Cheat sheet Answer aloud, then open. Pointer vs reference? A pointer holds an address, can be null, can be re-pointed, and needs * to use. A reference is a permanent alias that must be initialized and cannot be null. Use references for parameters, pointers when \"nothing\" is a valid value or you need to re-point. Stack vs heap? The stack holds locals and is freed automatically when the scope ends; it is fast but small. The heap holds new -allocated objects that live until you free them; it is large but slower and you must manage the lifetime (or let a smart pointer do it). What is a dangling pointer? A pointer to memory that has been freed or has gone out of scope. Dereferencing it is undefined behavior. Setting a pointer to nullptr after delete helps, and smart pointers avoid the problem. const int* vs int* const ? Read right to left. const int* is a pointer to a const int (data is read-only, pointer can move). int* const is a const pointer to an int (pointer is fixed, data can change). delete vs delete[] ? Memory from new must be freed with delete ; memory from new[] must be freed with delete[] so every element's destructor runs. Mixing them is undefined behavior. Deleting nullptr is safe and does nothing. What is undefined behavior? Code the standard places no requirements on: the compiler may assume it never happens, so anything can occur (crash, wrong output, or apparently fine). Examples: out-of-bounds access, null dereference, signed overflow, reading uninitialized memory, data races. How do you pass a large object to a function efficiently? By const reference if the function only reads it, to avoid a copy. By non-const reference if it must modify the caller's object. Keep going Next lesson: Types, Initialization and Casts . Your own notes: Module 1: C++ Basics and the learncpp.com recaps (chapters 1 to 13 and 19). Reference: modern-cpp-features and the modern-cpp-tutorial ."},{"f":"16-types-and-casts.html","n":"Types, Initialization and Casts","id":"init","h":"Initialization and brace traps","x":"Initialization and brace traps int a; // default-initialized: INDETERMINATE value, never read it int b{}; // value-initialized: zero int c = 5; // copy-initialized int d(5); // direct-initialized int e{5}; // list-initialized: the preferred form, rejects narrowing // int f{3.7}; // ERROR: narrowing double -> int inside braces int g = 3.7; // compiles: silently truncates to 3 Kind Example Notes aggregate Point p{1, 2}; no constructors: members set in order designated (C++20) Point p{.x = 3, .y = 4}; names the members, order must match value-initialize Point p{}; every member zero Braces prefer initializer_list std::vector v1(3, 7); // parentheses: 3 elements, each 7 std::vector v2{3, 7}; // braces: the initializer_list constructor wins -> the two elements 3 and 7 If a class has an initializer_list constructor, {...} picks it even when another constructor matches better (compiled: Widget w{1, 2} called the list constructor, Widget w(1, 2) the (int, int) one). Empty braces {} call the default constructor. Initialize everything. An uninitialized local is indeterminate ; reading it is undefined behaviour. Braces catch accidental narrowing at compile time: a free safety check. A static_cast in braces ( int{x} ) is a conversion that must not narrow. Read more C++ Primer (Lippman, Lajoie, Moo) 2.2 Variables (PDF pages 70 to 78)"},{"f":"16-types-and-casts.html","n":"Types, Initialization and Casts","id":"deduction","h":"auto , decltype and modern syntax","x":"auto , decltype and modern syntax int x = 0; const int& r = x; auto a = r; // int auto drops const and the reference decltype(r) b = x; // const int& decltype gives the exact declared type decltype(auto) c = r; // const int& decltype(auto) = \"deduce like auto, but keep it exact\" auto t = {1, 2, 3}; // std::initializer_list , NOT a vector auto traps auto y = v[0]; copies : write const auto& to avoid it. Same for for (auto [k, v] : map) (a copy of each pair). auto hides what a function returns: fine for iterators and lambdas, poor for public interfaces. Feature Instead of Example Range-based for index / iterator loops for (const auto& x : v) {} nullptr NULL / 0 int* p = nullptr; using alias typedef using Map = std::map<int, std::string>; override / final hoping you matched the signature void f() override; = default / = delete empty bodies, private unimplemented copy Foo(const Foo&) = delete; static_assert runtime checks of compile-time facts static_assert(sizeof(int) == 4); Structured bindings (C++17) .first / .second for (auto& [key, val] : m) {} if with initializer (C++17) variable leaking into outer scope if (auto it = m.find(k); it != m.end()) {} [[nodiscard]] ignored error codes [[nodiscard]] bool open(); Read more C++ Primer (Lippman, Lajoie, Moo) 2.5 Dealing with types (PDF pages 96 to 100) Programming: Principles and Practice (Stroustrup) 2.8 to 2.10 Type safety, conversions, type deduction with auto (PDF pages 56 to 60) Effective Modern C++ (Meyers) Item 2, Understand auto type deduction (PDF pages 36 to 41) Item 5, Prefer auto to explicit type declarations (PDF pages 55 to 61)"},{"f":"16-types-and-casts.html","n":"Types, Initialization and Casts","id":"conversions","h":"Implicit conversions and enum class","x":"Implicit conversions and enum class The compiler converts silently between arithmetic types. Compiled results of the classic surprises: Expression Result Why -1 < 1u false the signed value is converted to unsigned and becomes huge uint8_t(200) + uint8_t(100) 300, stored back as 44 small types are promoted to int before arithmetic; storing back wraps 7 / 2 3 integer division; 7 / 2.0 is 3.5 static_cast<int>(-3.9) -3 conversion to an integer truncates toward zero char 0xFF widened to int -1 (signed char ) whether plain char is signed is implementation-defined Usual arithmetic conversions: smaller than int becomes int ; mixing signed and unsigned of the same rank converts to unsigned; int with double becomes double . Never compare signed with unsigned ( -Wsign-compare ); use std::ssize or cast deliberately. Single-argument constructors are implicit conversions: mark them explicit ( OOP lesson ). enum Color { Red, Green }; // plain enum: leaks names into the scope, converts to int silently int n = Green; // compiles enum class Level { Info = 1, Error = 2 };// scoped: Level::Error, no implicit conversion int m = static_cast (Level::Error); // an explicit cast is required (compiled: 2) Prefer enum class , with an explicit underlying type when the size matters: enum class Side : std::uint8_t { Buy, Sell }; Read more C++ Primer (Lippman, Lajoie, Moo) 2.1 Primitive built-in types (PDF pages 61 to 69) 4.11 Type conversions (PDF pages 188 to 194) 19.3 Enumerations (PDF pages 861 to 863) Programming: Principles and Practice (Stroustrup) 8.4 to 8.6 Evolving a class (Date), enumerations, operator overloading (PDF pages 238 to 249)"},{"f":"16-types-and-casts.html","n":"Types, Initialization and Casts","id":"casts","h":"Casts and type conversion","x":"Casts and type conversion Four named casts replace the C cast (T)x : each states intent and is searchable. Cast Use for Danger static_cast<T>(x) well-defined conversions: int to double , base to derived pointer when you are sure low dynamic_cast<T>(x) checked downcast in a polymorphic hierarchy. Returns nullptr (pointer) or throws std::bad_cast (reference) on failure runtime cost; base needs a virtual function const_cast<T>(x) add or remove const (legacy APIs) modifying a truly const object is UB reinterpret_cast<T>(x) reinterpret raw bits (pointer to integer, unrelated pointer types) very high, rarely correct double avg = static_cast (total) / count; // avoids integer division Animal* a = getAnimal(); if (auto* d = dynamic_cast (a)) { // safe downcast: null if it is not a Dog d->fetch(); } Rule Many casts mean a design problem. Prefer virtual functions to dynamic_cast chains; no C casts. Read more C++ Primer (Lippman, Lajoie, Moo) 4.11 Type conversions (PDF pages 188 to 194)"},{"f":"16-types-and-casts.html","n":"Types, Initialization and Casts","id":"const","h":"const , constexpr and static","x":"const , constexpr and static const : unchangeable after initialization. Use everywhere. constexpr : computable at compile time (array sizes, template arguments), and still callable at runtime. constexpr int square(int x) { return x * x; } constexpr int N = square(4); // 16, computed by the compiler std::array a; // size 9: legal because it is a constant expression int runtime = readInput(); int r = square(runtime); // also fine: same function runs at runtime static_assert(N == 16, \"math is broken\"); // checked at compile time static means three things: Where Meaning Local variable in a function one instance that lives for the whole program and keeps its value between calls Global / namespace scope internal linkage: visible only inside this .cpp file Class member belongs to the class, shared by all objects Linkage and extern \"C\" C++ mangles function names (it encodes the parameter types so that overloads get different symbols); C does not. To call C code, or to be called by it, tell the compiler to use C linkage: extern \"C\" int compareInts(const void* a, const void* b); // an unmangled symbol, callable from C std::qsort(arr, 5, sizeof(int), compareInts); // a C API taking a callback (compiled: sorted 12345) extern \"C\" { // a whole header of C declarations #include \"legacy.h\" } An extern \"C\" function cannot be overloaded and cannot be a template or a member. Pointers to extern \"C\" functions are technically a different type from C++ function pointers; compilers accept them for C callbacks in practice. Linkage rules: a non- static , non- inline function defined in a header and included twice gives a \"multiple definition\" link error; mark it inline . extern on a variable declares it without defining it. Read more C++ Primer (Lippman, Lajoie, Moo) 6.5 Features for specialized uses (PDF pages 265 to 270) Effective Modern C++ (Meyers) Item 15, Use constexpr whenever possible (PDF pages 115 to 121)"},{"f":"16-types-and-casts.html","n":"Types, Initialization and Casts","id":"standards","h":"What C++14, 17 and 20 added","x":"What C++14, 17 and 20 added A map of which standard added what, so you know which flag a feature needs: Standard Feature One-liner C++11 move semantics, smart pointers, lambdas, auto , constexpr , std::thread , variadic templates, nullptr , range-for The big modernisation. C++14 generic lambdas, make_unique , return type deduction, binary literals 0b101 Polishes C++11. C++17 std::optional , std::variant , std::string_view , structured bindings, if constexpr , fold expressions, std::filesystem , parallel algorithms, std::scoped_lock , CTAD Vocabulary types and compile-time branching. C++20 concepts, ranges, std::span , operator<=> , coroutines, modules, std::jthread , std::format Constraints on templates, composable algorithms. The C++17 and C++20 vocabulary types ( optional , variant , span , string_view ) have their own lesson: Vocabulary Types . Full lists: modern-cpp-features , modern-cpp-tutorial . Your notes: Module 3 ."},{"f":"16-types-and-casts.html","n":"Types, Initialization and Casts","id":"cheat","h":"Cheat sheet","x":"Cheat sheet Answer aloud, then open. When would you use dynamic_cast ? For a checked downcast from a polymorphic base to a derived type when you cannot express the need through a virtual function. It returns null (pointers) or throws bad_cast (references) on failure. Frequent use suggests a design problem. The four named casts? static_cast for well-defined conversions, dynamic_cast for checked downcasts, const_cast to add or remove const (modifying a truly const object is UB), reinterpret_cast to reinterpret bits (rarely correct). Never a C cast: it can silently pick any of them. Difference between const and constexpr ? const means read-only after initialization (the value may be determined at runtime). constexpr means the value or function can be evaluated at compile time and used in constant expressions. What does auto deduce, and what are its traps? The type of the initializer with top-level const and references dropped. auto x = {1,2,3} is an initializer_list . auto y = v[0] copies; use const auto& . decltype(expr) keeps the exact type. What is wrong with if (-1 < 1u) ? The signed operand is converted to unsigned, becoming a huge value, so the comparison is false. Avoid mixing signed and unsigned; compile with -Wsign-compare . Why prefer enum class ? Its names are scoped ( Level::Error ) so they do not pollute the enclosing scope, and it does not convert implicitly to an integer. You can also fix its underlying type. std::vector<int> v{3, 7} versus v(3, 7) ? Braces prefer the initializer_list constructor: two elements, 3 and 7. Parentheses call the (count, value) constructor: three elements, each 7. What are the three meanings of static ? A local variable that lives for the whole program and keeps its value between calls; internal linkage for a global (visible only in this .cpp ); a class member shared by all objects. Which C++17 features do you use? std::optional , std::variant , std::string_view , structured bindings, if constexpr , if with initializer, fold expressions and [[nodiscard]] : vocabulary types and clearer code."},{"f":"02-oop.html","n":"Object-Oriented Programming in C++","id":"s1","h":"Classes and encapsulation","x":"Classes and encapsulation private: only the class (and friends) protected: plus derived classes public: everyone balance_ (the data) name_ in a base withdraw(), balance() Hide the data, expose operations that keep the invariant true. A class bundles data with the functions that use it. Encapsulation : keep data private, expose only operations that keep it valid. class BankAccount { public: // the interface other code may use explicit BankAccount(double opening) : balance_(opening) {} bool withdraw(double amount) { if (amount balance_) return false; // invariant protected balance_ -= amount; return true; } double balance() const { return balance_; } private: // the implementation, hidden double balance_; }; BankAccount acc{100.0}; acc.withdraw(30); // fine // acc.balance_ = 1e9; // ERROR: private, nobody can break the invariant Specifier Who can access public everyone protected the class itself and classes derived from it private only the class itself (and its friends) \"Valid\" is the class invariant (here: balance never negative). Hide the representation, let constructors build only valid objects, keep the public interface small. No invariant to protect? Use a plain struct . struct vs class Only the default access differs: struct public, class private. Use struct for plain data. Read more C++ Primer (Lippman, Lajoie, Moo) 7.1 Defining abstract data types (PDF pages 283 to 296) 7.2 Access control and encapsulation (PDF pages 297 to 299) 7.4 Class scope (PDF pages 311 to 316) Programming: Principles and Practice (Stroustrup) 8.2, 8.3 Classes and members, interface and implementation (PDF pages 236 to 238) 8.7 Class interfaces (PDF pages 250 to 258)"},{"f":"02-oop.html","n":"Object-Oriented Programming in C++","id":"s4","h":"Member functions: this , const , static , friend","x":"Member functions: this , const , static , friend class Counter { int n_ = 0; static int instances_; // ONE copy shared by all Counter objects public: Counter() { ++instances_; } ~Counter() { --instances_; } Counter& add(int k) { n_ += k; return *this; } // `this` is a pointer to the current object int get() const { return n_; } // const: promises not to modify the object static int instances() { return instances_; } // no `this`: callable as Counter::instances() friend std::ostream& operator const member function : callable on const objects, cannot modify members (except mutable ). Make getters const. static member function : belongs to the class, no this . friend : access to privates. Use sparingly. Read more C++ Primer (Lippman, Lajoie, Moo) 7.1 Defining abstract data types (PDF pages 283 to 296) 7.3 Additional class features (PDF pages 300 to 310) 7.6 static class members (PDF pages 329 to 334) Programming: Principles and Practice (Stroustrup) 15.6 to 15.8 Element access, an example list, the this pointer (PDF pages 464 to 473) C++ Move Semantics (Josuttis) Chapter 5 Overloading on reference qualifiers (PDF pages 97 to 106)"},{"f":"02-oop.html","n":"Object-Oriented Programming in C++","id":"s2","h":"Constructors and destructors","x":"Constructors and destructors A constructor sets an object up; the destructor ( ~Name() ) runs automatically when its lifetime ends. This is the basis of RAII. class Widget { const int id_; std::string name_; public: Widget() : Widget(0) {} // delegating constructor Widget(int id, std::string n = \"none\") : id_(id), name_(std::move(n)) {} // member initializer list ~Widget() { /* release resources here */ } }; Use the initializer list : it initializes members directly. const and reference members can only be set there. Members initialize in declaration order , not list order ( -Wreorder ). explicit on a one-argument constructor blocks silent conversions. Order of construction and destruction Construction: base, members, derived body. Destruction: the exact reverse. Key point Construct: base, members, derived body. Destruct: reverse. Read more C++ Primer (Lippman, Lajoie, Moo) 7.5 Constructors revisited (PDF pages 317 to 328) 15.7 Constructors and copy control (PDF pages 651 to 658) Programming: Principles and Practice (Stroustrup) 15.3 to 15.5 Memory, addresses and pointers; free store; destructors (PDF pages 452 to 463)"},{"f":"02-oop.html","n":"Object-Oriented Programming in C++","id":"s3","h":"Copying and the Rule of 3, 5 and 0","x":"Copying and the Rule of 3, 5 and 0 The compiler generates copy operations that copy every member. For a raw pointer that copies only the address : a shallow copy , and a bug: Fix: a deep copy . Rule of Three : a custom destructor, copy constructor or copy assignment means you need all three. class Buffer { int* data_; public: Buffer(int v) : data_(new int(v)) {} ~Buffer() { delete data_; } // 1. destructor Buffer(const Buffer& o) : data_(new int(*o.data_)) {} // 2. copy constructor (deep) Buffer& operator=(const Buffer& o) { // 3. copy assignment (deep) if (this != &o) { *data_ = *o.data_; } // guard against a = a return *this; } }; Rule What you write When Rule of 0 none of them The goal. Hold resources in members that manage themselves: std::vector , std::string , std::unique_ptr . Rule of 3 destructor, copy ctor, copy assign The class owns a raw resource and must define copying. Rule of 5 the 3 plus move ctor and move assign Same, and you also want cheap moves (see Move Semantics ). class NoCopy { public: NoCopy() = default; NoCopy(const NoCopy&) = delete; // forbid copying outright NoCopy& operator=(const NoCopy&) = delete; }; What the compiler still generates once you declare something You declare Copying Moving nothing generated generated a destructor, a copy constructor or a copy assignment still generated (but deprecated) not generated : an rvalue silently falls back to a copy a move constructor or a move assignment disabled only what you declared A class can quietly lose its moves (a later logging destructor turns every move into a copy). Ask for defaults with = default ; a polymorphic base needs them defaulted too. Never = delete a move : it kills the copy fallback too. For a move-only type, delete the copy operations. In short \"Rule of Zero: vector and smart pointers make the generated members correct. Rule of Five only when wrapping a raw resource.\" Read more C++ Primer (Lippman, Lajoie, Moo) 13.1 Copy, assign and destroy (PDF pages 525 to 538) 13.2 Copy control and resource management (PDF pages 539 to 544) 13.5 Classes that manage dynamic memory (PDF pages 553 to 559) 15.7 Constructors and copy control (PDF pages 651 to 658) Programming: Principles and Practice (Stroustrup) 17.4 to 17.5 Copying and moving; essential operations (PDF pages 501 to 512) Effective Modern C++ (Meyers) Item 17, Understand special member function generation (PDF pages 127 to 133) C++ Move Semantics (Josuttis) 3.1 and 3.1.1 to 3.1.2 Move semantics in ordinary classes (PDF pages 53 to 58) 3.2 Implementing the special copy and move member functions (PDF pages 58 to 66) 3.3 to 3.4 Rules for special member functions; the Rule of Five or Three (PDF pages 66 to 76)"},{"f":"02-oop.html","n":"Object-Oriented Programming in C++","id":"conversions","h":"explicit , conversion operators and copy-and-swap","x":"explicit , conversion operators and copy-and-swap struct Meters { double v; Meters(double x) : v(x) {} }; // implicit: a double silently becomes Meters struct Feet { double v; explicit Feet(double x) : v(x) {} }; // explicit: must be written out takeMeters(3.0); // compiles: surprising // takeFeet(3.0); // ERROR takeFeet(Feet(3.0)); // OK struct Handle { int fd; explicit operator bool() const { return fd >= 0; } // a conversion operator, but explicit }; if (Handle h{3}) { } // OK: an if is a \"contextual\" conversion to bool // int n = h + 1; // ERROR: no implicit conversion to bool or int Mark single-argument constructors explicit unless an implicit conversion is clearly wanted (compiled: double converts to Meters but not to Feet ). explicit operator bool is the safe way to say \"valid\": it works in if , while , && and ! , but not in arithmetic or in comparisons with other types (the old \"safe bool idiom\" is obsolete). The standard streams and std::optional use it. A non-explicit operator int() lets your class take part in arithmetic and overload resolution in ways you did not plan. Copy-and-swap class Buffer { std::size_t n_; int* p_; public: Buffer(const Buffer& o); // deep copy (written once) friend void swap(Buffer& a, Buffer& b) noexcept { std::swap(a.n_, b.n_); std::swap(a.p_, b.p_); } Buffer& operator=(Buffer other) noexcept { // by VALUE: the copy is made before we touch *this swap(*this, other); // steal the copy's resources return *this; // `other` dies and frees our OLD resources } }; Strong exception guarantee: if the copy throws, it happens before *this is touched. Self-assignment safe without an if (this != &o) check (compiled: b1 = b1 worked). One operator serves copy and move assignment ( Move Semantics ). Cost: a move-assignment written by hand can be a little faster. Read more C++ Primer (Lippman, Lajoie, Moo) 7.5 Constructors revisited (PDF pages 317 to 328) 13.3 Swap (PDF pages 545 to 547) 14.9 Overloading, conversions and operators (PDF pages 608 to 619)"},{"f":"02-oop.html","n":"Object-Oriented Programming in C++","id":"s8","h":"Operator overloading","x":"Operator overloading Overload operators only when the meaning is obvious ( + on vectors, == on values, << to print). struct Vec2 { double x{}, y{}; Vec2& operator+=(const Vec2& o) { x += o.x; y += o.y; return *this; } // member: modifies *this bool operator==(const Vec2& o) const { return x == o.x && y == o.y; } }; Vec2 operator+(Vec2 a, const Vec2& b) { return a += b; } // non-member built from += std::ostream& operator Non-mutating binary operators ( + , == ): non-member , so both sides convert. += : member. Keep usual semantics: == with != , < a strict weak ordering. C++20 <=> generates all comparisons. No new operators; :: . .* ?: cannot be overloaded. Callable objects struct Greater { // a functor: an object you can call like a function bool operator()(int a, int b) const { return a > b; } }; std::sort(v.begin(), v.end(), Greater{}); // a lambda is a compiler-made functor operator() makes a class callable (the idea behind lambdas, std::function and comparators). Conversion operators, and why to mark them explicit , are covered earlier, in explicit, conversion operators and copy-and-swap . User-defined literals constexpr long long operator\"\"_km(unsigned long long v) { return static_cast (v) * 1000; } // kilometres to metres constexpr double operator\"\"_pct(long double v) { return static_cast (v) / 100.0; } // percent to a fraction auto dist = 4_km; // 4000 (compiled) auto rate = 25.0_pct; // 0.25 using namespace std::literals; auto name = \"abc\"s; // std::string; also 5ms, 2s (chrono) and \"view\"sv A literal suffix is an operator you define: operator\"\"_name . User suffixes must start with an underscore (the others are reserved for the standard library). Use them for units (kilometres, percent, bytes) so the meaning is in the code, not in a comment. Read more C++ Primer (Lippman, Lajoie, Moo) 14.1 to 14.7 Basic concepts through member access (PDF pages 581 to 599) Programming: Principles and Practice (Stroustrup) 8.4 to 8.6 Evolving a class (Date), enumerations, operator overloading (PDF pages 238 to 249)"},{"f":"02-oop.html","n":"Object-Oriented Programming in C++","id":"s5","h":"Inheritance","x":"Inheritance Public inheritance models is-a : a Dog works anywhere an Animal is expected. class Animal { public: explicit Animal(std::string name) : name_(std::move(name)) {} const std::string& name() const { return name_; } protected: std::string name_; // visible to Dog, hidden from the outside world }; class Dog : public Animal { public: explicit Dog(std::string name) : Animal(std::move(name)) {} // call the base constructor void fetch() const { std::cout Object slicing Animal a = Dog(\"Rex\"); chops off the Dog part and its virtual behaviour. Use pointers or references. final forbids deriving. Prefer composition unless it is truly is-a. Step through slicing: Read more C++ Primer (Lippman, Lajoie, Moo) 15.1 to 15.2 OOP overview, defining base and derived classes (PDF pages 621 to 631) 15.5 Access control and inheritance (PDF pages 640 to 645) Programming: Principles and Practice (Stroustrup) 12.3, 12.5 Base and derived classes; benefits of object-oriented programming (PDF pages 380 to 387 and 389 to 390) C++ Move Semantics (Josuttis) 4.4 Move semantics in class hierarchies (PDF pages 93 to 96)"},{"f":"02-oop.html","n":"Object-Oriented Programming in C++","id":"s6","h":"Polymorphism and virtual functions","x":"Polymorphism and virtual functions p->speak() through a base pointer reaches the derived behaviour only if speak is virtual . Without it the static type (the type of the pointer, known at compile time) decides; with it the dynamic type (the type of the object, known at run time) decides. Try it: How it works: vtable and vptr The compiler builds one vtable per class that has virtual functions: an array of function pointers, one slot per virtual function. A slot holds the most derived override of that function. A derived class's vtable starts as a copy of its base's, and every function the derived class overrides replaces the matching slot. Every object of such a class carries one hidden pointer, the vptr , normally at the start of the object, set to its class's vtable by the constructor. Dog object vptr legs (from Animal) tricks (Dog) Animal vtable speak: Animal::speak eat: Animal::eat ~Animal: Animal::~Animal Dog vtable speak: Dog::speak eat: Animal::eat ~Animal: Dog::~Dog green: overridden plain: inherited (same function as base) One vtable per class, shared by all its objects; one vptr per object. Only the overridden slots differ. Cost. A class with virtual functions has a vptr in every object: struct Plain { int legs; } is 4 bytes, the same struct with one virtual function is 16 on a typical 64-bit platform (8 for the vptr, 4 for legs , 4 of padding). The data members come after the vptr. The call. p->speak() compiles to three steps: load the vptr from the object, index the vtable at the slot number that speak has in Animal , and jump to the address found there. The slot number is fixed at compile time because every derived vtable keeps the base's slot order. That is also why virtual calls usually cannot be inlined. Only virtual functions have a slot. Non-virtual functions are called directly, chosen by the static type. A virtual destructor takes a slot too. That is how delete p through an Animal* runs Dog::~Dog . A pure virtual function ( = 0 ) still has a slot, but it holds no useful code; that is why the class cannot be instantiated. Step through a virtual call. Notice that eat goes through the same mechanism but lands in the base's function, because Dog did not override it: Writing it: override , final and a virtual destructor struct Shape { virtual double area() const = 0; virtual ~Shape() = default; // ALWAYS virtual in a polymorphic base }; struct Circle : Shape { double r; explicit Circle(double r) : r(r) {} double area() const override { return 3.14159 * r * r; } // `override`: the compiler checks you really override }; struct Ball final : Circle { // `final` on a class: nothing can derive from it using Circle::Circle; }; // `final` on a function stops further overriding: double area() const final; Two famous traps 1. Always write override . If you mistype the signature (forget const ), without it you silently create a new function instead of overriding. 2. Virtual calls inside a constructor or destructor do not reach the derived class. The constructor of Base sets the vptr to Base 's vtable, and only after it finishes does the constructor of Derived set it to Derived 's vtable. So while Base 's constructor runs, the object's dynamic type is Base , and Base::init() is called even if Derived overrides it. A destructor does the same in reverse. Overloading, overriding and hiding Term Meaning Overloading same name, different parameters, resolved at compile time Overriding a derived class replaces a virtual base function with the same signature, resolved at runtime Hiding a derived function with the same name (but not virtual / different signature) hides every base function of that name struct B { void f(int); void g(); }; struct D : B { void f(double); }; // hides B::f(int), it does not overload it D d; d.f(1); // calls D::f(double): the int overload of B is not even considered d.g(); // fine: D has no g, so B::g is found d.B::f(1); // explicit qualification reaches the hidden one // or add `using B::f;` inside D to bring B's overloads back into D's scope Multiple base classes, dynamic_cast and the exact object layout are in The Object Model in Depth . Read more C++ Primer (Lippman, Lajoie, Moo) 15.3 Virtual functions (PDF pages 632 to 636) 15.6 Class scope under inheritance (PDF pages 646 to 650) 15.8 Containers and inheritance (PDF pages 659 to 662) Effective Modern C++ (Meyers) Item 12, Declare overriding functions override (PDF pages 97 to 104) C++ Move Semantics (Josuttis) 4.4 Move semantics in class hierarchies (PDF pages 93 to 96)"},{"f":"02-oop.html","n":"Object-Oriented Programming in C++","id":"s7","h":"Abstract classes and interfaces","x":"Abstract classes and interfaces = 0 makes a function pure virtual ; a class with one is abstract (cannot be instantiated). Only pure virtuals and no data: an interface . struct Logger { // interface virtual void log(const std::string& msg) = 0; virtual ~Logger() = default; }; struct ConsoleLogger : Logger { void log(const std::string& msg) override { std::cout > shapes; shapes.push_back(std::make_unique (2.0)); for (const auto& s : shapes) std::cout area() Read more C++ Primer (Lippman, Lajoie, Moo) 15.4 Abstract base classes (PDF pages 637 to 639)"},{"f":"02-oop.html","n":"Object-Oriented Programming in C++","id":"s9","h":"Composition, relationships and design","x":"Composition, relationships and design Animal Dog is-a (inherits) Car Engine owns: dies with Car Team Player uses: lives on its own Doctor Patient association (a pointer) Prefer composition; inheritance is the strongest coupling. \"Is it a, or does it have a?\" Prefer composition unless you need polymorphism. Relationship Meaning C++ shape Lifetime Inheritance (is-a) Dog is an Animal class Dog : public Animal one object Composition (owns) Car has an Engine, which dies with the car member Engine e; part dies with whole Aggregation (uses, shared) Team has Players who exist independently Player* / std::shared_ptr member independent Association Doctor treats Patients reference or pointer parameter independent Liskov Substitution Code using a base must keep working with any derived type. If Square : Rectangle breaks on setting the width, inheritance was the wrong model: it must mean \"behaves like\", not \"shares code\". Going deeper Multiple and virtual inheritance, the diamond problem, RTTI and private inheritance are in The Object Model in Depth . Related notes in your repo: Module 2: C++ OOP , objects relationships , and the learncpp.com recaps for chapters 14, 15, 21, 23, 24 and 25."},{"f":"02-oop.html","n":"Object-Oriented Programming in C++","id":"s10","h":"Cheat sheet","x":"Cheat sheet Why must a base class destructor be virtual? If you delete a derived object through a base pointer and the destructor is not virtual, only the base destructor runs: undefined behavior, and the derived part's resources leak. A virtual destructor makes delete dispatch to the most derived destructor first. Any class meant to be a polymorphic base needs one. What is the difference between overloading and overriding? Overloading: several functions with the same name and different parameters in the same scope, chosen at compile time. Overriding: a derived class provides its own implementation of a base virtual function with the same signature, chosen at runtime. What is a pure virtual function and an abstract class? A pure virtual function is declared = 0 and has no required implementation. A class with at least one cannot be instantiated; it defines an interface that derived classes must implement. What is the Rule of Three / Five / Zero? Three: if you define a destructor, copy constructor or copy assignment, define all three. Five: add the move constructor and move assignment. Zero: design classes so none are needed by holding resources in self-managing members like vector and unique_ptr . Remember that declaring a destructor or a copy operation suppresses the move operations, so moves silently become copies. What is object slicing? Copying a derived object into a base-class value discards the derived members and virtual behaviour. Avoid it by passing polymorphic objects by reference or pointer. Can a constructor be virtual? Can a destructor be pure virtual? A constructor cannot be virtual: the vptr is set up by the constructor, so there is no dynamic type yet. A destructor can be pure virtual to make a class abstract, but it still needs a body because derived destructors call it. Composition or inheritance? Prefer composition. Use public inheritance only for a true is-a relationship where you need runtime polymorphism, and make sure it satisfies Liskov substitution. What does explicit do on a constructor and on operator bool ? On a constructor it forbids implicit conversions from the argument type ( Feet f = 3.0 fails). On operator bool it allows the conversion only in contexts such as if and while , not in arithmetic. Mark single-argument constructors explicit by default. What is copy-and-swap? Write the copy constructor and a non-throwing swap , then implement operator=(T other) by value that swaps with the parameter. The copy happens before *this is modified, giving the strong exception guarantee and safe self-assignment with one function."},{"f":"19-object-model.html","n":"The Object Model in Depth","id":"multiple","h":"Multiple inheritance and object layout","x":"Multiple inheritance and object layout A class may have several bases. The sensible use is several pure interfaces (abstract classes with no data), as in Java: struct Reader { virtual int read() = 0; virtual ~Reader() = default; }; struct Writer { virtual void write(int) = 0; virtual ~Writer() = default; }; struct File : Reader, Writer { // implements both interfaces int v = 0; int read() override { return v; } void write(int x) override { v = x; } }; One File object in memory (24 bytes) Reader: vptr Writer: vptr int v Reader* r = &f Writer* w = &f Converting File* to Writer* adjusts the address: the two pointers differ (compiled). Each polymorphic base brings its own vptr, so the pointer value changes when you convert to the second base. The object is the bases laid out one after another, then the derived members. Compiled: sizeof(File) is 24 bytes (two vptrs and an int ). Converting File* to the second base adjusts the pointer value ( static_cast and implicit conversion do it for you). That is why you must not cast with reinterpret_cast or a C cast. Both bases are polymorphic, so delete through either needs a virtual destructor . Name clashes: if both bases have a member f , calling obj.f() is ambiguous. Qualify it ( obj.Reader::f() ) or override it in the derived class. Multiple inheritance of implementation (bases with data) is where trouble starts: the diamond. Read more C++ Primer (Lippman, Lajoie, Moo) 18.3 Multiple and virtual inheritance (PDF pages 831 to 847) Programming: Principles and Practice (Stroustrup) 12.3 Base and derived classes (PDF pages 380 to 387) 12.4 to 12.5 Copying a hierarchy; benefits of OOP (PDF pages 388 to 390)"},{"f":"19-object-model.html","n":"The Object Model in Depth","id":"diamond","h":"The diamond problem and virtual inheritance","x":"The diamond problem and virtual inheritance Plain: two Base copies Base Base Left Right Diamond Virtual: one shared Base Base Left Right Diamond Plain: d.id is ambiguous (two ids). Virtual: one id. Virtual inheritance makes the shared base appear once. struct Base { int id; }; struct Left : virtual Base {}; // \"virtual\" goes on the MIDDLE classes struct Right : virtual Base {}; struct Diamond : Left, Right {}; // one Base inside; d.id is not ambiguous Plain inheritance Virtual inheritance Base subobjects in Diamond two (compiled sizeof 8: two ids) one (compiled sizeof 24: one id plus the machinery) d.id ambiguous: write d.Left::id fine Constructor order (compiled) Base Left Base Right Diamond : Base built twice VBase VLeft VRight VDiamond : shared base once, first Who initializes the base? each middle class the most derived class, directly Cost none extra pointer(s) per object, indirect access to the base, slower casts The standard library's std::iostream is the classic use: istream and ostream inherit ios virtually, so iostream has one stream state. Advice: avoid diamonds. Use interfaces without data, or composition. When you need virtual inheritance, put the data in one place and let the most derived class initialize the base. Key point \"What is the diamond problem and how do you solve it?\" Two paths to one base duplicate its members and make access ambiguous. virtual inheritance shares one copy; the most derived class constructs it first. It costs memory and indirection, so prefer interfaces and composition. Read more C++ Primer (Lippman, Lajoie, Moo) 18.3 Multiple and virtual inheritance (PDF pages 831 to 847) Programming: Principles and Practice (Stroustrup) 15.5 Destructors, including 15.5.2 Virtual destructors (PDF pages 460 to 463)"},{"f":"19-object-model.html","n":"The Object Model in Depth","id":"rtti","h":"RTTI: typeid and dynamic_cast","x":"RTTI: typeid and dynamic_cast RTTI (run-time type information) lets you ask for the dynamic type of an object behind a base pointer. It works only for polymorphic classes (at least one virtual function), because the type information lives next to the vtable. Animal* a = new Dog; typeid(*a) == typeid(Dog); // true: the dynamic type (compiled) typeid(*a) == typeid(Cat); // false typeid(*a).name(); // an implementation-defined name, for debugging only if (Dog* d = dynamic_cast (a)) { } // set only if *a really is a Dog (or derived from Dog) Cat* c = dynamic_cast (a); // nullptr: a Dog is not a Cat Cat& r = dynamic_cast (*a); // a reference cannot be null: throws std::bad_cast Writer* w = dynamic_cast (readerPtr); // a CROSS-cast: sideways between two bases of the same object Question Answer What if the class has no virtual function? typeid gives the static type; dynamic_cast down does not compile Cost? dynamic_cast walks the class hierarchy at run time: slower than a virtual call, avoid it on hot paths static_cast down instead? no check: undefined behaviour if you are wrong -fno-rtti compilers can turn RTTI off to save space; typeid and dynamic_cast then disappear (some embedded and low-latency code does this) A smell Chains of if (dynamic_cast<A*>...) else if (dynamic_cast<B*>...) usually mean a missing virtual function or a Visitor ( More Patterns ). Read more C++ Primer (Lippman, Lajoie, Moo) 19.2 Run-time type identification (PDF pages 854 to 860)"},{"f":"19-object-model.html","n":"The Object Model in Depth","id":"private","h":"Private inheritance versus composition","x":"Private inheritance versus composition Kind Meaning Converts to base outside? public inheritance is-a : usable wherever a Base is yes private inheritance implemented-in-terms-of : an implementation detail no (compiled: Car* to Engine* is not convertible) protected inheritance like private, but derived classes of the derived class see the base no struct Car : private Engine { void drive() { start(); } // uses the base internally using Engine::start; // re-export one member if you want to }; // Car c; Engine* e = // ERROR: the base is private Prefer composition (a member Engine engine_; ): it is simpler and does not expose the base's virtual functions to accidental overriding. Use private inheritance only to override a virtual function of the base, access a protected member, or get the empty base optimisation (an empty base takes no space; a member takes at least 1 byte). Never inherit publicly just to reuse code: if it is not is-a , it breaks the Liskov substitution principle ( SOLID )."},{"f":"19-object-model.html","n":"The Object Model in Depth","id":"cheat","h":"Cheat sheet","x":"Cheat sheet Say each answer aloud, then open it. What is the diamond problem and how is it solved? A class inherits the same base through two paths, so the base's members are duplicated and access is ambiguous. Virtual inheritance ( class Left : virtual Base ) makes all paths share one base subobject, which the most derived class constructs first. It adds memory and indirection, so prefer interfaces without data or composition. Why do Reader* and Writer* to the same File hold different addresses? With multiple inheritance the object contains each base subobject at its own offset, each with its own vptr. Converting to the second base adjusts the pointer to that subobject, which is why C casts and reinterpret_cast are wrong here. What does dynamic_cast need and what does it cost? A polymorphic class (a virtual function) because it reads the type information next to the vtable. It checks the real type at run time: null for pointers, std::bad_cast for references. It is slower than a virtual call, so avoid it on hot paths; repeated use suggests a missing virtual function or a Visitor. typeid on a polymorphic versus non-polymorphic type? On a polymorphic object typeid(*p) gives the dynamic type. On a type with no virtual function it gives the static type, known at compile time. type_info::name() is implementation-defined and only for diagnostics. When is private inheritance appropriate? For \"implemented in terms of\": to override a virtual function of the base, access its protected members, or use the empty base optimisation. Otherwise prefer composition. It does not allow conversion to the base outside the class."},{"f":"08-strings-regex-time.html","n":"Strings, Regex and Time","id":"s1","h":"std::string and std::string_view","x":"std::string and std::string_view std::string s = \"hello world\" (owns the characters) h e l l o w o r l d string_view word { ptr = &s[6], len = 5 } two words, no copy The view is valid only while s is alive and unchanged. Not guaranteed null-terminated. View for read-only parameters; string for anything you store. std::string owns its characters. std::string_view (C++17) is a non-owning pointer plus length: two words, no allocation, valid only while the text lives. #include #include void log(std::string_view msg); // accepts std::string, string literal, char array... without copying std::string s = \"hello world\"; log(s); log(\"literal\"); // no temporary std::string is built auto pos = s.find(\"world\"); // 6 if (pos != std::string::npos) { // find returns npos (a huge value) when nothing matches std::string_view word = std::string_view(s).substr(pos); // \"world\", no copy } Classic bug: dangling view std::string_view bad = std::string(\"temporary\"); // the string dies at the semicolon // 'bad' now points at freed memory: undefined behaviour if you read it std::string_view f() { std::string local = \"oops\"; return local; // returns a view of a dead local } A view must not outlive its string and is not null-terminated : do not pass sv.data() to a C API. SSO: short strings (about 15 chars) live inside the object, no heap allocation. string_view for read-only parameters; std::string for what you store. Building big strings in a loop: append or reserve , not chained + temporaries. Key point View when you only read (accepts literals without a temporary); const string& when you need null termination. Read more C++ Primer (Lippman, Lajoie, Moo) 9.5 Additional string operations (PDF pages 389 to 396)"},{"f":"08-strings-regex-time.html","n":"Strings, Regex and Time","id":"sso","h":"Inside std::string : SSO and capacity","x":"Inside std::string : SSO and capacity Short: \"hello\" (up to 15 chars) size 5 | chars live right here, no heap Long: 40 chars size 40 | capacity | ptr heap block with the characters sizeof(std::string) is 32 bytes (libstdc++) The small string optimisation: no allocation for short text. A std::string is a small object (compiled: 32 bytes , libstdc++) that holds a pointer, a size and a capacity. For short text it stores the characters inside the object , so there is no heap allocation: SSO . Compiled: an empty string has capacity 15 , \"hello\" was stored inside the object and a 40-character string was not. Fact Why it matters SSO limit is implementation-defined (15 in libstdc++, 22 in libc++) do not rely on the exact number Capacity is the room before the next reallocation; size is the length s.size() <= s.capacity() Growth is geometric (usually doubling) compiled: appending 1000 characters one at a time reallocated 7 times reserve(n) allocates once up front compiled: with reserve(1000) first, the buffer never moved A reallocation invalidates pointers, references and iterators into the string do not keep s.c_str() across a modification Moving a long string steals the pointer; moving a short one copies the inline bytes a move is cheap but never worse than a copy std::string s; s.reserve(1000); // one allocation, then no reallocations while we append up to 1000 for (int i = 0; i Key point \"What is SSO?\" Short strings are stored in the string object, so no heap allocation and no pointer chase. It is why short-string copies are cheap and why string moves are not always pointer steals. On a hot path reserve and avoid building strings at all ( Performance & Memory )."},{"f":"08-strings-regex-time.html","n":"Strings, Regex and Time","id":"numbers","h":"Numbers and text: from_chars , to_chars , stoi","x":"Numbers and text: from_chars , to_chars , stoi Function Failure Allocates? Locale? Use std::from_chars / to_chars (C++17) returns an error code, no exception no no hot paths , parsing feeds std::stoi , stod , to_string throws invalid_argument / out_of_range yes (a string ) yes (slower) ordinary code atoi , strtol , sscanf silent 0 or errno no yes avoid in new C++ std::istringstream >> sets failbit yes yes mixed text parsing int v = 0; std::string_view num = \"1234\"; auto [ptr, ec] = std::from_chars(num.data(), num.data() + num.size(), v); if (ec == std::errc{} && ptr == num.data() + num.size()) use(v); // parsed the WHOLE input char buf[32]; auto r = std::to_chars(buf, buf + sizeof buf, 255, 16); // \"ff\": no allocation std::string_view out(buf, r.ptr - buf); Compiled: from_chars(\"12x4\") parsed 12 , reported no error, and left ptr at \"x4\" . Always check that ptr reached the end when the whole token must be a number. Compiled: stoi(\"abc\") threw invalid_argument and stoi(\"99999999999\") threw out_of_range . from_chars does not skip leading whitespace and does not accept a leading + . Floating-point from_chars needs a recent standard library (GCC 11+, MSVC 2019+)."},{"f":"08-strings-regex-time.html","n":"Strings, Regex and Time","id":"format","h":"Formatting: std::format and streams","x":"Formatting: std::format and streams #include // C++20 std::format(\"{:>8}|{: Spec Meaning {:>8} {:<8} {:^8} right, left, centre in a field of width 8 {:08.3f} zero padded, width 8, 3 digits after the point {:#x} {:#b} hex and binary with the 0x / 0b prefix {:+d} always show the sign Which one to use Tool Good Bad std::format (C++20) type-safe, readable, fast, no state, errors at compile time allocates a std::string ; check your compiler supports it (GCC 13+) std::print (C++23) format straight to stdout C++23 operator<< streams works everywhere, extensible for your types stateful ( std::hex sticks!), verbose, slower printf fast, familiar no type checking: a wrong specifier is undefined behaviour Streams keep state std::cout << std::hex << 255; leaves every later integer printed in hex until you switch back with std::dec . setw applies only to the next output. std::format has none of this. To format your own type, specialise std::formatter<T> (or provide operator<< for streams). std::format_to(out, ...) writes into an existing buffer or iterator to avoid the temporary string. Do not build log strings on a hot path unless the log level is enabled."},{"f":"08-strings-regex-time.html","n":"Strings, Regex and Time","id":"streams","h":"String streams and files","x":"String streams and files Type Reads from / writes to Header std::istringstream reads from a std::string <sstream> std::ostringstream builds a std::string ( .str() ) <sstream> std::ifstream / ofstream / fstream a file <fstream> std::istringstream in(\"42 3.5 word\"); int a; double b; std::string w; in >> a >> b >> w; // 42, 3.5, \"word\": >> skips whitespace and converts std::ostringstream out; out Loop on the read, not on eof() : while (std::getline(f, line)) is right; while (!f.eof()) processes the last line twice. Check the stream after opening: if (!f) { /* could not open */ } . A failed stream silently does nothing afterwards. Stream state bits: good , eof , fail (a format error, such as reading text into an int ), bad (a hard I/O error). clear() resets them. Compiled: getline(csv, field, ',') split a three-field line, and a file of two lines read back as two lines. Files are closed by the destructor, so a plain scope is the RAII way to flush and close. std::endl flushes every time (slow). Use '\\n' . Read more C++ Primer (Lippman, Lajoie, Moo) 8.1 to 8.3 IO classes, file IO and string streams (PDF pages 339 to 353) Programming: Principles and Practice (Stroustrup) 9.11 String streams (PDF pages 296 to 297)"},{"f":"08-strings-regex-time.html","n":"Strings, Regex and Time","id":"s2","h":"Regular expressions","x":"Regular expressions A regex describes a set of strings . Match vs search is a favourite question: Function Question it answers std::regex_match Does the whole string match the pattern? std::regex_search Does some part of the string match? (first match; captures in std::smatch ) std::regex_replace Replace every match with a replacement string. #include std::regex re(\"ab+c\"); std::regex_match(\"abbbc\", re); // true : the entire string fits std::regex_match(\"xabbbcx\", re); // false : extra characters around it std::regex_search(\"xabbbcx\", re); // true : found inside Syntax Meaning Example . any character a.b matches aab , axb ? * + 0 or 1, 0 or more, 1 or more ab*c matches ac , abbbc {n} {n,m} exactly n, between n and m ab{3}c matches abbbc [abc] [a-z] [^a-z] one of, a range, none of a[bcd]e (x|y) group and alternation gr(e|a)y matches grey , gray ^ $ start and end of the string ^\\d+$ \\d \\w \\s digit, word character, whitespace \\w+@\\w+ Try patterns ( std::regex uses ECMAScript grammar, like this tester): Backslashes Write \"\\\\d+\" or a raw string R\"(\\d+)\" . POSIX classes like [[:alnum:]] work in std::regex but not in the tester. Captures, replace and iterating matches Parentheses make capture groups ; std::smatch holds the whole match at 0, groups after. std::string line = \"temp=21.5C humidity=40%\"; std::regex kv(R\"((\\w+)=([\\d.]+))\"); std::smatch m; if (std::regex_search(line, m, kv)) { // m[0] = \"temp=21.5\" m[1] = \"temp\" m[2] = \"21.5\" std::cout \" 21.5, then humidity -> 40 // replace: $1, $2 refer to groups std::string swapped = std::regex_replace(line, kv, \"$2:$1\"); // \"21.5:tempC 40:humidity%\" (only the matched parts change) Practical limits of std::regex Constructing a regex is expensive : build once, not in a loop. std::regex is slow ; on hot paths use find / from_chars (or RE2, CTRE). Nested quantifiers like (a+)+b can backtrack exponentially: no untrusted input. Invalid pattern: std::regex_error . Key point regex_match(\"hello world\", regex(\"hello\")) is false: it must cover the entire string. regex_search is true. Read more C++ Primer (Lippman, Lajoie, Moo) 17.3 Regular expressions (PDF pages 757 to 773)"},{"f":"08-strings-regex-time.html","n":"Strings, Regex and Time","id":"s4","h":"Time with std::chrono","x":"Time with std::chrono Clock steady_clock::now() Time point an instant (t1, t0) t1 - t0 Duration count + unit: 250 ms Elapsed time and timeouts: steady_clock (monotonic). Timestamps for people: system_clock (can jump). Durations are type-safe: seconds and milliseconds cannot be confused. Three ideas, in this order: Duration : count plus unit; type-safe. Time point : an instant since a clock's epoch. Clock : the source of time points. Clock Behaviour Use it for system_clock Wall-clock time. Can jump (NTP, manual changes, daylight handling by the OS). Convertible to time_t . Timestamps, dates shown to people steady_clock Monotonic : never goes backwards. Measuring elapsed time, timeouts high_resolution_clock Smallest tick the implementation has. Often just an alias of one of the above. Rarely the right choice; prefer steady_clock #include #include using namespace std::chrono_literals; auto t0 = std::chrono::steady_clock::now(); std::this_thread::sleep_for(250ms); // duration literal auto t1 = std::chrono::steady_clock::now(); auto elapsed = std::chrono::duration_cast (t1 - t0); std::cout (s); // 1 (truncates) steady_clock for intervals system_clock can jump, giving negative or wrong intervals: bad for timeouts."},{"f":"08-strings-regex-time.html","n":"Strings, Regex and Time","id":"s5","h":"Cheat sheet","x":"Cheat sheet Answer aloud, then open. When is a string_view dangerous? When it outlives the string it views: views of temporaries, of locals returned from a function, or of a string that is later modified or reallocated. regex_match versus regex_search ? regex_match must match the whole input. regex_search finds a match anywhere inside it. How do you iterate over every match? std::sregex_iterator (or std::cregex_iterator for C strings) over the range, compared with a default-constructed end iterator. Which clock for a timeout, and why? steady_clock , because it is monotonic and unaffected by changes to the system time. Why can seconds = milliseconds not compile but milliseconds = seconds can? Converting to a coarser unit would truncate, so it requires an explicit duration_cast . Converting to a finer unit is lossless, so it is implicit. What is the small string optimisation? Short strings (about 15 characters in libstdc++) are stored inside the std::string object, so no heap allocation happens and copying is cheap. Longer strings keep a pointer to a heap block. reserve avoids repeated reallocations when you know the size. from_chars versus stoi ? from_chars (C++17) returns an error code, never throws, never allocates and ignores the locale, so it suits hot paths and feed parsing; check that the end pointer reached the end of the token. stoi throws invalid_argument or out_of_range and is for ordinary code. std::format versus streams versus printf ? std::format (C++20) is type-safe, stateless and readable. Streams are extensible but keep state ( std::hex sticks) and are verbose. printf has no type checking, so a wrong specifier is undefined behaviour. Why is while (!f.eof()) the wrong way to read a file? eof becomes true only after a read has failed, so the loop body runs once more with a failed read. Loop on the read itself: while (std::getline(f, line)) . Output: what does this print? std::string s = \"abc\"; std::cout << s.find('z') == std::string::npos; It does not compile as intended: << binds tighter than == , so it tries to compare the stream with npos . Parenthesise: std::cout << (s.find('z') == std::string::npos); prints 1."},{"f":"04-stl.html","n":"The C++ Standard Template Library","id":"s1","h":"The big picture","x":"The big picture Containers vector, map, list Iterators [begin, end) Algorithms sort, find, accumulate Algorithms never touch containers, only iterator ranges. One sort works on any container that offers the right iterators. Part What it is Examples Containers classes that own and organise a collection of elements vector , map , set , deque Iterators generalised pointers that walk a container v.begin() , m.find(k) Algorithms generic functions that operate on iterator ranges sort , find , accumulate Algorithms see only [first, last) , so one std::sort serves a vector, array or deque. That separation is the design. #include // std::vector #include // std::map, std::multimap #include // std::unordered_map #include // std::set #include // std::sort, std::find, std::transform ... #include // std::accumulate, std::iota #include // std::string Default choice std::vector . Contiguous memory is so cache-friendly it often beats \"better\" containers."},{"f":"04-stl.html","n":"The C++ Standard Template Library","id":"s2","h":"vector : the workhorse","x":"vector : the workhorse buffer on the heap 10 20 30 spare spare size = 3 (elements in use) capacity = 5 (slots allocated) push_back, buffer full: allocate bigger, move all, free old = amortised O(1), but every iterator and pointer into it is invalidated reserve() grows capacity only; resize() changes size. A vector is one contiguous heap array with a size and a capacity . When full, push_back allocates a bigger buffer, moves everything and frees the old one: occasional O(n), amortised O(1) . std::vector v; v.reserve(100); // capacity 100, size still 0: no reallocation for the next 100 push_backs v.push_back(1); // copy/move an existing object in v.emplace_back(2); // construct the element in place: no temporary v.resize(10); // size becomes 10; new elements are value-initialised (0) v[3]; // no bounds check: out-of-range is undefined behavior v.at(3); // bounds-checked: throws std::out_of_range v.front(); v.back(); v.data(); // data() is the raw int* for C APIs v.size(); v.capacity(); v.empty(); reserve vs resize reserve(n) only allocates; resize(n) creates elements. v[5] = x after only reserve(10) is UB. Braces vs parentheses std::vector a(3, 7); // parentheses: size 3, every element 7 -> {7, 7, 7} std::vector b{3, 7}; // braces: the initializer_list constructor wins -> {3, 7} With braces, a constructor taking std::initializer_list is preferred whenever it can match. Read more C++ Primer (Lippman, Lajoie, Moo) 3.3 Library vector type (PDF pages 125 to 134) 9.3 Sequential container operations (PDF pages 370 to 383) 9.4 How a vector grows (PDF pages 384 to 388) Programming: Principles and Practice (Stroustrup) 3.5 to 3.6 Functions; vector introduced (PDF pages 81 to 89) 17.6 to 17.9 Other operations, remaining Vector problems, changing size (PDF pages 513 to 523) Effective Modern C++ (Meyers) Item 42, Consider emplacement instead of insertion (PDF pages 310 to 320) C++ Move Semantics (Josuttis) Chapter 15 Move semantics in standard library types (PDF pages 229 to 250)"},{"f":"04-stl.html","n":"The C++ Standard Template Library","id":"s6","h":"Iterators","x":"Iterators An iterator acts like a pointer: *it , ++it , it == end . end() is one past the last ; ranges are half-open [begin, end) . std::vector v{1, 2, 3}; for (auto it = v.begin(); it != v.end(); ++it) std::cout Category Can do Containers Forward ++ , read (multi-pass) forward_list , unordered containers Bidirectional also -- list , map , set Random access also it + n , it[i] , it1 - it2 , < vector , deque , array , string So std::sort needs random access and fails on std::list (use list.sort() ). Iterator adaptors: filling a container An algorithm like std::copy assigns through its output iterator, so the destination must already be big enough. An inserter turns each assignment into an insertion instead: Adaptor Each assignment does std::back_inserter(c) c.push_back(x) std::front_inserter(c) c.push_front(x) (so the order reverses; needs deque / list ) std::inserter(c, pos) c.insert(pos, x) (works for set and map ) std::vector src{3, 1, 2}, out; std::copy(src.begin(), src.end(), std::back_inserter(out)); // out is {3, 1, 2}: it grew // std::copy(src.begin(), src.end(), out.begin()); // BUG if out is empty: writes past the end std::set sorted; std::copy(src.begin(), src.end(), std::inserter(sorted, sorted.begin())); std::reverse_iterator (what rbegin() returns) and std::move_iterator (moves instead of copying) are the other two adaptors worth knowing. Iterator invalidation Changing a container can invalidate iterators, pointers and references; using one is UB. Container Operation What is invalidated vector push_back , insert all if it reallocated; otherwise those at or after the insertion point vector erase the erased element and everything after it deque insert/erase in the middle everything; push at either end invalidates iterators but not references list , map , set insert nothing list , map , set erase only the erased element unordered_* insert that triggers a rehash all iterators (references stay valid) Spot the bug Erasing inside a loop std::vector v{1, 2, 3, 4}; for (auto it = v.begin(); it != v.end(); ++it) if (*it % 2 == 0) v.erase(it); // BUG erase invalidates it , and the loop then does ++it on a dead iterator. The correct idiom uses the iterator erase returns: for (auto it = v.begin(); it != v.end(); ) { if (*it % 2 == 0) it = v.erase(it); // erase returns the next valid iterator else ++it; } // or, in C++20: std::erase_if(v, [](int x) { return x % 2 == 0; }); Step through a correct erase loop: Read more C++ Primer (Lippman, Lajoie, Moo) 3.4 Introducing iterators (PDF pages 135 to 141) 10.4 Revisiting iterators (PDF pages 430 to 438) 10.5 Structure of generic algorithms (PDF pages 439 to 443) Programming: Principles and Practice (Stroustrup) 19.1 to 19.3, 19.6 Containers, sequences and iterators, linked lists, vector/list/string (PDF pages 559 to 572 and 585 to 586)"},{"f":"04-stl.html","n":"The C++ Standard Template Library","id":"s3","h":"Other sequence containers","x":"Other sequence containers Container Storage Best at Watch out std::array<T,N> fixed size, inline (stack) size known at compile time; zero overhead cannot grow std::vector<T> one contiguous heap block almost everything; fast iteration and indexing insert/erase in the middle is O(n) std::deque<T> chunks of arrays fast push/pop at both ends, still indexable not contiguous std::list<T> doubly linked nodes O(1) insert/erase anywhere if you hold an iterator ; iterators stay valid no indexing, a heap allocation per element, poor cache behaviour std::forward_list<T> singly linked nodes minimal memory list forward iteration only Operation vector deque list index [i] O(1) O(1) not available push_back amortised O(1) O(1) O(1) push_front O(n) O(1) O(1) insert / erase in the middle O(n) O(n) O(1) at a known position find a value O(n) O(n) O(n) Read more C++ Primer (Lippman, Lajoie, Moo) 9.1 to 9.2 Overview and the container library (PDF pages 355 to 369) 9.6 Container adaptors (PDF pages 397 to 402) 10.6 Container-specific algorithms (PDF pages 444 to 447)"},{"f":"04-stl.html","n":"The C++ Standard Template Library","id":"s4","h":"Associative and unordered containers","x":"Associative and unordered containers map / set: balanced tree, sorted, O(log n) 20 10 30 in-order walk = sorted order unordered_map: hash table, average O(1) 0 \"Ana\" 1 2 \"Bob\" \"Eve\" hash(key) picks a bucket; collisions chain (Bob, Eve) Need order or range queries: map. Plain lookup: unordered_map. map / set unordered_map / unordered_set Implementation balanced binary tree (usually red-black) hash table Order of iteration sorted by key unspecified find / insert / erase O(log n) average O(1), worst case O(n) Key requirement operator< (a strict weak ordering) a hash function and operator== Range queries ( lower_bound ) yes no std::map age; age[\"Ana\"] = 30; // operator[] INSERTS a default (0) if the key is missing age.insert({\"Bob\", 25}); // does nothing if \"Bob\" already exists age.insert_or_assign(\"Bob\", 26); // C++17: insert or overwrite if (auto it = age.find(\"Ana\"); it != age.end()) // find never inserts std::cout second; // it->first is the key, it->second the value for (const auto& [name, years] : age) // structured bindings; sorted by name std::cout seen; if (!seen.insert(5).second) { /* second == false means it was already there */ } Predict Does looking up make the map bigger? std::map m; if (m[\"x\"] == 0) { } std::cout 1 operator[] on a missing key inserts it with a default-constructed value. To only check, use m.find(\"x\") , m.count(\"x\") or m.contains(\"x\") (C++20). try_emplace , multimap and multiset Call If the key already exists m[k] = v overwrites (and inserts a default first if missing) m.insert({k, v}) / m.emplace(k, v) does nothing, but may already have moved from or built the value m.try_emplace(k, args...) (C++17) does nothing and guarantees the arguments are not touched : compiled, a moved-in 40-character string was intact afterwards m.insert_or_assign(k, v) (C++17) overwrites std::map a{{1, \"old\"}}; std::string payload(40, 'p'); a.try_emplace(1, std::move(payload)); // key 1 exists: nothing happens, payload still holds 40 chars a.try_emplace(2, std::move(payload)); // new key: the string is moved into the map std::multimap mm{{\"fruit\", 1}, {\"fruit\", 2}, {\"veg\", 9}}; // duplicate keys allowed auto [lo, hi] = mm.equal_range(\"fruit\"); // the range of all entries with that key mm.count(\"fruit\"); // 2: there is no operator[] because a key may have many values multimap and multiset allow duplicate keys and keep equal keys in insertion order. There is no operator[] . try_emplace is the right call for \"insert if absent\" when building the value is expensive or the argument is move-only. Container adaptors They wrap another container and restrict the interface: std::stack (LIFO: push/pop/top ), std::queue (FIFO: push/pop/front ) and std::priority_queue (a heap: top() is the largest element by default). std::priority_queue maxHeap; // largest first std::priority_queue , std::greater > minHeap; // smallest first Step through operator[] : Read more C++ Primer (Lippman, Lajoie, Moo) 11.1 to 11.2 Using an associative container, overview (PDF pages 449 to 456) 11.3 Operations on associative containers (PDF pages 457 to 471) Programming: Principles and Practice (Stroustrup) 20.1 to 20.6 map, unordered_map, set, container overview (PDF pages 591 to 609)"},{"f":"04-stl.html","n":"The C++ Standard Template Library","id":"keys","h":"Custom keys: comparators and hashes","x":"Custom keys: comparators and hashes A map / set needs an ordering ; an unordered_map / unordered_set needs a hash and equality . For your own key types, you provide them. struct NoCase { // an ordering that ignores case bool operator()(const std::string& a, const std::string& b) const { return std::lexicographical_compare(a.begin(), a.end(), b.begin(), b.end(), [](unsigned char x, unsigned char y) { return std::tolower(x) m; // the comparator is part of the TYPE m[\"Alpha\"] = 1; m[\"ALPHA\"] = 2; // the same key: size is 1 (compiled: 2 keys with \"beta\") struct Product { std::string shop; int id; bool operator==(const Product&) const = default; }; struct ProductHash { std::size_t operator()(const Product& p) const { std::size_t h = std::hash {}(p.shop); h ^= std::hash {}(p.id) + 0x9e3779b97f4a7c15ULL + (h > 2); // combine the member hashes return h; } }; std::unordered_map price; price[{\"north\", 7}] = 101.5; Container Key must provide Rule map , set a strict weak ordering ( operator< or a comparator) irreflexive and transitive; equivalence means \"neither is less\". <= is not valid: undefined behaviour unordered_map , unordered_set a hash and operator== equal keys must have equal hashes; a poor hash makes every lookup O(n) Transparent comparators: std::map<std::string, int, std::less<>> lets find take a string_view or const char* without building a temporary std::string (compiled: it found \"apple\" from a string_view ). C++20 adds the same for unordered containers. Never change a key while it is in the container: its position or bucket would be wrong. Keys are const for that reason. Hash quality: combine all fields; avoid returning just one field. std::hash for int is the identity in libstdc++, which is fine. A rehash (growth beyond max_load_factor ) invalidates iterators; reserve(n) up front avoids it, and avoids the allocations on a hot path. Ordered or hashed? Ordered when you need sorted iteration or range queries ( lower_bound ); hashed for plain lookup. For a few dozen keys a sorted vector and binary search often beats both. Key point \"How do you use your own type as a key in unordered_map ?\" Provide operator== and a hash functor (or specialise std::hash ), combining all members, and make sure equal objects hash equally. Read more C++ Primer (Lippman, Lajoie, Moo) 11.3 Operations on associative containers (PDF pages 457 to 471) 11.4 The unordered containers (PDF pages 472 to 477)"},{"f":"04-stl.html","n":"The C++ Standard Template Library","id":"s5","h":"Which container should I use?","x":"Which container should I use? Pick what you mostly do: Key point \"vector vs list?\" Answer with memory : contiguous means cache-friendly, so vector wins even for middle inserts at modest sizes. List wins for stable iterators and cheap splicing. Read more Programming: Principles and Practice (Stroustrup) 20.1 to 20.6 map, unordered_map, set, container overview (PDF pages 591 to 609)"},{"f":"04-stl.html","n":"The C++ Standard Template Library","id":"s7","h":"Algorithms","x":"Algorithms Over a hundred generic functions in <algorithm> / <numeric> . Prefer them to loops: named, tested, often faster. Most take a lambda. Try some on {5, 2, 9, 2, 7, 4} : std::sort(v.begin(), v.end(), [](const P& a, const P& b) { return a.age 6; }); int n = std::count_if(v.begin(), v.end(), [](int x) { return x % 2 == 0; }); bool any = std::any_of(v.begin(), v.end(), [](int x) { return x std::transform(v.begin(), v.end(), v.begin(), [](int x) { return x * x; }); auto pos = std::lower_bound(sorted.begin(), sorted.end(), 5); // binary search: range MUST be sorted Predict The remove surprise std::vector v{1, 2, 3, 4, 5}; std::remove(v.begin(), v.end(), 3); std::cout 5 Algorithms cannot change a container's size. std::remove shifts kept elements forward and returns the new logical end. Use the erase-remove idiom : v.erase(std::remove(v.begin(), v.end(), 3), v.end()); // classic std::erase(v, 3); // C++20: one call Strict weak ordering Use < , never <= : a comparator true for equal elements can crash sort or corrupt a set . sort is not stable; use stable_sort . Read more C++ Primer (Lippman, Lajoie, Moo) 10.1 to 10.2 Overview and a first look (PDF pages 405 to 413) Programming: Principles and Practice (Stroustrup) 21.1 to 21.2, 21.5 Standard algorithms, function objects, sorting and searching (PDF pages 617 to 626 and 633 to 635) C++ Move Semantics (Josuttis) Chapter 14 Moving algorithms and iterators (PDF pages 219 to 228)"},{"f":"04-stl.html","n":"The C++ Standard Template Library","id":"ranges","h":"Ranges and views (C++20)","x":"Ranges and views (C++20) #include std::ranges::sort(v); // pass the container, not begin() and end() std::ranges::sort(v, std::greater<>{}); // descending (compiled: first element 6) auto evenSquares = v | std::views::filter([](int x) { return x % 2 == 0; }) | std::views::transform([](int x) { return x * x; }); // a LAZY view: nothing is computed yet for (int x : evenSquares) use(x); // computed on demand, no temporary vector (compiled: sum 56) A range is anything with begin() and end() . The std::ranges:: algorithms take the whole range and support projections : std::ranges::sort(people, {}, &Person::age) . A view is a lightweight, non-owning, lazy window ( filter , transform , take , drop , reverse , iota ). Pipe them with | . A view does not own its data: if the container dies or reallocates, the view dangles, like string_view . Ranges make pipelines readable; you do not need to know every view."},{"f":"04-stl.html","n":"The C++ Standard Template Library","id":"s9","h":"Common pitfalls","x":"Common pitfalls pair , tuple , optional and variant are covered in Vocabulary Types . Pitfall Fix Using an iterator/pointer/reference after a push_back reallocated the vector reserve up front, or re-fetch the iterator, or index instead map[key] silently inserts use find / contains to test; at() to read-or-throw v.size() - 1 on an empty vector ( size() is unsigned, so it wraps to a huge number) check empty() first or write i + 1 < v.size() Passing a big container by value pass const std::vector<T>& std::vector<bool> is a packed-bit special case with proxy references use std::vector<char> or std::bitset auto x = v[0] copies the element const auto& x = v[0] std::find on a map is O(n) use the member m.find(key) , O(log n) Read more C++ Primer (Lippman, Lajoie, Moo) 9.3 Sequential container operations (PDF pages 370 to 383)"},{"f":"04-stl.html","n":"The C++ Standard Template Library","id":"random","h":"Random numbers: <random>","x":"Random numbers: <random> std::mt19937 gen(12345); // an ENGINE: produces raw random bits (here seeded for repeatability) std::uniform_int_distribution dice(1, 6); // a DISTRIBUTION: shapes the bits into the numbers you want int roll = dice(gen); std::random_device rd; // a non-deterministic seed source (hardware or OS) std::mt19937 gen2(rd()); std::normal_distribution noise(0.0, 1.0); Engine plus distribution : never use rand() % n (poor quality, biased, global state). Same seed gives the same sequence (compiled: true), which is what you want in tests and simulations. Compiled: 6000 rolls of the dice gave every face between 850 and 1150 times; 10,000 normal samples had a mean near 0. Keep one engine per thread; engines are not thread-safe. Notes: Module 3 . Read more C++ Primer (Lippman, Lajoie, Moo) 17.4 Random numbers (PDF pages 774 to 780)"},{"f":"04-stl.html","n":"The C++ Standard Template Library","id":"s10","h":"Cheat sheet","x":"Cheat sheet How do you use your own type as a key in unordered_map , and in map ? unordered_map : provide operator== and a hash functor (or specialise std::hash ) that combines all members; equal objects must hash equally. map : provide a strict weak ordering ( operator< or a comparator type). Never <= , and never modify a key while it is stored. Why use try_emplace instead of emplace or insert ? If the key exists, try_emplace does nothing and does not touch its arguments, so a moved-in value is not lost. emplace and insert may already have built or moved from the value. It is the right call for \"insert if absent\" with expensive or move-only values. What does std::back_inserter do and when do you need it? It is an output iterator whose assignment calls push_back . Algorithms such as copy and transform write through iterators and do not grow the destination, so with an empty target you would write past the end; back_inserter makes them grow it instead. What are C++20 ranges and views? Ranges algorithms take a whole container ( std::ranges::sort(v) ) and support projections. Views are lazy, non-owning pipelines ( v | views::filter(f) | views::transform(g) ) that compute on demand without temporaries. Like string_view , a view dangles if its container dies. How does std::vector grow? When size == capacity it allocates a larger buffer (geometric growth, typically 1.5x to 2x, implementation-defined), moves or copies the elements, and frees the old buffer. This makes push_back amortised O(1), but it invalidates all iterators, pointers and references. reserve avoids the reallocations. map vs unordered_map ? map : balanced tree, sorted iteration, O(log n), needs operator< , supports range queries. unordered_map : hash table, average O(1) (worst O(n) with many collisions), no order, needs a hash and == . Choose unordered_map for plain lookups and map when you need order. push_back vs emplace_back ? push_back takes an existing object (a copy or move). emplace_back forwards its arguments to the element's constructor and builds it in place, avoiding a temporary. For simple types there is no difference; for objects with expensive construction emplace_back can save a move. What is the erase-remove idiom? std::remove / remove_if only moves the elements to keep to the front and returns the new end; it cannot shrink the container. Pass that to the container's erase to actually delete the tail: v.erase(std::remove_if(...), v.end()) . C++20 has std::erase_if . What is a hash collision and how is it handled? Two different keys hash to the same bucket. The standard library's unordered containers resolve it with chaining (a bucket holds a list of entries), so lookups in a heavily colliding bucket degrade toward O(n). A good hash function and a sensible load factor ( rehash , reserve ) keep it near O(1). Why can't I std::sort a std::list ? sort needs random-access iterators (it jumps around by index). A list only provides bidirectional iterators, so it has its own member function list::sort . Which container for a task queue? For \"top K\" elements? A queue ( std::queue , backed by deque ) for FIFO tasks. A priority_queue (heap) for top K: keep a heap of size K and compare against its root: O(n log K)."},{"f":"15-lambdas.html","n":"Lambdas and Callables","id":"before","h":"Before lambdas: function pointers and functors","x":"Before lambdas: function pointers and functors Algorithms such as std::sort and std::count_if take \"something callable\". Before C++11 there were two ways to supply it, and a lambda is a shorter way to write the second. A function pointer bool descending(int a, int b) { return a > b; } int twice(int x) { return 2 * x; } void apply(int (*f)(int), int x) { std::cout v{5, 2, 9, 1}; std::sort(v.begin(), v.end(), descending); // 9 5 2 1: pass the function by name apply(twice, 21); // 42 A function pointer is just an address (8 bytes). It can point to any function with the same signature , chosen at run time. It has no state : the function sees only its arguments and globals. To give descending a limit you would need a global variable. The call goes through the pointer, so the compiler usually cannot inline it. A functor (function object) A functor is a class with an operator() . An object of it can be called like a function, and it can carry state in its members. struct GreaterThan { int limit; // the state bool operator()(int x) const { return x > limit; } }; GreaterThan gt{4}; gt(5); // true gt(3); // false std::count_if(v.begin(), v.end(), GreaterThan{1}); // 3: how many elements are above 1 Each functor class is its own type, so a template like std::count_if knows exactly which operator() to call and can inline it. The cost: a whole class, written away from the place where it is used, for one small piece of logic. So what is a lambda? A lambda is a functor the compiler writes for you, in place: [limit](int x) { return x > limit; } is the GreaterThan above, with limit as its member. The next sections show how it works and where it goes wrong. Read more C++ Primer (Lippman, Lajoie, Moo) 6.7 Pointers to functions (PDF pages 276 to 281) Programming: Principles and Practice (Stroustrup) 21.1 to 21.2, 21.5 Standard algorithms, function objects, sorting and searching (PDF pages 617 to 626 and 633 to 635) Effective Modern C++ (Meyers) Item 31, Avoid default capture modes (PDF pages 234 to 242) Item 32, Use init capture to move objects into closures (PDF pages 242 to 247)"},{"f":"15-lambdas.html","n":"Lambdas and Callables","id":"anatomy","h":"What a lambda is","x":"What a lambda is [factor](int x) { return x * factor; } captures in [ ] parameters in ( ) body in { } compiles to class __lambda { int factor; // by-value capture int operator()(int x) const { return x * factor; } Capture by reference stores a reference instead; it dangles if the lambda outlives the variable. A lambda is an unnamed function object written in place: the compiler generates a small class whose constructor stores the captures and whose operator() holds the body. // [captures] (parameters) -> return_type { body } auto add = [](int a, int b) { return a + b; }; add(2, 3); // 5 std::vector v{5, 2, 9, 1}; std::sort(v.begin(), v.end(), [](int a, int b) { return a > b; }); // descending: an inline comparator Every lambda has its own unique type , so store it with auto (or a template parameter). A lambda with no captures converts to a plain function pointer. Compiled: sizeof of such a lambda is 1, of one capturing an int is 4, of a function pointer 8. operator() is const by default; mutable lifts that (next sections). Read more C++ Primer (Lippman, Lajoie, Moo) 10.3 Customizing operations (PDF pages 414 to 429)"},{"f":"15-lambdas.html","n":"Lambdas and Callables","id":"captures","h":"Captures: by value and by reference","x":"Captures: by value and by reference int factor = 3; auto byValue = [factor](int x) { return x * factor; }; // copies factor now auto byRef = [&factor](int x) { return x * factor; }; // refers to the live variable auto all = [=] (int x) { return x * factor; }; // [=] all by value, [&] all by reference Predict Capture by value vs by reference int x = 1; auto byVal = [x] { return x; }; auto byRef = [&x] { return x; }; x = 2; std::cout 12 byVal took a snapshot of x (1) when it was created. byRef sees the variable itself, so it reads the new value 2. A by-value capture is const inside the lambda unless you add mutable . Step through the two capture modes: Dangling captures A lambda that captures a local by reference and then outlives it (returned from the function, or run later on another thread) reads destroyed memory. When a lambda escapes its scope, capture by value. [=] and this Inside a member function [=] also captures this (the pointer), so the lambda uses the object through that pointer and dangles if the object dies. This implicit capture is deprecated in C++20: write [=, this] to say so, or [*this] to copy the object. Read more C++ Primer (Lippman, Lajoie, Moo) 10.3 Customizing operations (PDF pages 414 to 429)"},{"f":"15-lambdas.html","n":"Lambdas and Callables","id":"advanced","h":"mutable , init-capture, generic and recursive lambdas","x":"mutable , init-capture, generic and recursive lambdas auto count = [n = 0]() mutable { return ++n; }; // own state; mutable lets it change count(); count(); count(); // 1 2 3 (compiled: \"123\") auto owned = std::make_unique (42); auto init = [p = std::move(owned)] { return *p; }; // C++14 init-capture: MOVE a move-only object in // (compiled: returns 42, the original is null) auto bigger = [](const auto& a, const auto& b) { return a Init-capture [name = expr] creates a new member: the way to move a unique_ptr in, or to precompute something. mutable changes the lambda's own copy; each copy of the lambda has its own state. A generic lambda has a templated operator() : auto parameters are template parameters. A lambda cannot name itself. For recursion, pass it to itself: auto fact = [](auto self, int n) -> int { return n <= 1 ? 1 : n * self(self, n - 1); }; (compiled: 5! = 120), or use std::function . [this] in a member function captures the pointer; [*this] copies the object."},{"f":"15-lambdas.html","n":"Lambdas and Callables","id":"callables","h":"Function pointer, functor, lambda, std::function","x":"Function pointer, functor, lambda, std::function Four ways to pass \"something callable\": Kind Can hold state? Inlinable? Overhead function pointer no rarely (indirect call) 8 bytes functor (class with operator() ) yes yes: type known at compile time size of its members lambda yes (captures) yes: it is a functor size of its captures std::function<R(Args...)> yes no: type-erased indirect call, and may allocate template void apply(F f) { f(1); } // template parameter: the lambda's exact type, fully inlined void apply2(std::function f) { f(1); } // type-erased: accepts anything, one indirect call std::vector > ops{ [](int x) { return x + 1; }, [k = 10](int x) { return x * k; } }; // different lambdas, one type Templates for hot paths and algorithms ( std::sort takes its comparator as a template parameter and inlines it). std::function when you must store callables of different types or keep them behind an interface. std::function may allocate when the callable does not fit its small buffer. Compiled with libstdc++: a small capture did not allocate, a 64-byte capture did. std::invoke(f, args...) calls anything callable, including a member function pointer: std::invoke(&Account::balance, acct) . A stateless lambda converts to a function pointer, handy for C APIs and callbacks. std::bind : know it, prefer a lambda using namespace std::placeholders; auto f = std::bind(add3, 1, _1, _2); // fix the first argument, leave two open: f(2, 3) is add3(1, 2, 3) auto g = std::bind(add3, _2, _1, 9); // placeholders can reorder: g(4, 5) is add3(5, 4, 9) auto h = [](int b, int c) { return add3(1, b, c); }; // the same as f, and easier to read Compiled: f(2, 3) gave 123, g(4, 5) gave 549 and the lambda gave 123. std::bind predates lambdas (C++11); it copies its arguments, hides overloads and produces unreadable errors. You will see it in older code, so be able to read it; in new code write a lambda, which also inlines better. Key point \"Lambda vs std::function ?\" A lambda has its own type and is inlined; std::function erases the type, costs an indirect call and maybe an allocation. Use it only when you must store different callables in one place. Read more C++ Primer (Lippman, Lajoie, Moo) 14.8 The function-call operator (PDF pages 600 to 607)"},{"f":"15-lambdas.html","n":"Lambdas and Callables","id":"cheat","h":"Cheat sheet","x":"Cheat sheet Answer aloud, then open. What is a lambda and what does the compiler generate? An unnamed function object: the compiler creates a unique class with the captured variables as members and the body as operator() . Because it has its own type, calls can be inlined. Capture by value vs by reference? By value copies the variable when the lambda is created (const inside unless mutable ). By reference stores a reference to the live variable and dangles if the lambda outlives it. Capture by value for lambdas that escape the scope or run on another thread. How do you move a unique_ptr into a lambda? With a C++14 init-capture: [p = std::move(ptr)] { ... } . A plain capture would need a copy, which unique_ptr forbids. What does mutable do on a lambda? It makes operator() non-const so the lambda can modify its by-value captures. The changes live in the lambda's own copy, not in the original variable. std::function vs a template parameter? A template parameter keeps the exact callable type, so the call is inlined and nothing allocates. std::function erases the type: one indirect call, possible allocation, but it can store different callables behind one type. What is a generic lambda? A lambda with auto parameters (C++14): its operator() is a template, so one lambda works for any argument type. What is wrong with capturing this by [=] ? [=] inside a member function captures the this pointer, not a copy of the object, so the lambda dangles if the object is destroyed first. It is deprecated in C++20: write [=, this] or [*this] to copy."},{"f":"05-templates.html","n":"C++ Templates","id":"s1","h":"Why templates, and function templates","x":"Why templates, and function templates template<T> maxOf a blueprint: generates no code int maxOf(int, int) double maxOf(double, double) string maxOf(string, string) real functions, made on first use Compile-time polymorphism: no runtime cost, more code per type. Without templates: maxInt , maxDouble , maxString ... A template takes the type as a parameter: template T maxOf(T a, T b) { return a (3, 2.5);// explicit: T = double, the int is converted The template is not compiled itself. Each new type instantiates a real function: no runtime cost, more binary size, and errors appear only when a bad type is used. Pick a type: Key point Templates: compile-time (static) polymorphism, fastest, inlinable, more code, types known at compile time. Virtual: runtime , one code path, mixed objects, a vtable indirection. Read more C++ Primer (Lippman, Lajoie, Moo) 16.1 Defining a template (PDF pages 681 to 706)"},{"f":"05-templates.html","n":"C++ Templates","id":"deduction","h":"Template argument deduction","x":"Template argument deduction When you call a function template, the compiler works out T from the arguments. The rule depends on the shape of the parameter , and it is the same rule auto uses. Parameter Call Deduced T Rule T x (by value) f(ci) with const int ci int a copy: top-level const and references are dropped T x (by value) f(arr) with int arr[3] int* arrays and functions decay to pointers T& x f(ci) const int the reference keeps the argument's const T& x f(arr) int[3] an array keeps its size (usable to get its length) const T& x f(5) int binds temporaries; const is not part of T T&& x f(i) / f(5) int& / int forwarding reference (see Forwarding references ) Compiled and checked with static_assert : by value, const int deduced int and the array deduced int* ; by reference they deduced const int& and int(&)[3] . Deduction looks only at the arguments , never at the return type, and no conversions happen: maxOf(3, 2.5) fails because T would be both int and double . Default arguments and braced lists do not take part in deduction: f({1, 2}) cannot deduce T . Name T explicitly: f<std::vector<int>>({1, 2}) . Name the type yourself when needed: maxOf<double>(3, 2.5) . Class template argument deduction (C++17) std::pair p{1, 2.5}; // std::pair std::vector v{1, 2, 3}; // std::vector std::array a{1, 2, 3}; // std::array template struct Box { T value; }; template Box(T) -> Box ; // a user-defined deduction guide (needed for aggregates before C++20) Box b{std::string(\"x\")}; // Box The compiler deduces the class's arguments from the constructor call. A deduction guide tells it how when the constructors alone are not enough. All of the above was compiled and checked. When deduction fails The error says \"couldn't deduce template parameter\". Look for conflicting arguments, a braced list, or a parameter that does not mention T at all (e.g. only the return type does). Read more C++ Primer (Lippman, Lajoie, Moo) 16.2 Template argument deduction (PDF pages 707 to 722) 16.3 Overloading and templates (PDF pages 723 to 727) Effective Modern C++ (Meyers) Item 1, Understand template type deduction (PDF pages 27 to 36)"},{"f":"05-templates.html","n":"C++ Templates","id":"s2","h":"Class templates","x":"Class templates Same idea for classes: vector<T> , map<K,V> , unique_ptr<T> . template // a type parameter, a NON-TYPE parameter, with a default class Stack { std::array items_; std::size_t size_ = 0; public: bool push(const T& v) { if (size_ == Capacity) return false; items_[size_++] = v; return true; } T pop() { return items_[--size_]; } bool empty() const { return size_ == 0; } }; Stack a; // Stack Stack b; // a different, unrelated type std::pair p{1, 2.5}; // C++17 CTAD: class template argument deduction -> std::pair std::vector v{1, 2, 3}; // std::vector Non-type parameters are compile-time constants (how std::array<int,5> knows its size). Each instantiation is a separate, unrelated class. Members are instantiated only if called. Why templates live in headers The compiler needs the body at instantiation. In a .cpp , others see only a declaration: linker errors. Use headers, or explicit instantiation ( template class Stack<int>; ). Read more C++ Primer (Lippman, Lajoie, Moo) 16.1 Defining a template (PDF pages 681 to 706) Programming: Principles and Practice (Stroustrup) 18.1 to 18.3 Templates, generalizing Vector, range checking and exceptions (PDF pages 527 to 541)"},{"f":"05-templates.html","n":"C++ Templates","id":"s3","h":"Specialization","x":"Specialization A specialization replaces the generic template for specific arguments. template struct Printer { // primary template static void print(const T& v) { std::cout // full specialization for bool struct Printer { static void print(bool v) { std::cout struct Printer { // PARTIAL specialization: any pointer type static void print(T* p) { std::cout Kind Meaning Allowed for Full specialization all parameters fixed: template<> ... Printer<bool> class and function templates Partial specialization a pattern: Printer<T*> class templates only Functions: prefer overloads No partial specialization for function templates. Overload instead; an exact non-template match wins. Function specializations do not take part in overload resolution. Read more C++ Primer (Lippman, Lajoie, Moo) 16.5 Template specializations (PDF pages 735 to 743)"},{"f":"05-templates.html","n":"C++ Templates","id":"s5","h":"Variadic templates and fold expressions","x":"Variadic templates and fold expressions A parameter pack ( typename... Args ) takes any number of arguments; args... expands it. Before C++17: recursion with a base case. template T sum(T v) { return v; } // base case: one argument template T sum(T first, Rest... rest) { return first + sum(rest...); } // peel one off, recurse sum(1, 2, 3); The compiler instantiates a chain of real functions: C++17 fold expressions replace the recursion: template auto sum(Args... args) { return (args + ...); } // unary right fold: a + (b + (c ...)) template void print(Args&&... args) { ((std::cout Behind make_unique , make_shared , emplace_back , tuple , thread , function . Read more C++ Primer (Lippman, Lajoie, Moo) 16.4 Variadic templates (PDF pages 728 to 734)"},{"f":"05-templates.html","n":"C++ Templates","id":"s4","h":"Forwarding references and perfect forwarding","x":"Forwarding references and perfect forwarding template<class T> void f(T&& x) f(lvalue) s T = X& X& && collapses to X& f(rvalue) string(\"x\") T = X parameter is X&& Collapsing rule: any lvalue reference involved gives an lvalue reference. std::forward restores the original category. One signature accepts both; std::forward passes the category on. T&& in a function template is a forwarding reference , not an rvalue reference: it binds to anything and remembers lvalue vs rvalue. Explore: Is it really a forwarding reference? Only when T is deduced for that function and the form is exactly T&& or auto&& : Declaration Forwarding reference? template<class T> void f(T&& x) yes: T is deduced for f auto&& x = expr; yes template<class T> void f(std::vector<T>&& v) no: not exactly T&& , so it binds rvalues only template<class T> void f(const T&& x) no: const disqualifies it template<class T> struct S { void f(T&& x); }; no: T is fixed by the class, so nothing is deduced for f void f(Widget&& w) no: a plain rvalue reference Reference collapsing Reference to reference collapses: any lvalue reference makes it an lvalue reference; only && + && stays rvalue. Written Collapses to T& & , T& && , T&& & T& T&& && T&& Perfect forwarding A wrapper's parameter has a name , so it is an lvalue and the callee would always copy. std::forward restores the category: template std::unique_ptr makeUnique(Args&&... args) { // forwarding references return std::unique_ptr (new T(std::forward (args)...)); // lvalues stay lvalues, rvalues stay rvalues } std::string s = \"abc\"; auto a = makeUnique (s); // s is an lvalue -> Widget copies it auto b = makeUnique (std::string(\"x\"));// temporary rvalue -> Widget MOVES it std::move vs std::forward move always casts to rvalue. forward<T> casts to rvalue only if the argument was one. Use forward only on a forwarding reference, once. Step through perfect forwarding: Read more C++ Primer (Lippman, Lajoie, Moo) 16.2 Template argument deduction (PDF pages 707 to 722) Effective Modern C++ (Meyers) Item 23, Understand std::move and std::forward (PDF pages 176 to 182) Item 24, Distinguish universal references from rvalue references (PDF pages 182 to 186) Items 26 to 27, and 30 Overloading on universal references; perfect forwarding failure cases (PDF pages 195 to 215 and 225 to 232) C++ Move Semantics (Josuttis) 9.1 to 9.3 Perfect forwarding; universal versus rvalue references (PDF pages 159 to 166) 9.4 to 9.5 Overload resolution with universal references; perfect forwarding in lambdas (PDF pages 167 to 170) Chapter 11 Perfect passing with auto&& (PDF pages 191 to 202)"},{"f":"05-templates.html","n":"C++ Templates","id":"tools","h":"Alias templates, variable templates and more","x":"Alias templates, variable templates and more Tool Example Use Alias template template <typename T> using Table = std::map<std::string, std::vector<T>>; a shorter name that still takes a parameter; unlike typedef it can be templated Variable template (C++14) template <typename T> constexpr T zero = T{}; a constant per type: zero<int> , zero<double> . The standard's std::is_integral_v<T> is one Template template parameter template <template <typename...> class C, typename T> pass a template ( std::vector ) rather than a type: makeTwo<std::vector>(3, 4) Non-type parameter template <typename T, std::size_t N> a value as parameter; auto accepts any type of value in C++17: template <auto V> Abbreviated template (C++20) void f(auto x) each auto parameter is an implicit template parameter template using Table = std::map >; Table t; // std::map > template constexpr T zero = T{}; // zero is 0, zero is 0.0 template class Container, typename T> Container makeTwo(T a, T b) { Container c; c.push_back(a); c.push_back(b); return c; } auto v = makeTwo (3, 4); // a vector with two elements Unpacking with std::index_sequence To call a function with the elements of a std::tuple you need the indices 0, 1, 2... as a pack. std::make_index_sequence<N> generates it: template auto applyImpl(F f, const Tuple& t, std::index_sequence ) { return f(std::get (t)...); } // expands to get , get , ... template auto applyTuple(F f, const Tuple& t) { return applyImpl(f, t, std::make_index_sequence >{}); } applyTuple([](int x, int y, int z) { return x + y + z; }, std::tuple{1, 2, 3}); // 6 (compiled) C++17 ships this as std::apply . Know the idea: a pack of integers lets you expand an expression once per element."},{"f":"05-templates.html","n":"C++ Templates","id":"s6","h":"Type traits and if constexpr","x":"Type traits and if constexpr describe<int> is_integral: compiled and kept is_floating_point: discarded else: discarded not even compiled, so they may use code that is invalid for T A normal if would fail to compile as soon as one branch is invalid for T. A template often needs to ask about its type. <type_traits> answers at compile time ( std::is_integral_v<T> ), and if constexpr (C++17) keeps only the matching branch: template std::string describe(const T& v) { if constexpr (std::is_integral_v ) return \"integer \" + std::to_string(v); else if constexpr (std::is_floating_point_v ) return \"float \" + std::to_string(v); else return \"something else\"; } Traits, SFINAE and enable_if , concepts, constexpr , consteval and template metaprogramming have their own lesson: Type Traits & Compile-time C++ . Read more Effective Modern C++ (Meyers) Items 26 to 27, and 30 Overloading on universal references; perfect forwarding failure cases (PDF pages 195 to 215 and 225 to 232) C++ Move Semantics (Josuttis) Chapter 12 Perfect returning with decltype(auto) (PDF pages 203 to 210)"},{"f":"05-templates.html","n":"C++ Templates","id":"s8","h":"Pitfalls and good practice","x":"Pitfalls and good practice Pitfall Explanation / fix Huge unreadable error messages The error is reported deep inside an instantiation. Read the first error and the line \"required from here\". Concepts or static_assert on traits give early, clear errors. Template body in a .cpp file Linker errors. Put the definition in the header. typename for dependent types Inside a template, T::iterator could be a type or a value. Write typename T::iterator it; . Members of a dependent base class not found Write this->member or Base<T>::member . Code bloat Every distinct T generates more machine code. Move type-independent logic into a non-template helper. Deduction conflicts: maxOf(3, 2.5) Two different deduced T s fail. Cast, specify maxOf<double> , or use two parameters. maxOf(\"a\", \"b\") with const char* Compares pointer addresses, not text. Use std::string or std::string_view . Notes: Module 4 ; modern-cpp-features ."},{"f":"05-templates.html","n":"C++ Templates","id":"s9","h":"Cheat sheet","x":"Cheat sheet How are template arguments deduced? From the arguments of the call, by the shape of the parameter. By value ( T x ): const and references are dropped and arrays decay to pointers. By reference ( T& x ): const and the array size are kept. T&& is a forwarding reference. No conversions take part, so maxOf(3, 2.5) fails; name T explicitly. What are alias templates and variable templates? An alias template is a parameterised using ( template<class T> using Vec = std::vector<T>; ), which a typedef cannot be. A variable template is a constant or variable per type ( template<class T> constexpr T zero = T{}; ); std::is_integral_v is one. What happens when you use a function template? The compiler deduces the template arguments from the call, then instantiates a concrete function for that combination of types (once per distinct combination across the program), and compiles it like normal code. The template itself generates no code. What is a forwarding reference? A T&& parameter where T is deduced for that very function template (or auto&& ). It binds to lvalues and rvalues alike: for an lvalue T becomes X& and reference collapsing makes the parameter X& ; for an rvalue T is X and the parameter is X&& . std::forward then preserves that category. What is perfect forwarding, and why do we need it? Passing arguments through a wrapper to another function without changing their value category or constness, so the callee can still choose copy vs move correctly. Without it you would need an overload per combination of lvalue/rvalue/const for every parameter. Explain SFINAE. If substituting deduced types into a function template's signature yields an invalid type or expression, that candidate is removed from the overload set rather than raising an error. std::enable_if exploits it to enable a function only for suitable types. C++17 if constexpr and C++20 concepts are the clearer modern tools. Full vs partial specialization? Full: all template arguments fixed. Partial: a pattern with some parameters still free ( Foo<T*> ), allowed for class templates and variable templates but not for function templates (use overloading). Why must template definitions be in headers? The compiler needs the body to instantiate it in each translation unit that uses a particular type. If only the declaration is visible, no instantiation happens and the linker cannot find the function. Alternative: explicit instantiation in one .cpp . What is a variadic template and how do you process the pack? A template taking zero or more arguments via a parameter pack ( typename... Args ). Expand with args... . Process by recursion with a base case, or in C++17 with a fold expression like (args + ...) . Templates vs inheritance for polymorphism? Templates give static (compile-time) polymorphism: no runtime cost and inlinable, but the type must be known when compiling and each type duplicates code. Inheritance with virtual functions gives dynamic (runtime) polymorphism: one code path and heterogeneous collections, at the cost of an indirection and heap-allocated objects."},{"f":"03-core-language.html","n":"Core Language: RAII and Exceptions","id":"s1","h":"RAII: the central idea","x":"RAII: the central idea RAII : tie a resource (memory, file, lock, socket) to an object. Acquire in the constructor, release in the destructor. Destructors run even when an exception is thrown, so nothing leaks. class File { std::FILE* f_; public: explicit File(const char* path) : f_(std::fopen(path, \"r\")) {} // acquire ~File() { if (f_) std::fclose(f_); } // release File(const File&) = delete; // one owner only File& operator=(const File&) = delete; }; Watch a throw in the middle: vector , unique_ptr , lock_guard , fstream are all RAII. Never manage a resource with a bare new / delete , lock() / unlock() or fopen / fclose : wrap it. Key point RAII binds resource lifetime to object lifetime: automatic, exception-safe cleanup. It replaces try/finally and removes the need for a garbage collector. Read more Programming: Principles and Practice (Stroustrup) 18.4 to 18.5 Resources and exceptions; resource-management pointers (RAII, unique_ptr) (PDF pages 542 to 553)"},{"f":"03-core-language.html","n":"Core Language: RAII and Exceptions","id":"s7","h":"Exceptions and stack unwinding","x":"Exceptions and stack unwinding double divide(double a, double b) { if (b == 0) throw std::invalid_argument(\"division by zero\"); return a / b; } try { divide(1, 0); } catch (const std::invalid_argument& e) { // catch by const reference std::cerr Throw by value, catch by const& (catching by value slices a derived exception). Stack unwinding : leaving scopes runs destructors, in reverse order of construction, until a catch matches. This is why RAII is exception-safe. A destructor must never throw : if it throws while another exception is unwinding, std::terminate is called. Destructors are implicitly noexcept . A constructor that throws leaves no object: the destructors of already-built members and bases run, its own does not. catch (...) catches everything; rethrow with a bare throw; (never throw e; , which copies and slices). Derive your own exceptions from std::exception (or a standard subclass) so generic handlers still work. Cost The no-throw path is almost free, but a throw is orders of magnitude slower than a return and its latency is unpredictable. Do not throw on a hot path or as ordinary control flow (see Exceptions, error codes and optional ). Read more C++ Primer (Lippman, Lajoie, Moo) 5.6 try blocks and exception handling (PDF pages 222 to 229) 18.1 Exception handling (PDF pages 801 to 813) Programming: Principles and Practice (Stroustrup) 4.5 to 4.6 Run-time errors and exceptions (PDF pages 102 to 111) 18.4 to 18.5 Resources and exceptions; resource-management pointers (RAII, unique_ptr) (PDF pages 542 to 553)"},{"f":"03-core-language.html","n":"Core Language: RAII and Exceptions","id":"raii-types","h":"RAII everywhere: guards and standard wrappers","x":"RAII everywhere: guards and standard wrappers Almost every resource has a standard RAII wrapper. Use it instead of a manual acquire/release pair. Resource RAII type Released by heap memory vector , string , unique_ptr destructor frees the block a file std::fstream destructor closes it a mutex lock_guard , unique_lock , scoped_lock destructor unlocks a thread std::jthread (C++20) destructor joins anything else your own scope guard destructor runs a cleanup template class ScopeExit { // runs F when the scope ends, however it ends F f_; bool active_ = true; public: explicit ScopeExit(F f) : f_(std::move(f)) {} ScopeExit(const ScopeExit&) = delete; // a guard must not be copied void release() { active_ = false; } // commit: cancel the cleanup ~ScopeExit() { if (active_) f_(); } }; void transfer() { begin(); ScopeExit rollback([] { undo(); }); // runs on return AND on an exception step1(); step2(); // any of these may throw rollback.release(); // success: keep the changes } A RAII class owns one resource. Copying it must be deleted (one owner) or a deep copy ; moving transfers ownership (the Rule of Five, OOP lesson ; Move Semantics ). Prefer the Rule of Zero : hold resources in members that are already RAII, and you write no destructor at all. Destructors must not throw (see Exceptions and stack unwinding and Exception-safety guarantees ). Key point \"What is RAII?\" The resource's lifetime is tied to an object: acquire in the constructor, release in the destructor. Destructors run on every exit path, including exceptions, so nothing leaks and no finally is needed."},{"f":"03-core-language.html","n":"Core Language: RAII and Exceptions","id":"guarantees","h":"Exception-safety guarantees and noexcept","x":"Exception-safety guarantees and noexcept Level Promise if an exception escapes Typical example No-throw it never throws destructors, swap , moves, anything noexcept Strong state is exactly as before (commit or rollback) vector::push_back when the element's move is noexcept Basic nothing leaks and invariants hold, but values may have changed most operations Getting the strong guarantee: build aside, then swap void Table::replaceAll(const std::vector & fresh) { std::vector copy(fresh); // may throw: *this is still untouched rows_.swap(copy); // commit: swap cannot throw } Do everything that can fail on a copy , then publish the result with an operation that cannot throw. If anything throws before the swap, the object is unchanged. noexcept noexcept promises a function will not throw. If it does anyway, the program calls std::terminate : there is no unwinding past it. Destructors are noexcept by default. A throwing destructor during unwinding means two exceptions at once, and the program terminates. Mark move operations and swap noexcept when they cannot throw. std::vector grows by moving its elements only if the move constructor is noexcept ; otherwise it copies , to keep the strong guarantee. Compiled example: growing a vector whose elements have a noexcept move made 0 copies; with a possibly-throwing move it copied every element. noexcept(expr) asks the compiler whether an expression can throw; generic code uses it to pick the cheap path ( std::move_if_noexcept ). Do not mark a function noexcept just to be fast: a wrong promise turns an error into a crash. Read more C++ Primer (Lippman, Lajoie, Moo) 18.1 Exception handling (PDF pages 801 to 813) Effective Modern C++ (Meyers) Item 14, Declare functions noexcept if they won't emit exceptions (PDF pages 108 to 115) C++ Move Semantics (Josuttis) 7.1 and 7.4 Move constructors with and without noexcept; when and where to use noexcept (PDF pages 125 to 134 and 140 to 141)"},{"f":"03-core-language.html","n":"Core Language: RAII and Exceptions","id":"errors","h":"Exceptions, error codes and optional","x":"Exceptions, error codes and optional Situation Use a failure the caller cannot sensibly handle here (out of memory, broken invariant, constructor failure) exception : it unwinds to code that can cope an expected failure in normal flow (bad input, \"not found\", parse error) return a value that says so: std::optional , an error code or enum, std::expected (C++23) a hot path with steady latency (real-time code) no exceptions : error codes or optional ( Performance & Memory ) a noexcept context, or a destructor never throw: catch inside and log or ignore std::optional parseInt(std::string_view s) { // failure is expected: no exception int v = 0; auto r = std::from_chars(s.data(), s.data() + s.size(), v); if (r.ec != std::errc{} || r.ptr != s.data() + s.size()) return std::nullopt; return v; } if (auto n = parseInt(input)) use(*n); // the caller must look at the result else report(\"not a number\"); Exceptions keep the normal path clean and cannot be silently ignored; the cost is that a throw is slow and the control flow is invisible. Return values make failure explicit and cheap; the cost is that every caller must check, and [[nodiscard]] helps remind them. Constructors cannot return a code: either throw, or use a factory function returning optional or an expected . Be consistent inside a module, and document what a function can throw. Key point \"Exceptions or error codes?\" Exceptions for rare failures that need unwinding, return values for expected failures and for hot paths. Mention RAII (so nothing leaks either way) and that a destructor must never throw."},{"f":"03-core-language.html","n":"Core Language: RAII and Exceptions","id":"s11","h":"Cheat sheet","x":"Cheat sheet Answer aloud, then open. What is RAII and why is it important? It binds a resource's lifetime to an object: acquire in the constructor, release in the destructor. Cleanup is automatic and exception-safe because destructors run on every exit path. It replaces try/finally and is why C++ needs no garbage collector. What is stack unwinding? When an exception propagates, the runtime exits scopes up the call stack, calling the destructors of all fully constructed locals until a handler is found. It is what makes RAII exception-safe. Why should destructors not throw? Destructors run during unwinding. If one throws while another exception is active, std::terminate is called. Destructors are noexcept by default; catch and handle errors inside them. What are the exception-safety guarantees? No-throw (never throws: destructors, swap, moves), strong (if it throws, the state is exactly as before: commit or rollback), and basic (nothing leaks and invariants hold, but values may have changed). Get the strong guarantee by doing the work on a copy and publishing with a non-throwing swap. Why mark move operations noexcept ? std::vector moves its elements when it grows only if the move cannot throw; otherwise it copies them to keep the strong guarantee. A missing noexcept silently turns every reallocation into a round of copies. What happens if a noexcept function throws? std::terminate is called: the exception does not propagate. So only promise noexcept when it is true. Exceptions or error codes? Exceptions for rare failures the local code cannot handle (and constructors); return values ( optional , error codes, expected ) for expected failures and for hot paths where a throw's cost and unpredictability are unacceptable. How do you run cleanup on every path without finally ? Put it in a destructor: a standard RAII wrapper ( lock_guard , unique_ptr , fstream ) or a small scope guard class. Release the guard on success to commit."},{"f":"21-vocabulary-types.html","n":"Vocabulary Types","id":"why","h":"Why vocabulary types","x":"Why vocabulary types A signature like int find(const char* s) hides its contract: does -1 mean \"not found\"? Is s allowed to be null? Who owns the result? A vocabulary type puts the answer in the type, so every reader and the compiler see it. The question The old answer The type Two or three values back from one function? out-parameters, a throwaway struct std::pair , std::tuple , or a named struct A value that may be absent? a magic value ( -1 ), a null pointer std::optional<T> One of several different types? a union plus a tag, a base class std::variant<A, B> A value, or the reason there is none? an error code plus an out-parameter, an exception std::expected<T, E> (C++23) A run of elements? a pointer plus a count std::span<T> , std::string_view All of them are ordinary value types: no heap allocation, no virtual functions, nothing to free."},{"f":"21-vocabulary-types.html","n":"Vocabulary Types","id":"pairs","h":"pair , tuple and structured bindings","x":"pair , tuple and structured bindings std::pair<A, B> holds two values, std::tuple<A, B, C...> any number. Structured bindings (C++17) unpack them into named variables. std::pair minmax(const std::vector & v); // returns {smallest, largest} auto [lo, hi] = minmax({3, 9, 1, 7}); // lo = 1, hi = 9: two names instead of .first / .second auto t = std::make_tuple(1, 2.5, std::string(\"x\")); std::get (t); // 2.5 (index must be a compile-time constant) std::get (t); // \"x\" (or by type, when the type appears once) std::map m{{\"a\", 1}}; auto [it, inserted] = m.insert({\"a\", 2}); // insert returns pair // inserted == false, it->second == 1: the key existed, nothing changed A pair or tuple has no names : .first and std::get<2> say nothing about meaning. For a result that leaves a function and is used in more than one place, a small named struct reads better. Comparison is built in and lexicographic: pairs and tuples compare element by element, so they work as map keys and in std::sort . std::tie(a, b) = f(); assigns into existing variables; std::ignore skips one. Structured bindings also unpack plain structs and arrays: auto [x, y] = point; . They are copies unless you write auto& or const auto& . Read more C++ Primer (Lippman, Lajoie, Moo) 17.1 The tuple type (PDF pages 747 to 751)"},{"f":"21-vocabulary-types.html","n":"Vocabulary Types","id":"optional","h":"optional : a value or nothing","x":"optional : a value or nothing std::optional<T> holds either a T or nothing. The \"nothing\" is part of the type, so a caller cannot forget it exists. std::optional parseInt(std::string_view s) { int v = 0; auto r = std::from_chars(s.data(), s.data() + s.size(), v); if (r.ec != std::errc{} || r.ptr != s.data() + s.size()) return std::nullopt; // \"no value\" return v; // converts to optional } if (auto n = parseInt(\"42\")) use(*n); // test, then dereference: n is true when a value is present parseInt(\"4x\").value_or(-1); // -1: a default for the empty case parseInt(\"\").has_value(); // false std::optional e; e.value(); // throws std::bad_optional_access *e; // undefined behaviour: no check, like dereferencing a null pointer Operation On an empty optional if (o) / o.has_value() false *o , o->member undefined behaviour: check first o.value() throws bad_optional_access o.value_or(x) returns x o.reset() , o = std::nullopt makes it empty The value lives inside the optional (compiled: sizeof(int) is 4, sizeof(optional<int>) is 8: the value plus a flag, padded). No heap allocation, unlike a pointer or unique_ptr . Use it for a result that is legitimately absent: a lookup that may miss, a parse of untrusted input, an optional configuration value. It does not say why the value is missing; for that, use expected . Not a replacement for a reference or pointer that means \"refers to an object owned elsewhere\": optional<T&> does not exist. Use a pointer there. Marking a function [[nodiscard]] keeps callers from dropping the result."},{"f":"21-vocabulary-types.html","n":"Vocabulary Types","id":"variant","h":"variant and std::visit","x":"variant and std::visit std::variant<A, B, C> is a type-safe union: it holds exactly one of its alternatives and knows which. variant<int, string> holding 7 index 0 storage: int 7 (string unused) after v = \"hi\" index 1 storage: string \"hi\" Size: the largest alternative plus the index. No heap allocation for the variant itself. One alternative at a time; the index says which. std::variant v = 7; v.index(); // 0: the int is active std::holds_alternative (v); // true std::get (v); // 7 std::get (v); // throws std::bad_variant_access: the wrong alternative if (auto* s = std::get_if (&v)) { } // a pointer, or nullptr: no exception v = std::string(\"hi\"); // now index 1; the int is gone std::variant d; // default: holds a value-initialised FIRST alternative (index 0, int 0) Visiting every alternative std::visit calls a function with whichever alternative is active. Give it a callable that handles every alternative, and a missing one is a compile error , not a silent bug. template struct overloaded : Ts... { using Ts::operator()...; }; // glue lambdas into one callable template overloaded(Ts...) -> overloaded ; // deduction guide (needed before C++20) struct Circle { double r; }; struct Rect { double w, h; }; using Shape = std::variant ; // no base class, no virtual functions double area(const Shape& s) { return std::visit(overloaded{ [](const Circle& c) { return 3.0 * c.r * c.r; }, [](const Rect& r) { return r.w * r.h; } }, s); } std::vector shapes{Circle{2}, Rect{3, 4}}; // a heterogeneous collection stored by value // total area: 12 + 12 = 24 (compiled) Variant or virtual functions? A variant fits a closed set of types you control (messages, tokens, syntax-tree nodes), where you keep adding operations. Virtual functions fit an open set that others may extend. The trade-off is spelled out under Visitor in More Patterns . If an alternative's constructor throws during assignment, the variant can end up valueless by exception ( valueless_by_exception() ). It is rare, and it is why you should keep alternatives cheap and non-throwing to move. A reference alternative is ill-formed (use std::reference_wrapper ). The same type twice is legal, but then you can only reach it by index, not by type. std::monostate is an empty alternative: put it first to make a variant default-constructible when no alternative should be the default. Read more C++ Primer (Lippman, Lajoie, Moo) 19.5 to 19.6 Nested classes and unions (PDF pages 872 to 880)"},{"f":"21-vocabulary-types.html","n":"Vocabulary Types","id":"expected","h":"expected : a value or an error","x":"expected : a value or an error std::expected<T, E> (C++23) holds either the result T or an error E . It is optional that explains why the value is missing, and an alternative to exceptions for failures that are normal. enum class ParseError { Empty, NotANumber }; std::expected parse(std::string_view s) { if (s.empty()) return std::unexpected(ParseError::Empty); // the error path if (auto n = parseInt(s)) return *n; // the value path return std::unexpected(ParseError::NotANumber); } auto r = parse(\"12\"); if (r) use(*r); // true: it holds a value else report(r.error()); // otherwise: the error parse(\"zz\").value_or(0); // 0: a default when it holds an error parse(\"\").value(); // throws std::bad_expected_access Failure style Cost and visibility Use for Exception invisible in the signature; a throw is slow rare failures the caller cannot handle here, constructors Error code plus out-parameter easy to ignore, clumsy to write C APIs optional<T> visible, free, says nothing about why \"not found\", where the reason is obvious expected<T, E> visible, free, carries the reason expected failures: parsing, I/O, validation It needs a C++23 standard library (GCC 12 or later, recent MSVC). With an older one, std::variant<T, E> or a small result struct does the same job, and the free tl::expected library has the same interface. Mark the function [[nodiscard]] so the result cannot be ignored."},{"f":"21-vocabulary-types.html","n":"Vocabulary Types","id":"views","h":"span and string_view : non-owning views","x":"span and string_view : non-owning views A view is a pointer plus a length: it looks at elements that someone else owns. std::span<T> (C++20) views any contiguous run of T ; std::string_view (C++17) is the same idea for characters. long sum(std::span xs) { long t = 0; for (int x : xs) t += x; return t; } std::vector vec{1, 2, 3, 4, 5}; int arr[3] = {10, 20, 30}; sum(vec); // 15: a vector converts to a span with no copy sum(arr); // 60: so does a C array, and its size is remembered sum(std::span(vec).subspan(1, 3)); // 9: elements 2, 3, 4: a slice is just a new pointer and length sum(std::span(vec).first(2)); // 3 A span is two words (compiled: 16 bytes on a 64-bit machine). Pass it by value . span<const int> is read-only; span<int> lets the function modify the elements in place. It still cannot grow or shrink the container. It replaces the (T* data, size_t n) pair. Overflows are easier to spot, and a function written against span accepts a vector, an array, a std::array or a part of any of them. Dangling. A view must not outlive what it looks at. A span of a vector that then reallocates ( push_back ), or a string_view of a temporary string, points at freed memory. Use views for parameters and short-lived local use, not for members that live longer than the data. Rule of thumb Take a view to read or modify existing elements; take or return the owning type ( vector , string ) to keep them."},{"f":"21-vocabulary-types.html","n":"Vocabulary Types","id":"choose","h":"Which one when","x":"Which one when Situation Use Return two related values that are used right away pair / structured bindings; a named struct if they travel further A lookup that may find nothing optional<T> A parse or I/O call whose failure has a reason expected<T, E> (or an exception if it is rare) A value that is one of a few known types variant plus visit A function that reads a run of elements span<const T> ; string_view for text A value of any type, unknown at compile time std::any (rare: you lose compile-time checking; prefer a variant) The same idea runs through all of them: make illegal or missing states explicit in the type so the compiler, not a comment, enforces them."},{"f":"21-vocabulary-types.html","n":"Vocabulary Types","id":"cheat","h":"Cheat sheet","x":"Cheat sheet Answer aloud, then open. What is a vocabulary type? A small standard type that expresses a common idea in a signature: optional (maybe a value), variant (one of several types), expected (a value or an error), span and string_view (a non-owning view). They replace magic values, tagged unions and pointer-plus-count pairs, so the contract is visible and checked. optional versus a pointer for \"may be absent\"? optional<T> holds the value itself (no heap allocation, no ownership question) and is empty when absent. A pointer means \"refers to something owned elsewhere\" and can be null. Use optional for a possibly missing value , a pointer for a possibly missing reference . What happens on *opt , opt.value() and opt.value_or(x) when it is empty? *opt is undefined behaviour, value() throws std::bad_optional_access , and value_or(x) returns x . Test with if (opt) first when you can. How does std::variant differ from a union ? A variant remembers which alternative is active ( index() ), destroys the old one and constructs the new one on assignment, works with non-trivial types such as std::string , and throws bad_variant_access if you read the wrong one. A raw union does none of that, and reading the wrong member is undefined behaviour. Why is std::visit safer than a chain of if / get_if ? The visitor must handle every alternative, so adding a type to the variant makes every incomplete visitor a compile error instead of a silent missing case. It also reads the active alternative without a manual check. Variant or virtual functions for a set of message or shape types? A variant when the set of types is closed and you keep adding operations: values are stored inline, there are no allocations or virtual calls, and the compiler checks completeness. Virtual functions when the set of types is open for others to extend and the operations are few. optional , expected , exception or error code? optional when \"no value\" needs no explanation. expected<T, E> (C++23) when the failure is normal and the reason matters. An exception for rare failures the local code cannot handle, and for constructors. An error code only at C boundaries. What does std::span replace, and what is its danger? It replaces a (pointer, count) pair: a non-owning view of contiguous elements that remembers its size and accepts a vector, an array or a part of either, without copying. Like string_view it dangles if the data is destroyed or reallocated while the view is alive. How do you return several values from a function? A std::pair or std::tuple , unpacked with structured bindings ( auto [a, b] = f(); ), or a small named struct when the fields need names or the result travels. Not out-parameters, except where you want to reuse a buffer."},{"f":"13-move-semantics.html","n":"Move Semantics","id":"values","h":"Lvalues, rvalues and value categories","x":"Lvalues, rvalues and value categories An lvalue has a name and an address ( x , v[0] ). An rvalue is an unnamed temporary ( 5 , a + b , a returned value): about to vanish, so its contents can be stolen safely. Category Roughly Examples lvalue a named object x , v[0] prvalue an unnamed temporary 5 , a + b , a call that returns by value xvalue an object marked as movable std::move(x) \"Rvalue\" means prvalue or xvalue. Which reference a value can bind to: Reference type Binds to Typical use T& lvalues only modify the caller's object const T& lvalues and rvalues read-only, avoid a copy T&& (rvalue reference) rvalues only move constructors and move assignment void f(const std::string& s); // accepts anything, copies nothing void g(std::string&& s); // accepts only temporaries: \"I may take your contents\" std::string name = \"abc\"; f(name); // OK // g(name); // ERROR: name is an lvalue g(std::string(\"x\")); // OK: temporary g(std::move(name)); // OK: std::move says \"treat name as a temporary\" int x = 0; static_assert(std::is_same_v ); // double parentheses report the category: static_assert(std::is_same_v ); // lvalue int&, xvalue int&&, prvalue plain int static_assert(std::is_same_v ); A name is always an lvalue Inside g , s is an lvalue despite its && type. Pass it on with std::move(s) (or std::forward , see Templates ). Read more C++ Primer (Lippman, Lajoie, Moo) 4.1 Fundamentals (PDF pages 163 to 167) 13.6 Moving objects (PDF pages 560 to 579) C++ Move Semantics (Josuttis) 8.1.2 to 8.1.3 and 8.4 to 8.5 Value categories since C++11 and C++17; when lvalues become rvalues and the reverse (PDF pages 145 to 148 and 153 to 154) 8.2 to 8.3 and 8.6 Special value-category rules; binding references; checking with decltype (PDF pages 148 to 153 and 154 to 156)"},{"f":"13-move-semantics.html","n":"Move Semantics","id":"copy-move","h":"Copy versus move","x":"Copy versus move Copy: b = a a 1,000,000 ints b a second copy allocates and copies a million elements Move: b = std::move(a) a: null b same buffer b stole the pointer: two assignments, no copying. The moved-from object is valid but unspecified: destroy it or assign to it. std::move only casts; the move constructor does the stealing. Copying a million-element vector copies a million elements. If the source is a dying temporary, that is wasted work. A move just steals the internal pointer . std::move(x) moves nothing : it is a cast to an rvalue reference meaning \"you may steal\". The move constructor or assignment does the stealing. Overload resolution only looks at the kind of expression: an lvalue picks the copy, an rvalue picks the move. A moved-from object is valid but unspecified : destroy it or assign a new value, do not read it. Compiled: a moved-from std::string had size 0, and assigning to it worked. Key point \"What does std::move do?\" Nothing at runtime: an unconditional cast to an rvalue reference. The move constructor does the work. Read more C++ Primer (Lippman, Lajoie, Moo) 13.6 Moving objects (PDF pages 560 to 579) Effective Modern C++ (Meyers) Item 23, Understand std::move and std::forward (PDF pages 176 to 182) C++ Move Semantics (Josuttis) 1.1 Motivation for move semantics (PDF pages 21 to 36) 1.2 to 1.4 Implementing move semantics; copying as a fallback; const objects (PDF pages 36 to 41) Chapter 2 Core features of move semantics (PDF pages 43 to 52) 6.1 to 6.2 Moved-from states (required and guaranteed states; destructible and assignable) (PDF pages 107 to 115)"},{"f":"13-move-semantics.html","n":"Move Semantics","id":"writing","h":"Writing move operations","x":"Writing move operations A class that owns a raw resource needs the Rule of Five ( OOP lesson ). The move operations steal and leave the source safely empty: class Buffer { int* data_; std::size_t n_; public: Buffer(Buffer&& o) noexcept // steal and reset, in one line each : data_(std::exchange(o.data_, nullptr)), n_(std::exchange(o.n_, 0)) {} Buffer& operator=(Buffer o) noexcept { // copy-and-swap: by value covers copy AND move std::swap(data_, o.data_); std::swap(n_, o.n_); return *this; } /* ... constructor, destructor, copy constructor ... */ }; std::exchange(x, v) returns the old x and sets it to v : the idiom for \"steal and reset\". Mark moves noexcept . std::vector moves its elements on reallocation only if the move cannot throw; otherwise it copies them ( exception safety ). The by-value operator= handles self-assignment and gives the strong guarantee. The classic pair operator=(const T&) and operator=(T&&) is faster when you can reuse the existing storage. If you can avoid owning a raw resource, do (Rule of Zero): vector and unique_ptr members make the compiler-generated moves correct. Read more C++ Primer (Lippman, Lajoie, Moo) 13.6 Moving objects (PDF pages 560 to 579) Programming: Principles and Practice (Stroustrup) 17.4 to 17.5 Copying and moving; essential operations (PDF pages 501 to 512) 17.6 to 17.9 Other operations, remaining Vector problems, changing size (PDF pages 513 to 523) Effective Modern C++ (Meyers) Item 14, Declare functions noexcept if they won't emit exceptions (PDF pages 108 to 115) C++ Move Semantics (Josuttis) 6.3 Dealing with broken invariants (PDF pages 115 to 124)"},{"f":"13-move-semantics.html","n":"Move Semantics","id":"returning","h":"Returning objects: elision and std::move","x":"Returning objects: elision and std::move Compiled with a counting type (copies / moves performed): What you write Copies Moves Why return Counter(); 0 0 guaranteed elision (C++17): built in the caller's storage Counter c; return c; 0 0 NRVO: allowed, not required, but done in practice return std::move(c); 0 1 blocks NRVO: an extra move, so never write it return a by-value parameter 0 1 no elision for parameters, but it is moved, not copied return member; 1 0 a member is never moved implicitly return std::move(member); 0 1 here std::move is right: the object keeps living, you take its member Buffer make() { Buffer b(1000); return b; } // fine: elided or moved automatically Buffer take(Source& s) { return std::move(s.buffer); } // member of an object that outlives the call: move it out Rule Return locals and temporaries plainly. Use std::move on a member , or on something that is not the local you are returning by name. Read more Effective Modern C++ (Meyers) Item 25, Use std::move on rvalue references, std::forward on universal references (PDF pages 186 to 195)"},{"f":"13-move-semantics.html","n":"Move Semantics","id":"traps","h":"Sink parameters and move traps","x":"Sink parameters and move traps Taking a parameter you will store class Person { std::string name_; public: explicit Person(std::string n) : name_(std::move(n)) {} // by value, then move into the member }; Compiled with a counting type: Holder(lvalue) and Holder(rvalue) together made 1 copy and 2 moves : the lvalue is copied once into the parameter, and each call moves once into the member. For a constructor that stores its argument , take it by value and move. Not for setters: the extra construction can cost more than assigning. Traps Trap What happens std::move on a const object copies : a const T&& cannot bind to T&& , so the copy constructor is chosen (compiled: 1 copy, 0 moves) a type with no move operations silently copies using an object after std::move valid but unspecified; only destroy or assign to it std::move on a forwarding reference parameter moves an lvalue the caller still wanted: use std::forward moving inside a loop the second iteration sees a moved-from object push_back(lvalue) copies, push_back(temporary) moves, emplace_back(args...) builds in place (compiled together: 1 copy, 1 move). Move only the last use After auto b = std::move(a); treat a as empty. Never return const values by value: it disables the move. Read more Effective Modern C++ (Meyers) Item 29, Assume move operations are not present, not cheap, and not used (PDF pages 221 to 225) Item 41, Consider pass by value for copyable, cheap-to-move parameters that are always copied (PDF pages 299 to 310) C++ Move Semantics (Josuttis) 4.1 to 4.2 Avoid objects with names; avoid unnecessary std::move (PDF pages 77 to 79) 4.3.1 to 4.3.4 Initializing members with move semantics (PDF pages 79 to 90) 4.3.5 to 4.3.6 Member initialization summary; always pass by value and move? (PDF pages 90 to 93)"},{"f":"13-move-semantics.html","n":"Move Semantics","id":"cheat","h":"Cheat sheet","x":"Cheat sheet Answer aloud, then open. What does std::move actually do? Nothing at runtime: it is an unconditional cast to an rvalue reference. It enables the move constructor or move assignment to be chosen. The moved-from object stays valid but unspecified. On a const object it silently copies, because a const rvalue cannot bind to a move constructor. What are lvalues, prvalues and xvalues? An lvalue is a named object with an address. A prvalue is an unnamed temporary (a literal, a + b , a call returning by value). An xvalue is an object marked as movable, such as std::move(x) . Rvalue means prvalue or xvalue. A named rvalue-reference parameter is still an lvalue inside the function. What is copy elision / RVO? The compiler constructs a returned object directly in the caller's storage, skipping the copy or move. In C++17 it is guaranteed when returning a temporary (prvalue). NRVO for a named local is allowed but not required. Do not write return std::move(local) : it prevents it and costs a move. Why mark a move constructor noexcept ? std::vector moves its elements on reallocation only if the move cannot throw; otherwise it copies them to keep the strong guarantee. Without noexcept every growth becomes a round of copies. What state is a moved-from object in? Valid but unspecified: you may destroy it or assign a new value to it, but not rely on its contents. For standard types it is usually empty. How do you write a class that stores a string argument efficiently? Take the parameter by value and std::move it into the member. An lvalue costs one copy plus one move, an rvalue costs one move, and there is a single function to write. What is the difference between push_back and emplace_back ? push_back takes an existing object (copied or moved into the container). emplace_back forwards its arguments and constructs the element in place, saving a temporary. Both reallocate the same way."},{"f":"14-smart-pointers.html","n":"Smart Pointers and Ownership","id":"ownership","h":"Who owns it? The three smart pointers","x":"Who owns it? The three smart pointers RAII wrappers around new / delete ( <memory> ) that make ownership explicit in the type . The owner is whoever must free the object. Type Ownership Copyable? Cost std::unique_ptr<T> exclusive: one owner no , only movable same as a raw pointer std::shared_ptr<T> shared: reference counted, freed when the last owner goes yes (bumps the count) extra control block, atomic count std::weak_ptr<T> none: observes a shared_ptr without keeping it alive yes small raw pointer / reference none: \"I use it, I do not own it\" yes none Default A plain value member or local first. Then unique_ptr when it must live on the heap (polymorphism, large, optional). shared_ptr only for truly shared ownership. A function that... takes only uses the object const Widget& (or Widget* if it may be null): never the smart pointer takes over ownership std::unique_ptr<Widget> by value: the caller must std::move keeps a share std::shared_ptr<Widget> by value gives ownership to the caller returns std::unique_ptr<Widget> (a factory) std::unique_ptr makeWidget(std::string n); // factory: sole ownership to the caller void use(const Widget& w); // just uses: pass the object, not the owner void take(std::unique_ptr w); // takes ownership auto w = makeWidget(\"alpha\"); use(*w); take(std::move(w)); // w is now null Read more C++ Primer (Lippman, Lajoie, Moo) 12.1 Dynamic memory and smart pointers (PDF pages 479 to 504) Programming: Principles and Practice (Stroustrup) 18.4 to 18.5 Resources and exceptions; resource-management pointers (RAII, unique_ptr) (PDF pages 542 to 553) C++ Move Semantics (Josuttis) Chapter 13 Move-only types (PDF pages 213 to 218)"},{"f":"14-smart-pointers.html","n":"Smart Pointers and Ownership","id":"unique","h":"unique_ptr in detail","x":"unique_ptr in detail auto p = std::make_unique (1, \"a\"); // preferred over `new` // auto q = p; // ERROR: unique_ptr cannot be copied auto q = std::move(p); // OK: ownership moves, p is now null Zero overhead: with the default deleter sizeof(unique_ptr<T>) == sizeof(T*) (checked). A function-pointer deleter makes it twice as big; a stateless lambda deleter adds nothing. Custom deleter for anything that is not delete : std::unique_ptr<std::FILE, decltype(closer)> f(std::fopen(...), closer); closes the file on scope exit. Arrays: std::make_unique<int[]>(3) uses delete[] . Prefer std::vector unless you need a fixed block. Polymorphism: unique_ptr<Base> holding a Derived needs a virtual destructor on Base , as with any delete through a base pointer. .get() returns the raw pointer without giving up ownership; .release() gives it up (you must delete it); .reset() destroys the object now. Key point Why make_unique ? No naked new , the type is written once, and before C++17 f(unique_ptr<A>(new A), g()) could leak when g() threw between the two steps. Read more C++ Primer (Lippman, Lajoie, Moo) 12.1 Dynamic memory and smart pointers (PDF pages 479 to 504) Effective Modern C++ (Meyers) Item 18, Use std::unique_ptr for exclusive-ownership resource management (PDF pages 136 to 143) Item 21, Prefer make_unique and make_shared to new (PDF pages 157 to 165)"},{"f":"14-smart-pointers.html","n":"Smart Pointers and Ownership","id":"shared","h":"shared_ptr and the control block","x":"shared_ptr and the control block s1 shared s2 shared w weak control block strong count: 2 weak count: 1 Widget Object freed when strong count hits 0. weak_ptr.lock() gives a shared_ptr or null. unique_ptr has no control block: zero overhead, one owner. auto s1 = std::make_shared (2, \"b\"); // one allocation for object + control block auto s2 = s1; // two owners, use_count() == 2 A shared_ptr is two pointers : to the object and to a control block holding the strong count, the weak count and the deleter. make_shared allocates object and control block together (one allocation). shared_ptr<T>(new T) allocates twice. The deleter is not part of the shared_ptr type (unlike unique_ptr ): type-erased in the control block. The counts are atomic : copying or destroying shared_ptr s on several threads is safe. The object is not protected, and changing the same shared_ptr variable from two threads is a race. Copying costs an atomic increment. On hot paths pass const shared_ptr& or the object itself, not a copy per call. Read more C++ Primer (Lippman, Lajoie, Moo) 12.1 Dynamic memory and smart pointers (PDF pages 479 to 504) Effective Modern C++ (Meyers) Item 19, Use std::shared_ptr for shared-ownership resource management (PDF pages 143 to 152)"},{"f":"14-smart-pointers.html","n":"Smart Pointers and Ownership","id":"weak","h":"Reference cycles and weak_ptr","x":"Reference cycles and weak_ptr If A holds a shared_ptr<B> and B holds a shared_ptr<A> , each keeps the other alive: neither is ever destroyed . Compiled: after the scope, both objects were still alive (leaked). Make the back link (child to parent) a weak_ptr : both were destroyed. struct Node { std::shared_ptr parent; }; // cycle: leak struct WNode { std::weak_ptr parent; }; // weak back-pointer: freed std::weak_ptr w = s1; // observe only: does not change use_count if (auto sp = w.lock()) { /* still alive: use sp for as long as you hold it */ } // lock() gives a shared_ptr or null w.expired(); // true once every owner is gone lock() is the only way to use the object: it gives a shared_ptr that keeps it alive while you work, or null. Typical uses: parent links, caches, observer lists that must not keep subscribers alive. A weak_ptr keeps the control block alive (not the object) until the last weak reference goes. Read more C++ Primer (Lippman, Lajoie, Moo) 12.1 Dynamic memory and smart pointers (PDF pages 479 to 504) Effective Modern C++ (Meyers) Item 20, Use std::weak_ptr for shared_ptr-like pointers that can dangle (PDF pages 152 to 157)"},{"f":"14-smart-pointers.html","n":"Smart Pointers and Ownership","id":"pitfalls","h":"Pitfalls and enable_shared_from_this","x":"Pitfalls and enable_shared_from_this One control block per object auto raw = new Widget; std::shared_ptr a(raw); std::shared_ptr b(raw); // a second, independent control block: double delete! Never build a second shared_ptr from a raw pointer or from this . Copy an existing one. When a member function needs a shared_ptr to its own object (to register itself, to hand to a callback), inherit std::enable_shared_from_this and call shared_from_this() : struct Session : std::enable_shared_from_this { std::shared_ptr self() { return shared_from_this(); } // a safe second owner, SAME control block }; auto s = std::make_shared (); auto t = s->self(); // use_count() == 2 Session raw; raw.self(); // throws std::bad_weak_ptr: no shared_ptr owns it (compiled) Pitfall Fix shared_ptr cycle weak_ptr for the back link shared_ptr from this or a raw pointer enable_shared_from_this shared_ptr by default \"to be safe\" unique_ptr : you can always convert to shared later storing .get() somewhere that outlives the owner keep the smart pointer, or a weak_ptr delete on a .get() result never: the smart pointer will delete it again"},{"f":"14-smart-pointers.html","n":"Smart Pointers and Ownership","id":"cheat","h":"Cheat sheet","x":"Cheat sheet Answer aloud, then open. unique_ptr vs shared_ptr vs weak_ptr ? unique_ptr : sole owner, move-only, zero overhead. shared_ptr : reference-counted shared ownership with a control block and atomic count. weak_ptr : non-owning observer used to break cycles and to check whether a shared object is still alive ( lock() ). Is shared_ptr thread-safe? The reference count is atomic, so copying and destroying shared_ptr s from different threads is safe. The pointed-to object is not protected: concurrent access to it still needs synchronization. Concurrently modifying the same shared_ptr variable is also a race. Why prefer make_unique / make_shared ? No naked new , less typing (and exception safe before C++17), and make_shared allocates the object and its control block once instead of twice. How do you break a shared_ptr cycle? Make one direction (usually the child's link to its parent) a weak_ptr . It does not count toward ownership, so the objects can be destroyed; use lock() to get a temporary shared_ptr when needed. How do you pass a smart pointer to a function? If the function only uses the object, pass a reference or raw pointer, not the smart pointer. To transfer ownership, take a unique_ptr by value and have the caller std::move . To share, take a shared_ptr by value. Return unique_ptr from factories. Why can two shared_ptr s built from the same raw pointer crash? Each creates its own control block and count, so the object is deleted twice. Copy an existing shared_ptr , or inherit enable_shared_from_this when an object must hand out a shared pointer to itself. Does unique_ptr cost anything compared with a raw pointer? Not with the default deleter: it is the same size and the destructor inlines to a delete . A stateful deleter (a capturing lambda, a function pointer) makes it larger."},{"f":"09-compile-time.html","n":"Type Traits and Compile-time C++","id":"s1","h":"Type traits","x":"Type traits A type trait answers a question about a type at compile time ( ::value / _v ) or transforms one ( ::type / _t ). #include static_assert(std::is_integral_v ); // query: true static_assert(!std::is_integral_v ); static_assert(std::is_same_v >); // transform, then compare template T multiply(T a, T b) { static_assert(std::is_arithmetic_v , \"multiply needs a number type\"); return a * b; } // multiply(std::string(\"a\"), std::string(\"b\")); // compile error with YOUR message Explore, especially the references and const rows, where people guess wrong: Key point static_assert : compile-time, free. assert : run-time, gone when NDEBUG is defined. Read more Programming: Principles and Practice (Stroustrup) 18.1.3 Concepts (PDF pages 531 to 533) 19.4 and 21.1 Generalizing Vector again; standard algorithms (PDF pages 573 to 578 and 617 to 622)"},{"f":"09-compile-time.html","n":"Type Traits and Compile-time C++","id":"s2","h":"enable_if and SFINAE","x":"enable_if and SFINAE SFINAE : an invalid substitution drops that overload instead of erroring. std::enable_if uses it to switch overloads by a condition. template , bool> = true> void print(const T& v) { std::cout , bool> = true> void print(const T& v) { std::cout Noisy syntax, poor errors. Better tools: Tool Standard Looks like if constexpr C++17 Branch inside one function; the untaken branch is not even compiled for that type Concepts / requires C++20 template <std::integral T> void print(T v); template void print(const T& v) { if constexpr (std::is_integral_v ) std::cout // C++20: constrained template, readable errors T twice(T v) { return v * 2; } Plain if is not enough With if (is_integral_v<T>) both branches must compile for every T . if constexpr discards the false one. Read more Effective Modern C++ (Meyers) Items 26 to 27, Overloading on universal references and its alternatives (tag dispatch, enable_if) (PDF pages 195 to 215)"},{"f":"09-compile-time.html","n":"Type Traits and Compile-time C++","id":"concepts","h":"Concepts and requires (C++20)","x":"Concepts and requires (C++20) A concept is a named, compile-time condition on a type. It replaces enable_if with something you can read, and it turns a page of substitution errors into one clear message at the call. template concept Addable = requires(T a, T b) { { a + b } -> std::convertible_to ; }; // what must compile, and what it must give template // 1. constrained template parameter T sum2(T a, T b) { return a + b; } template requires std::integral // 2. a requires clause T twice(T v) { return v * 2; } void show(std::integral auto x) { std::cout && Addable && !Addable ); // concepts are compile-time booleans sum2(2, 3); // 5 sum2(std::string(\"a\"), std::string(\"b\")); // \"ab\" twice(21); // 42 (compiled) show(7); show(2.5); // integral 7 / floating 2.5 // twice(2.5); // error: no matching function, constraint 'std::integral ' not satisfied A requires-expression ( requires(T a) { ... } ) lists operations that must compile. { expr } -> Concept also checks the result type. The standard library ships many: std::integral , std::floating_point , std::same_as , std::convertible_to , std::invocable , std::regular , and the range concepts such as std::ranges::range . Overloads can differ only by concept; when two are satisfied, the more constrained one wins. A concept checks syntax and types , not behaviour: Addable does not promise that + is commutative. Document the meaning you expect. Tool Error when it fails Use for enable_if / SFINAE pages of \"no matching function\" notes pre-C++20 code static_assert on a trait your message, but inside the function body a single check with a custom message concept \"constraint not satisfied\" at the call site the interface of a template (C++20 and later)"},{"f":"09-compile-time.html","n":"Type Traits and Compile-time C++","id":"s3","h":"constexpr , consteval , constinit","x":"constexpr , consteval , constinit compile time run time constexpr variable always computed constexpr function if arguments are constants otherwise at run time consteval function MUST be compile time error constinit: initialised at compile time, still mutable (avoids static-init-order problems). constexpr does not force compile time; assign to a constexpr variable or use consteval. Write an ordinary function and ask for compile-time evaluation. Keyword Meaning constexpr variable Must be initialised with a constant expression; usable where constants are required (array sizes, template arguments). constexpr function Can run at compile time if its arguments are constants; can still be called at run time with ordinary values. consteval (C++20) An \"immediate\" function: every call must be evaluated at compile time. constinit (C++20) The variable is initialised at compile time (no static-initialisation-order problems), but remains mutable. constexpr int factorial(int n) { return n Read more Effective Modern C++ (Meyers) Item 15, Use constexpr whenever possible (PDF pages 115 to 121)"},{"f":"09-compile-time.html","n":"Type Traits and Compile-time C++","id":"s4","h":"Template metaprogramming (TMP)","x":"Template metaprogramming (TMP) Before constexpr loops, compile-time computation was recursive template instantiation . You will meet it in older code. template struct Fact { static constexpr int value = Fact ::value * N; // the compiler instantiates Fact , Fact , ... }; template <> struct Fact { // full specialisation ends the recursion static constexpr int value = 1; }; static_assert(Fact ::value == 120); Each Fact<N> is a separate class; the result is a baked-in constant. Limits: recursion depth cap (a few hundred), slow builds, long errors. Today: constexpr for values, traits + if constexpr /concepts for types; TMP only to compute types . Task Old style Modern style Compute a value recursive struct with ::value constexpr function Pick code by type enable_if overloads if constexpr or concepts Reject bad types obscure substitution errors static_assert / requires"},{"f":"09-compile-time.html","n":"Type Traits and Compile-time C++","id":"s5","h":"Cheat sheet","x":"Cheat sheet What does SFINAE stand for and why does it exist? Substitution Failure Is Not An Error: an invalid substitution while deducing a template removes that candidate from overload resolution instead of causing a hard error. It is what makes enable_if overload selection work. std::is_same_v<int, const int> ? False. They are different types. const and references are part of the type, which is why traits like std::decay_t and std::remove_cvref_t exist. constexpr function versus consteval function? constexpr may run at compile time or run time. consteval must run at compile time and is a compile error otherwise. Why is if constexpr better than if in a template? The discarded branch is not instantiated, so code that is invalid for some T does not break the build for that T . What is a concept, and why is it better than enable_if ? A concept is a named compile-time condition on template arguments ( std::integral<T> , or your own with requires ). It states the interface of a template in the signature and fails at the call site with a readable \"constraint not satisfied\" message, where enable_if gives pages of substitution errors. How do you avoid writing the factorial struct by hand today? Write constexpr int factorial(int n) and use it in a constexpr context. It is shorter, readable and can also run at run time."},{"f":"06-design-patterns.html","n":"Design Patterns in C++","id":"s1","h":"What patterns are and are not","x":"What patterns are and are not A pattern is a vocabulary : \"this is an Observer\" conveys structure and intent. Three families: Family Concerned with Examples Creational how objects are created Singleton, Factory, Builder Structural how objects are composed Adapter, Decorator, Proxy, PIMPL Behavioural how objects interact and share responsibility Strategy, Observer, State, Command, Visitor Not goals Each pattern adds indirection: use one only for a real problem, and know when not to use it . Modern C++ shortens many A lambda or std::function replaces Strategy and Command hierarchies; std::visit replaces Visitor; smart pointers are a Proxy; RAII is built in."},{"f":"06-design-patterns.html","n":"Design Patterns in C++","id":"s8","h":"SOLID principles","x":"SOLID principles SOLID are five design principles. Most patterns in this lesson exist to serve one of them, so read this table first and refer back to it. Principle In one sentence Pattern that serves it S Single Responsibility a class has one reason to change split parsing, logic and I/O into separate classes O Open/Closed open for extension, closed for modification: add behaviour by adding code, not editing old code Strategy, Factory registry, Decorator L Liskov Substitution a derived object must be usable wherever the base is expected without breaking correctness correct inheritance hierarchies I Interface Segregation prefer several small interfaces over one fat one narrow abstract classes D Dependency Inversion depend on abstractions, not concrete classes; inject dependencies Strategy, Observer, dependency injection (instead of Singleton) Notes: Module 4 , examples ."},{"f":"06-design-patterns.html","n":"Design Patterns in C++","id":"s2","h":"Singleton","x":"Singleton code in file A thread B Logger::instance() static local, built once the one Logger Thread-safe creation since C++11; its methods still need their own locking. Intent: exactly one instance with global access (logger, config). class Logger { public: static Logger& instance() { static Logger inst; // created the first time control passes here return inst; // ...and the same object is returned forever after } Logger(const Logger&) = delete; // forbid copies: there must be only one Logger& operator=(const Logger&) = delete; void log(const std::string& msg) { std::cout The Meyers Singleton : Thread safe creation: a function-local static is initialized exactly once since C++11 (\"magic statics\"): no mutex or double-checked locking. Calls on the object still need their own locking. Lazy: built on first use, avoiding static initialization order problems. Drawbacks (say them): global state in disguise, hidden dependencies, hard to test with a fake, unspecified destruction order at exit. Alternative: create one object in main and pass it by reference (dependency injection)."},{"f":"06-design-patterns.html","n":"Design Patterns in C++","id":"s3","h":"Factory","x":"Factory caller Kind::Circle makeShape() unique_ptr Shape Circle Square The caller never names Circle or Square. New type means editing the factory, unless it keeps a registry. Intent: create objects without the caller naming the concrete class; it gets something usable only through an interface. struct Shape { virtual double area() const = 0; virtual ~Shape() = default; }; struct Circle : Shape { double r; explicit Circle(double r) : r(r) {} double area() const override { return 3.14159 * r * r; } }; struct Square : Shape { double s; explicit Square(double s) : s(s) {} double area() const override { return s * s; } }; enum class Kind { Circle, Square }; std::unique_ptr makeShape(Kind k, double size) { // the factory function switch (k) { case Kind::Circle: return std::make_unique (size); case Kind::Square: return std::make_unique (size); } return nullptr; } auto s = makeShape(Kind::Circle, 2.0); // caller sees only Shape std::cout area(); Return unique_ptr : clear ownership, no leaks, convertible to shared_ptr . The base needs a virtual destructor . A new type means editing the factory. Avoid with a registry : map<string, function<unique_ptr<Shape>(double)>> (Open/Closed). Factory Method : subclasses decide what to create. Abstract Factory : families of related objects (a matching Button and Checkbox per platform)."},{"f":"06-design-patterns.html","n":"Design Patterns in C++","id":"s4","h":"Strategy","x":"Strategy Checkout holds a strategy PricingStrategy apply(price) Regular: price Member: price * 0.9 a lambda The client picks the algorithm; modern C++ often uses a lambda instead of a class. Intent: interchangeable algorithms chosen at runtime without changing the user (sort order, pricing, retry policy). // 1. Classic: an interface and several implementations struct PricingStrategy { virtual double apply(double price) const = 0; virtual ~PricingStrategy() = default; }; struct Regular : PricingStrategy { double apply(double p) const override { return p; } }; struct Member : PricingStrategy { double apply(double p) const override { return p * 0.90; } }; class Checkout { std::unique_ptr strategy_; public: explicit Checkout(std::unique_ptr s) : strategy_(std::move(s)) {} double total(double price) const { return strategy_->apply(price); } }; // 2. Modern: the strategy IS a callable. No class hierarchy needed. class Checkout2 { std::function pricing_; public: explicit Checkout2(std::function f) : pricing_(std::move(f)) {} double total(double price) const { return pricing_(price); } }; Checkout2 c([](double p) { return p * 0.70; }); // a lambda is a strategy // 3. Compile-time: the strategy is a template parameter (policy-based design): zero overhead template class Checkout3 { /* ... Pricing{}(price) ... */ }; Try it: Strategy vs State Similar shape, different intent. Strategy : the client picks the algorithm; strategies are independent. State : the object changes its own behaviour as its state changes, and states trigger the transitions."},{"f":"06-design-patterns.html","n":"Design Patterns in C++","id":"s5","h":"Observer","x":"Observer Subject set(value) update(v) Display Logger Alarm the subject does not know who they are Watch for dangling observers, and iterate over a copy while notifying. Intent: a subject notifies its observers on change without knowing who they are (events, signals, pub/sub). Try it: class Observer { public: virtual void update(double value) = 0; virtual ~Observer() = default; }; class Subject { std::vector observers_; public: void attach(Observer* o) { observers_.push_back(o); } void detach(Observer* o) { observers_.erase(std::remove(observers_.begin(), observers_.end(), o), observers_.end()); } void set(double v) { value_ = v; auto copy = observers_; // iterate a COPY: an observer may detach itself in update() for (auto* o : copy) o->update(v); } private: double value_ = 0; }; // Modern, lighter version: observers are just callbacks std::vector > listeners; listeners.push_back([](double t) { std::cout Classic Observer bugs Dangling observer: subject calls a destroyed object. Detach in the destructor (RAII) or store weak_ptr . Modifying the list while notifying: iterate a copy. Threads: lock, but never while calling observers (deadlock risk). Step through a notification: Read more Effective Modern C++ (Meyers) Item 20, Use std::weak_ptr for shared_ptr-like pointers that can dangle (PDF pages 152 to 157)"},{"f":"06-design-patterns.html","n":"Design Patterns in C++","id":"s6","h":"State","x":"State Intent: behaviour changes with internal state, replacing scattered if (state == ...) checks (a turnstile). Send events: // 1. Simplest: an enum and a switch. Fine for a few states. enum class State { Locked, Unlocked }; class Turnstile { State s_ = State::Locked; public: void coin() { s_ = State::Unlocked; } void push() { if (s_ == State::Unlocked) s_ = State::Locked; else alarm(); } }; // 2. Classic State pattern: one class per state struct TurnstileState { virtual std::unique_ptr coin() = 0; // each state returns the NEXT state virtual std::unique_ptr push() = 0; virtual ~TurnstileState() = default; }; // 3. Modern: std::variant of state types + std::visit; the compiler checks every state is handled. using StateV = std::variant ; Use it when valid operations depend on \"what mode am I in\": connections, parsers, protocol handlers. Explicit transitions make illegal states unrepresentable and testable."},{"f":"06-design-patterns.html","n":"Design Patterns in C++","id":"s7","h":"Other patterns worth recognising","x":"Other patterns worth recognising Pattern Idea Where you already see it in C++ RAII object owns a resource and releases it in its destructor vector , unique_ptr , lock_guard Adapter wrap an interface so it matches the one clients expect std::stack (adapts deque ), wrapping a C library Decorator wrap an object to add behaviour while keeping its interface buffered/compressed stream wrappers, logging wrappers Proxy a stand-in that controls access to another object shared_ptr , vector<bool>::reference Command turn a request into an object (queue it, undo it) task queues of std::function , thread pools Iterator traverse a collection without exposing its structure the entire STL Visitor add operations to a type hierarchy without changing it std::visit with std::variant Builder construct a complex object step by step fluent config objects: Req().url(u).timeout(5).build() PIMPL hide implementation behind a pointer to keep headers stable and compile times low library public headers CRTP a class derives from a template instantiated with itself: static polymorphism mix-ins, std::enable_shared_from_this // CRTP: compile-time polymorphism, no vtable template struct Shape { double area() const { return static_cast (this)->areaImpl(); } }; struct Circle : Shape { double r = 1; double areaImpl() const { return 3.14159 * r * r; } }; With real code Command, Decorator, Adapter, Builder, Visitor with std::variant and a session state machine are in More Patterns . Read more Effective Modern C++ (Meyers) Item 22, Pimpl: define special member functions in the implementation file (PDF pages 165 to 174)"},{"f":"06-design-patterns.html","n":"Design Patterns in C++","id":"s9","h":"Cheat sheet","x":"Cheat sheet How do you make a Singleton thread-safe in C++? Use a function-local static (Meyers Singleton). Since C++11 its initialization is guaranteed thread-safe and happens exactly once. Alternatives are std::call_once with an std::once_flag , or creating it before threads start. Hand-written double-checked locking is easy to get wrong. Remember the object's own methods still need locking if they mutate shared state. What is wrong with Singleton? It is global mutable state with a nice name: hidden dependencies, tight coupling, hard to test or mock, awkward lifetime and destruction order, and a problem for multithreading. Prefer passing a reference (dependency injection) unless a single shared instance is truly inherent. Strategy vs State? Both delegate behaviour to a swappable object. Strategy: the client selects an interchangeable algorithm and strategies are independent. State: the context changes its behaviour as its own state changes, and transitions are driven by the states or context. What problems can Observer cause? Dangling observers (destroyed but still registered), modification of the observer list during notification, unexpected notification order, cascading updates and performance issues, and thread-safety issues. Fixes: RAII detach, weak_ptr , iterate over a copy, and do not hold locks while calling out. Why return unique_ptr from a factory? It expresses that the caller takes sole ownership, prevents leaks, and the caller can convert it to a shared_ptr when sharing is needed. Returning a raw owning pointer is ambiguous and error-prone. How do you replace a virtual-function Strategy in modern C++? With a callable: store a std::function (runtime flexibility) or take a template parameter (zero overhead, compile-time choice). A lambda becomes the strategy, and no class hierarchy is needed. What is the Open/Closed principle? Software entities should be open to extension but closed to modification: you add new behaviour (a new strategy, a new shape type registered in a factory) without editing the existing, tested code."},{"f":"20-more-patterns.html","n":"More Patterns","id":"command","h":"Command (with undo)","x":"Command (with undo) Turn an action into an object . Because it is an object you can store it, queue it, log it, send it, replay it, and undo it. Editor (invoker) run(cmd) Append(\", world\") Append(\"Hello\") history (stack of executed commands) undo(): pop, cmd.undo(doc) Each command knows how to execute AND how to reverse itself. The history of command objects is the undo stack. struct Command { virtual ~Command() = default; virtual void execute(Document&) = 0; virtual void undo(Document&) = 0; }; struct Append : Command { std::string s; explicit Append(std::string x) : s(std::move(x)) {} void execute(Document& d) override { d.text += s; } void undo(Document& d) override { d.text.resize(d.text.size() - s.size()); } }; class Editor { Document doc_; std::vector > done_; public: void run(std::unique_ptr c) { c->execute(doc_); done_.push_back(std::move(c)); } void undo() { if (!done_.empty()) { done_.back()->undo(doc_); done_.pop_back(); } } }; Compiled: two appends gave \"Hello, world\" , one undo gave \"Hello\" . Uses: undo/redo, task queues and thread pools (a task is a command), macro recording, transaction logs, and order messages that can be stored and replayed. A lambda stored in a std::function is a lightweight command when you do not need undo. Store enough state to reverse the action (the old value), not just the new one."},{"f":"20-more-patterns.html","n":"More Patterns","id":"decorator","h":"Decorator","x":"Decorator Add behaviour to an object without changing its class by wrapping it in another object with the same interface . Wrappers stack. Bracket Upper Plain write(\"feed\") Plain returns \"feed\" Upper makes it \"FEED\" Bracket makes it \"[FEED]\" Each layer calls the one inside it and adds its own effect. struct Sink { virtual ~Sink() = default; virtual std::string write(const std::string&) = 0; }; struct Plain : Sink { std::string write(const std::string& s) override { return s; } }; struct Bracket : Sink { std::unique_ptr inner; // wraps ANY Sink explicit Bracket(std::unique_ptr i) : inner(std::move(i)) {} std::string write(const std::string& s) override { return \"[\" + inner->write(s) + \"]\"; } }; std::unique_ptr s = std::make_unique (std::make_unique (std::make_unique ())); s->write(\"feed\"); // \"[FEED]\" (compiled) vs inheritance: combinations multiply with subclasses ( UpperBracketCompressed ...), while decorators are chosen and stacked at run time. Seen in: stream wrappers (buffered, compressed, encrypted), logging and metrics wrappers, retry and caching layers, middleware. The cost is one virtual call and one allocation per layer: keep them out of hot paths."},{"f":"20-more-patterns.html","n":"More Patterns","id":"adapter","h":"Adapter","x":"Adapter Make an interface you cannot change fit the one your code expects . The adapter implements the expected interface and translates each call. struct LegacySensor { int tempInTenths() const; }; // a third-party class: cannot edit it struct TempSource { virtual ~TempSource() = default; virtual double celsius() const = 0; }; // what our code uses struct LegacyAdapter : TempSource { LegacySensor sensor; double celsius() const override { return sensor.tempInTenths() / 10.0; } // 215 tenths -> 21.5 (compiled) }; Class or object adapter: the sample holds the adaptee as a member (object adapter, preferred). Private inheritance gives a class adapter. A std::function or a lambda is often a quick adapter between two callable shapes. std::stack and std::queue are standard adapters over deque . Adapter vs Decorator: an adapter changes the interface; a decorator keeps it and adds behaviour. Facade hides a subsystem behind a simpler interface."},{"f":"20-more-patterns.html","n":"More Patterns","id":"builder","h":"Builder","x":"Builder Construct a complex object step by step with named steps, and validate at the end. Useful when a constructor would need many parameters. class RequestBuilder { Request r_; public: RequestBuilder& url(std::string u) { r_.url = std::move(u); return *this; } RequestBuilder& timeout(int secs) { r_.timeout = secs; return *this; } RequestBuilder& retries(int n) { r_.retries = n; return *this; } RequestBuilder& https() { r_.secure = true; return *this; } Request build() const { /* check url is set, timeout > 0, ... then */ return r_; } }; Request r = RequestBuilder().url(\"example.org/api\").timeout(5).retries(3).https().build(); Each setter returns *this so calls chain (a fluent interface ). Validate in build() : a request without a URL never gets out. Alternatives in modern C++: an aggregate with designated initializers ( Request{.url = \"example.org\", .timeout = 5} , C++20) is shorter when no validation is needed; named parameters via a small options struct. The classic use is building trees or immutable objects; a builder avoids half-built objects."},{"f":"20-more-patterns.html","n":"More Patterns","id":"visitor","h":"Visitor and std::variant","x":"Visitor and std::variant Add a new operation over a closed set of types without editing them, by dispatching on the type. Classic C++ does it with double dispatch ( accept(Visitor&) and one visit per type); modern C++ does it with std::variant and std::visit . struct Circle { double r; }; struct Rect { double w, h; }; using Shape = std::variant ; // a closed set of alternatives, no base class, no virtuals struct Area { // one operator() per alternative double operator()(const Circle& c) const { return 3.0 * c.r * c.r; } double operator()(const Rect& r) const { return r.w * r.h; } }; std::vector shapes{Circle{2}, Rect{3, 4}}; double total = 0; for (const auto& s : shapes) total += std::visit(Area{}, s); // 12 + 12 = 24 (compiled) Virtual functions variant + visit Add a new operation edit every class write one new visitor Add a new type write one new class edit the variant and every visitor (the compiler reports the ones that do not handle it) Storage pointers, heap, indirect calls values stored inline, no allocation, no virtual call Use virtual functions when the set of types is open and operations are few; use variant + visit when the set of types is closed (message types, AST nodes, events) and you keep adding operations. A forgotten alternative is a compile error , not a silent bug. Variants are a natural fit for protocol messages : std::variant<Connect, Data, Disconnect> and a visitor that handles each kind."},{"f":"20-more-patterns.html","n":"More Patterns","id":"state-machine","h":"A protocol state machine with variant","x":"A protocol state machine with variant A connection or session moves through states (disconnected, logged on, stopped) and reacts to events. Each state carries only the data it needs. Disconnected logon LoggedOn (nextSeq) logout Stopped msg: nextSeq + 1 Events that do not apply to the current state leave it unchanged. Each state carries only its own data. struct Disconnected {}; struct LoggedOn { int nextSeq = 1; }; struct Stopped { std::string reason; }; using Session = std::variant ; Session onEvent(const Session& s, const std::string& ev) { return std::visit([&](const auto& st) -> Session { using T = std::decay_t ; if constexpr (std::is_same_v ) { if (ev == \"logon\") return LoggedOn{}; } else if constexpr (std::is_same_v ) { if (ev == \"msg\") return LoggedOn{st.nextSeq + 1}; if (ev == \"logout\") return Stopped{\"logout\"}; } return st; // not applicable: stay in the same state }, s); } Compiled trace: msg while disconnected stayed Disconnected ; after logon the state was LoggedOn(next 1) , two msg events moved it to next 3 , logout gave Stopped , and a later msg stayed Stopped. Data such as the sequence number lives in the state that needs it , so it cannot be misused in another state. This is the State pattern without virtual functions ( Design Patterns shows the virtual version)."},{"f":"20-more-patterns.html","n":"More Patterns","id":"cheat","h":"Cheat sheet","x":"Cheat sheet Say each answer aloud, then open it. What is the Command pattern and how does it give you undo? It turns a request into an object with execute (and undo ). The invoker keeps executed commands on a stack; undo pops the last one and calls its undo . Because commands are objects they can also be queued, logged, sent and replayed (requests, thread-pool tasks, macros). Decorator versus inheritance? A decorator wraps an object with the same interface and adds behaviour, chosen and stacked at run time. Inheritance fixes the combination at compile time and multiplies classes for every combination. Streams with buffering or compression, and logging wrappers, are decorators. Adapter versus Decorator versus Facade? An adapter changes an interface to the one a client expects. A decorator keeps the interface and adds behaviour. A facade offers a simpler interface to a whole subsystem. std::stack and std::queue are adapters. When would you use a Builder? When an object has many optional or related parts, so a constructor would take a long argument list, or when it must be validated before use. Chain named setters and validate in build() . For simple aggregates, designated initializers (C++20) may be enough. Visitor with virtual functions or with std::variant ? Virtual functions make adding a type easy and adding an operation hard. A variant with std::visit makes adding an operation easy (one new visitor), keeps values inline without allocation or virtual calls, and the compiler flags every visitor that misses a new alternative. Use it when the set of types is closed, such as message types. How would you model a network session with several states? As a state machine: states such as Disconnected, LoggedOn and Stopped, each holding only its own data (for example the next expected sequence number), and an onEvent function that returns the next state. A variant of state structs with std::visit makes invalid combinations unrepresentable and avoids virtual calls."},{"f":"07-multithreading.html","n":"Multithreading","id":"s1","h":"Why threads? The mental model","x":"Why threads? The mental model One process (private memory, isolated from other processes) Thread A own stack + registers Thread B own stack + registers Heap + globals SHARED by every thread this is where races happen Threads share memory; that sharing is the source of both the speed and the bugs. A thread is an independent flow of execution. Several threads make progress at once, like several cooks in one kitchen. Term Meaning Concurrency tasks in progress at overlapping times (can be interleaved on one core) Parallelism tasks running at the same instant on different cores Why: speed (use all cores), responsiveness (keep a UI or network handler alive), hiding I/O waits. Cost: about 1 MB stack each, startup time, context switches. Use threads only where the gain is measurable. The whole difficulty Threads share memory, and the OS can switch between them between any two instructions . std::thread::hardware_concurrency(); // how many hardware threads (cores x hyperthreads) this machine has, e.g. 8 Read more C++ Concurrency in Action (Williams) 1.1 What is concurrency? (PDF pages 25 to 30) 1.2.3 When not to use concurrency (PDF pages 32 to 33)"},{"f":"07-multithreading.html","n":"Multithreading","id":"s2","h":"Your first thread","x":"Your first thread #include #include void hello(int id) { std::cout The order is not fixed . Run it repeatedly and you may see either: main continues worker 1 running worker 1 running or main continues A test that passed once proves almost nothing. Rules Always join() or detach() before the std::thread is destroyed , otherwise std::terminate . A detached thread must not touch anything that may be destroyed. Arguments are copied into the thread. Use std::ref to pass a reference. Threads are move-only : std::thread b = std::move(a); . An exception escaping the thread function calls std::terminate . Catch inside, or pass it out with a future . void addOne(int& n) { ++n; } int x = 0; std::thread t1(addOne, std::ref(x)); // std::ref: pass x by reference (without it this will not even compile) t1.join(); // x is now 1 std::thread t2([] { std::cout Classic detach bug void f() { int local = 0; std::thread t([&] { ++local; }); // captures local BY REFERENCE t.detach(); } // f returns, local is destroyed, the thread still writes to it Capture by value (or join() ) if the thread can outlive the variable. Converted too late void f(int i, const std::string& s); void oops() { char buffer[1024]; std::thread t(f, 3, buffer); // copies the char* POINTER, not the text t.detach(); // oops() may return before the thread builds the std::string } // fix: std::thread t(f, 3, std::string(buffer)); The conversion to std::string happens later, inside the new thread. Convert on the calling side. Join on every path If anything before join() can throw, the thread stays joinable and the program terminates. Use RAII: class ThreadGuard { std::thread& t_; public: explicit ThreadGuard(std::thread& t) : t_(t) {} ~ThreadGuard() { if (t_.joinable()) t_.join(); } // joins however the scope is left ThreadGuard(const ThreadGuard&) = delete; ThreadGuard& operator=(const ThreadGuard&) = delete; }; void f() { std::thread t(work); ThreadGuard g(t); // joins when f exits, even by an exception mayThrow(); } Why no implicit join or detach? A join hides long waits; a detach leaves a thread using dead locals. If a class owns a std::thread , declare it last among the members. C++20: std::jthread Joins automatically in its destructor and supports std::stop_token cancellation. Read more Effective Modern C++ (Meyers) Item 37, Make std::threads unjoinable on all paths (PDF pages 268 to 276) C++ Concurrency in Action (Williams) 2.1 Basic thread management (launching, joining, detaching, waiting in exceptional circumstances, running in the background) (PDF pages 40 to 47) 2.2 Passing arguments to a thread function (PDF pages 47 to 50) 2.3 Transferring ownership of a thread; 2.5 Identifying threads (PDF pages 50 to 53 and 57 to 59)"},{"f":"07-multithreading.html","n":"Multithreading","id":"s3","h":"Shared data and data races","x":"Shared data and data races time Thread 1 read: 0 0 + 1 write 1 Thread 2 read: 0 0 + 1 write 1 counter 0 1 still 1 Expected 2, got 1: both threads read 0 before either wrote. One update is lost. A data race: the two read-modify-write sequences overlap. ++counter is three steps, not one. Two threads each increment a shared counter: int counter = 0; void work() { for (int i = 0; i ++counter is three CPU steps: load the value from memory into a register: r = counter add one to the register: r = r + 1 store the register back to memory: counter = r If B loads between A's load and store, one increment is lost . Play the scheduler with Step A and Step B : Hint: A loads, B runs fully, A finishes. Or press Run 500 random schedules . Caveat The simulator switches far more often than a real OS. A real race may fail once in thousands of runs, which is why it survives testing. Data race = undefined behavior Two threads access the same memory, at least one writes, no synchronization. A race condition is the wider timing-dependent logic bug. Why it hurts: shared data has an invariant (links agree, count matches items). An update breaks it briefly, and a thread looking then sees corruption. Trouble starts when two or more pieces of data change together. Three ways to be safe Strategy How 1. Do not share own data per thread (locals, thread_local ), merge afterwards. Best option. 2. Share, never modify const data is safe to read from many threads. 3. Share and protect a mutex or an atomic . Read more C++ Concurrency in Action (Williams) 3.1 Problems with sharing data between threads (invariants, race conditions) (PDF pages 60 to 63)"},{"f":"07-multithreading.html","n":"Multithreading","id":"s4","h":"Mutexes: taking turns","x":"Mutexes: taking turns time Thread 1 lock, critical section, unlock Thread 2 blocked, waiting lock, critical section, unlock Only one thread is inside the protected region at any moment, so the updates cannot overlap. Cost: thread 2 does nothing while it waits. Keep critical sections short. A mutex lets one thread at a time into the critical section ; others wait at lock() . Same two threads, now locked: Run 500 random schedules: always 4 . std::mutex m; int counter = 0; void work() { for (int i = 0; i lock(m); // locks now, unlocks automatically at the end of the block ++counter; } } Use RAII locks, never raw lock()/unlock() An exception or early return between lock() and unlock() leaves the mutex locked forever. Type Use it when std::lock_guard<std::mutex> default: lock for the whole scope std::unique_lock<std::mutex> unlock early, relock, defer; required by cv.wait std::scoped_lock (C++17) several mutexes at once, deadlock-free std::shared_mutex + shared_lock many readers or one writer Protecting a class class SafeCounter { mutable std::mutex m_; // mutable: so const getters can still lock it int value_ = 0; public: void increment() { std::lock_guard lk(m_); ++value_; } int get() const { std::lock_guard lk(m_); return value_; } }; Good locking Keep critical sections short ; do slow work (I/O, computation) outside. Mutex next to its data ; never hand out references to that data. Never call unknown code (callbacks, virtuals) while locked. Too much locking means contention : the program becomes serial. Read more Effective Modern C++ (Meyers) Item 16, Make const member functions thread safe (PDF pages 121 to 127) C++ Concurrency in Action (Williams) 3.2.1 to 3.2.3 Mutexes in C++, structuring code to protect data, race conditions inherent in interfaces (PDF pages 64 to 74) 3.2.6 to 3.2.8 std::unique_lock, transferring mutex ownership, locking granularity (PDF pages 82 to 87)"},{"f":"07-multithreading.html","n":"Multithreading","id":"mutex-types","h":"Other mutex types and call_once","x":"Other mutex types and call_once Type What it adds Use when std::mutex nothing: one owner, not re-lockable the default std::recursive_mutex the same thread may lock it again; it must unlock as many times almost never: see below std::timed_mutex try_lock_for(d) and try_lock_until(t) : give up after a time a thread must not wait forever (watchdogs, real-time deadlines) std::shared_mutex (C++17) shared mode for readers, exclusive mode for writers reads dominate and the critical section is not tiny recursive_mutex is a design smell class Account { std::recursive_mutex m_; int balance_ = 0; public: void deposit(int n) { std::lock_guard lk(m_); balance_ += n; } void depositTwice(int n) { std::lock_guard lk(m_); deposit(n); deposit(n); } // locks the SAME mutex again }; It works (compiled: balance 10), but it hides a design problem: depositTwice does not know what state deposit assumes. Prefer a public method that locks once and a private depositLocked() that assumes the lock is held. A plain std::mutex locked twice by one thread is undefined behaviour (usually a deadlock). Reader-writer lock class PriceTable { mutable std::shared_mutex m_; std::map px_; public: void set(const std::string& k, double v) { std::unique_lock lk(m_); px_[k] = v; } // exclusive: one writer, no readers double get(const std::string& k) const { std::shared_lock lk(m_); // shared: any number of readers together auto it = px_.find(k); return it == px_.end() ? -1 : it->second; } }; A shared_mutex costs more than a mutex per lock. It pays off only when there are many readers and each read holds the lock for a while. Writers can starve if readers never leave (implementation-defined fairness). try_lock() returns immediately with true or false: compiled, a second thread's try_lock on a held mutex returned false; try_lock_for(20 ms) on one held for 200 ms returned false after 20 ms. std::call_once std::once_flag flag; void init() { std::call_once(flag, [] { loadConfig(); }); } // eight threads may call init(): loadConfig runs exactly once Use it (or a function-local static ) for lazy initialization shared by several threads, instead of a hand-written double-checked lock. Key point \"When would you use a recursive mutex?\" Rarely, and say so: it usually means a method that locks calls another that also locks. Restructure into a locking public method and a lock-assuming private one. Read more C++ Concurrency in Action (Williams) 3.3.1 Protecting shared data during initialization (std::call_once) (PDF pages 88 to 91) 3.3.2 to 3.3.3 Protecting rarely updated data (reader-writer lock); recursive locking (PDF pages 91 to 95)"},{"f":"07-multithreading.html","n":"Multithreading","id":"interface-races","h":"Races hidden in interfaces","x":"Races hidden in interfaces A mutex in every member function is necessary but not sufficient . Two threads run this on a shared stack whose methods all lock internally: SafeStack s; // shared by several threads; push, pop, top, empty all lock internally if (!s.empty()) { // 1. is there anything to take? int v = s.top(); // 2. read the top element s.pop(); // 3. remove it use(v); } Each call is atomic, the sequence is not . Play A and B on a one-element stack: Two failures, neither preventable by internal locking: B calls top() on an empty stack: undefined behavior (the empty() answer went stale). Both read top() before either pops: one element processed twice . This check-then-act race also hits lock-free structures. The cause is the interface, so change the interface. Fix: offer whole operations template class SafeStack { std::stack s_; mutable std::mutex m_; public: void push(T v) { std::lock_guard lk(m_); s_.push(std::move(v)); } std::optional try_pop() { // check + read + remove as ONE atomic operation std::lock_guard lk(m_); if (s_.empty()) return std::nullopt; T v = std::move(s_.top()); s_.pop(); return v; } // empty() may still exist, but treat its answer as a hint only: it can be stale the moment it returns. }; if (auto v = s.try_pop()) use(*v); // no window between the check and the removal Why does std::stack split top() and pop()? If pop() returned by value and the copy threw after removal, the element would be lost. A concurrent version must merge them: out-parameter, std::optional , shared_ptr<T> , or a non-throwing move. Same pattern elsewhere: Racy sequence Whole-operation fix if (!m.contains(k)) m[k] = v; one insert / try_emplace call that does both under the lock if (q.size() < max) q.push(x); a try_push that checks and pushes under one lock if (!file_exists(p)) create(p); an atomic \"create if absent\" call (for example the OS O_EXCL flag) if (counter.load() > 0) counter--; a compare_exchange loop Key point \"Mutex in every method, so thread-safe?\" No: each call is safe, the caller's sequence is not. Provide combined operations like try_pop and protect the whole invariant . Read more C++ Concurrency in Action (Williams) 3.2.1 to 3.2.3 Mutexes in C++, structuring code to protect data, race conditions inherent in interfaces (PDF pages 64 to 74) 6.1 to 6.2.2 Designing for concurrency, a thread-safe stack and a queue with condition variables (PDF pages 197 to 206)"},{"f":"07-multithreading.html","n":"Multithreading","id":"s5","h":"Deadlock","x":"Deadlock A deadlock : threads wait for each other forever. A holds a wants b ; B holds b wants a . Step A once, B once, then try to go on: \"Run 500 random schedules\": many end in deadlock. In real programs it may occur once in a million runs, only in production. It needs all four (Coffman conditions); remove one and it cannot happen: Mutual exclusion : one holder at a time Hold and wait : holds one lock, waits for another No preemption : a lock cannot be taken away Circular wait : A waits for B, B for A Fix: one global lock order If every thread takes a before b , no cycle is possible: std::mutex a, b; void thread1() { std::scoped_lock lk(a, b); /* ... */ } // C++17: locks both with a deadlock-avoidance algorithm void thread2() { std::scoped_lock lk(b, a); /* ... */ } // OK even though the order differs: scoped_lock sorts it out // pre-C++17: std::lock(a, b); // lock both atomically (deadlock-free) std::lock_guard la(a, std::adopt_lock); // adopt the already-held locks so RAII still unlocks std::lock_guard lb(b, std::adopt_lock); Four guidelines, one idea Do not wait for a thread that might be waiting for you. Avoid nested locks. If you need several, take them in one step with std::scoped_lock . No user-supplied code under a lock. A callback may take another lock, or keep a pointer to your data: class Wrapper { Data data_; std::mutex m_; public: template void process(F f) { std::lock_guard lk(m_); f(data_); // hands a reference to the protected data to unknown code } }; Data* leaked; wrapper.process([](Data& d) { leaked = }); // the callback keeps the pointer leaked->modify(); // later: modifies the data with NO lock held Never pass out a pointer or reference to protected data. Fixed lock order. Hand-over-hand locking on list nodes deadlocks when two threads walk in opposite directions. Fix the traversal direction. Lock hierarchy. Each mutex has a level; a thread may only lock a lower level than it holds. Violations throw the first time, even if the unlucky timing never occurs. It is a small wrapper around std::mutex that records the current level in a thread_local variable and throws if you lock upward. The idea is worth knowing; in practice, std::scoped_lock and a documented lock order cover most programs. No mutex needed Two threads that join() each other deadlock, as does any cycle of futures or condition-variable waits. std::thread tA, tB; // both threads are started elsewhere, running funcA and funcB void funcA() { tB.join(); } // A waits for B to finish... void funcB() { tA.join(); } // ...and B waits for A to finish: neither ever returns Key point \"Prevent deadlock?\" Global lock order; std::scoped_lock ; no foreign code under a lock; short locks; no nesting; try_lock with back-off. Re-locking a non-recursive mutex you own is undefined behavior. Read more C++ Concurrency in Action (Williams) 3.2.4 to 3.2.5 Deadlock: the problem, the solution and further guidelines (PDF pages 74 to 82)"},{"f":"07-multithreading.html","n":"Multithreading","id":"s7","h":"Condition variables","x":"Condition variables Producer lock, push, notify shared queue + mutex Consumer wait(lock, pred) push pop notify_one() wakes the sleeping consumer The consumer sleeps (no CPU) until notified, then re-checks the predicate to survive spurious wakeups. Condition variable: sleep until something changed, always paired with a mutex and a condition. To wait until something happens , a busy loop burns 100% CPU. A condition variable lets the thread sleep until woken, always together with a mutex and the shared state. std::mutex m; std::condition_variable cv; std::queue q; // CONSUMER std::unique_lock lk(m); cv.wait(lk, [] { return !q.empty(); }); // sleep until the predicate is true int x = q.front(); q.pop(); // PRODUCER { std::lock_guard lk(m); q.push(42); } cv.notify_one(); // wake one waiting thread Watch the hand-over: Three rules to follow Always wait with a predicate. Spurious wakeups happen, and another consumer may take the item first. Change the state under the mutex , then notify. Otherwise a lost wakeup: the waiter sees false, you notify, it then sleeps forever. The waiter needs unique_lock : wait unlocks while sleeping. notify_one() wakes one waiter; notify_all() wakes all (shutdown, or several may proceed). A reusable thread-safe queue template class SafeQueue { std::queue q_; std::mutex m_; std::condition_variable cv_; public: void push(T v) { { std::lock_guard lk(m_); q_.push(std::move(v)); } cv_.notify_one(); // notify AFTER releasing the lock: the woken thread can take it at once } T pop() { // blocks until an item exists std::unique_lock lk(m_); cv_.wait(lk, [this] { return !q_.empty(); }); T v = std::move(q_.front()); q_.pop(); return v; } }; Read more Effective Modern C++ (Meyers) Item 39, Consider void futures for one-shot event communication (PDF pages 280 to 289) C++ Concurrency in Action (Williams) 4.1 Waiting for an event: condition variables and a thread-safe queue (PDF pages 96 to 104) 6.1 to 6.2.2 Designing for concurrency, a thread-safe stack and a queue with condition variables (PDF pages 197 to 206)"},{"f":"07-multithreading.html","n":"Multithreading","id":"s11","h":"Debugging and common bugs","x":"Debugging and common bugs Timing bugs vanish under a debugger or a printf (\"Heisenbug\"). Use tools: ThreadSanitizer : -fsanitize=thread -g finds data races and prints both accesses. Run tests with it. Helgrind / DRD (Valgrind): races and lock-order violations. Thread ids in logs; gdb thread apply all bt on a hang. Symptom Likely cause Wrong totals, rare crashes data race Hang, CPU idle deadlock or lost wakeup Hang, CPU 100% busy-wait or livelock Crash at exit detached thread using destroyed data; joinable thread destroyed Slower with more threads lock contention, false sharing, oversubscription Garbled output several threads on std::cout : build the line, print once, or lock Vocabulary Thread-safe : usable from several threads without external locking ( shared_ptr 's reference count, not its pointee). thread_local : one copy per thread. Function-local static init is thread-safe since C++11 (Meyers Singleton). std::call_once(flag, fn) : initialize exactly once. Where next Atomics and the memory model are in Atomics and the Memory Model , futures, thread pools and C++20 coordination tools in Tasks and Parallelism , and false sharing, contention and pinning in Performance & Memory . Further reading: your notes in Module 3: multithreading and synchronization (with runnable examples ), the modern-cpp-tutorial concurrency chapter (threads, mutexes, futures, condition variables, atomics and the memory model), and the modern-cpp-features notes on std::thread , std::async , std::scoped_lock and parallel algorithms. Read more C++ Concurrency in Action (Williams) 11.1 Types of concurrency-related bugs, and 11.2.1 reviewing code to locate them (PDF pages 363 to 367)"},{"f":"07-multithreading.html","n":"Multithreading","id":"s12","h":"Cheat sheet","x":"Cheat sheet Say each answer aloud, then open it. What is the difference between a process and a thread? A process has its own address space; threads of one process share memory but each has its own stack and registers. Thread creation and switching are cheaper, communication is easy (shared memory) but needs synchronization. What is a data race? Is it the same as a race condition? A data race is unsynchronised concurrent access to the same memory with at least one write: undefined behavior in C++. A race condition is the wider class of bugs where correctness depends on timing, which can exist even without a data race (two correctly locked steps that needed to be one atomic operation). What happens if a std::thread is destroyed while still joinable, and why? std::terminate is called. The alternatives are worse: an implicit join can hide long waits, and an implicit detach leaves a running thread using locals that may be gone (undefined behavior). So make the thread unjoinable on every path: join in an RAII guard, or use std::jthread (C++20), which joins automatically. lock_guard vs unique_lock vs scoped_lock ? lock_guard : simplest RAII lock for a scope. unique_lock : flexible (unlock/relock, deferred, movable) and needed by condition variables. scoped_lock (C++17): locks multiple mutexes at once with deadlock avoidance. What is a deadlock and how do you prevent it? Threads wait for each other's locks forever. Needs mutual exclusion, hold-and-wait, no preemption and circular wait. Prevent with a consistent global lock order, std::scoped_lock / std::lock for multiple mutexes, short critical sections, no calls to foreign code under a lock, or try_lock . Why does condition_variable::wait need a predicate / a loop? Spurious wakeups (a thread may wake without a notification) and because the state may change again between the notification and the thread reacquiring the lock. The predicate re-checks the real condition. Why must the shared condition be modified under the mutex? Otherwise the waiter could check the condition (false), then the notifier changes it and notifies, then the waiter goes to sleep: a lost wakeup. The mutex makes check-and-sleep atomic with respect to the update. Is a function-local static initialised thread-safely? Yes, since C++11 initialization happens exactly once even with concurrent callers (\"magic statics\"). That is why the Meyers Singleton is thread-safe. Using the object afterwards still needs its own synchronization. My class locks a mutex in every method. Is it thread-safe? Not necessarily. Each call is safe on its own, but a caller's sequence of calls is not atomic. The classic case is a shared stack: if (!s.empty()) { v = s.top(); s.pop(); } races, because another thread can pop between the check and the use. The cause is the interface (it is also true of a lock-free stack), so the fix is to provide whole operations such as std::optional<T> try_pop() that check and remove under one lock. Can threads deadlock without any mutex? What are the practical rules against deadlock? Yes: two threads that join() each other, or any cycle of waits on futures or condition variables. The rules all mean \"do not wait for a thread that might be waiting for you\": avoid nested locks (or take several with scoped_lock ), never call user-supplied code or leak references to protected data while holding a lock, lock in a fixed order, and use a lock hierarchy so a wrong order is caught immediately at runtime. How would you find a data race in a large program? Build and run the tests with ThreadSanitizer ( -fsanitize=thread ), and Helgrind for lock-order problems; review shared state and make sure every access has a lock or is atomic; reduce sharing by design."},{"f":"17-tasks-parallelism.html","n":"Tasks and Parallelism","id":"futures","h":"Futures and async","x":"Futures and async To run a function in the background and get its result later , use a std::future<T> . #include int slowSquare(int n) { std::this_thread::sleep_for(std::chrono::seconds(1)); return n * n; } std::future f = std::async(std::launch::async, slowSquare, 7); // starts on another thread doOtherWork(); // main keeps working meanwhile int result = f.get(); // wait if needed, then get 49 f.get() blocks until ready (once only); wait() and wait_for() do not take the value. Exceptions travel : a throwing task rethrows from f.get() . Prefer tasks to raw threads : std::thread has no result channel and terminates on exceptions. Pass std::launch::async ; the default may defer the call to get() . An async future blocks in its destructor , so a discarded one makes the call effectively synchronous. // std::promise / std::future: a one-shot channel between two threads you manage yourself std::promise p; std::future f = p.get_future(); std::thread t([&p] { p.set_value(42); }); // the worker fulfils the promise (or p.set_exception(...)) std::cout std::packaged_task routes a function's result into a future (how pools return results). Step through a promise and its future: Read more Effective Modern C++ (Meyers) Item 35, Prefer task-based programming to thread-based (PDF pages 259 to 263) Item 36, Specify std::launch::async if asynchronicity is essential (PDF pages 263 to 268) Item 38, Be aware of varying thread handle destructor behavior (PDF pages 276 to 280) C++ Move Semantics (Josuttis) Chapter 15 Move semantics in standard library types (PDF pages 229 to 250) C++ Concurrency in Action (Williams) 4.2 Waiting for one-off events with futures (std::async, packaged_task, promise, shared_future) (PDF pages 104 to 115) 4.3 Waiting with a time limit (PDF pages 116 to 121)"},{"f":"17-tasks-parallelism.html","n":"Tasks and Parallelism","id":"signals","h":"promise , shared_future and packaged_task","x":"promise , shared_future and packaged_task Type Role std::promise<T> the writing end: set_value or set_exception , once std::future<T> the reading end: get() once; move-only std::shared_future<T> a copyable read end: several threads can get() or wait() on the same result std::packaged_task<R(Args)> wraps a callable and routes its result (or exception) into a future: how a pool returns results // promise : a one-shot \"go\" signal for several waiting threads std::promise go; std::shared_future start = go.get_future().share(); // copyable, so every worker gets a handle for (int i = 0; i task([](int n) { return n * n; }); std::future f = task.get_future(); std::thread t(std::move(task), 7); // runs on any thread int r = f.get(); // 49 t.join(); Compiled: four workers waiting on a shared promise<void> had started 0 before set_value() and 4 after. A promise destroyed without a value gives the future a broken_promise exception instead of hanging forever. For a repeated signal use a condition variable; a promise is one-shot . A future is a one-way, one-time channel. If you need many messages, use a queue. Read more C++ Concurrency in Action (Williams) 4.2 Waiting for one-off events with futures (std::async, packaged_task, promise, shared_future) (PDF pages 104 to 115)"},{"f":"17-tasks-parallelism.html","n":"Tasks and Parallelism","id":"pool","h":"Thread pools and producer/consumer","x":"Thread pools and producer/consumer Tasks task task task Task queue mutex + cv Worker 1 Worker 2 Worker N fixed number, created once Reusing N threads avoids the cost of one thread per task and keeps the thread count bounded. Thread pool: many tasks, few long-lived workers. A thread per task is expensive and thousands overload the scheduler. A thread pool starts N workers once; tasks go in a queue ( producer/consumer ). class ThreadPool { std::vector workers_; std::queue > tasks_; std::mutex m_; std::condition_variable cv_; bool stop_ = false; public: explicit ThreadPool(std::size_t n) { for (std::size_t i = 0; i task; { std::unique_lock lk(m_); cv_.wait(lk, [this] { return stop_ || !tasks_.empty(); }); if (stop_ && tasks_.empty()) return; // finish remaining tasks, then exit task = std::move(tasks_.front()); tasks_.pop(); } // lock released BEFORE running the task task(); } }); } void submit(std::function f) { { std::lock_guard lk(m_); tasks_.push(std::move(f)); } cv_.notify_one(); } ~ThreadPool() { { std::lock_guard lk(m_); stop_ = true; } // request shutdown under the lock cv_.notify_all(); // wake every sleeping worker for (auto& w : workers_) w.join(); // wait for them to finish } }; ThreadPool pool(std::thread::hardware_concurrency()); pool.submit([] { std::cout It combines everything: mutex, condition variable, stop flag, RAII shutdown. The task runs outside the lock, or only one could run at a time. Step through the pool: Read more C++ Concurrency in Action (Williams) 9.1 Thread pools (a simple pool, waiting for tasks, work stealing in 9.1.5) (PDF pages 324 to 337)"},{"f":"17-tasks-parallelism.html","n":"Tasks and Parallelism","id":"stealing","h":"Work stealing and sizing a pool","x":"Work stealing and sizing a pool One queue per worker (a deque) Worker 1 task task task task front: the owner pops here back: thieves take here Worker 2 empty: idle steals one task from the back Owners and thieves work at opposite ends, so they rarely contend. A shared queue has one lock everyone fights over. Work stealing: local deques keep the common case lock-light, stealing balances the load. Work stealing is how production pools (TBB, Java's fork/join) balance load: each worker pops its own deque and an idle worker steals from another's back. It suits recursive tasks that spawn subtasks: a spawned task goes on the spawner's own deque and stays hot in its cache. A simple pool with one shared queue (the previous section) is fine until that queue's lock becomes the bottleneck. Know that stealing is the next step; do not claim to have written one. Work Workers CPU-bound about hardware_concurrency() ; more is oversubscription and context switches I/O-bound or blocking more than the core count, because threads spend their time waiting Mixed separate pools, so slow blocking tasks cannot starve the fast ones Pool deadlock A task that submits a subtask to the same pool and waits for it can block every worker: all N workers wait for subtasks nobody is free to run. Do not block a pool thread on work that needs the same pool. Read more C++ Concurrency in Action (Williams) 2.4 Choosing the number of threads at runtime (PDF pages 54 to 56) 8.1 Techniques for dividing work between threads (PDF pages 275 to 282) 9.1 Thread pools (a simple pool, waiting for tasks, work stealing in 9.1.5) (PDF pages 324 to 337)"},{"f":"17-tasks-parallelism.html","n":"Tasks and Parallelism","id":"coordination","h":"latch , barrier , semaphore and stop_token (C++20)","x":"latch , barrier , semaphore and stop_token (C++20) Tool Idea Reusable? std::latch(n) wait until a counter has been counted down n times (\"N things are done\") no: one shot std::barrier(n) all n threads wait for each other at the end of each phase, then all go on yes : phase after phase std::counting_semaphore<N> at most k threads in a section (a permit counter) yes std::binary_semaphore a semaphore with one permit: signal from one thread to another yes Phase 1 Phase 2 thread A waits thread B (slowest) thread C waits The barrier (vertical bar): the last arrival releases everyone, so phase 2 starts together. Compiled: every thread saw all three phase-1 results before it began phase 2. A barrier separates phases of a parallel computation. std::latch done(3); // three workers will count down for (int i = 0; i slots(2); // at most two threads at once void limited() { slots.acquire(); use(); slots.release(); } // compiled: peak was 2 with six threads Cooperative cancellation: jthread and stop_token std::jthread worker([](std::stop_token st) { while (!st.stop_requested()) { doWork(); } // check the token regularly }); // worker.request_stop(); or just let the destructor do it: request_stop() then join() There is no safe way to kill a thread; you ask it to stop and it finishes its loop. std::stop_token is the standard channel for that request, and std::jthread joins in its destructor, which removes the \"forgot to join\" crash. Read more C++ Concurrency in Action (Williams) 4.4.7 to 4.4.10 Latches and barriers (the Concurrency TS versions of C++20 std::latch and std::barrier) (PDF pages 141 to 147) 9.2 Interrupting threads (the idea behind std::stop_token) (PDF pages 338 to 349)"},{"f":"17-tasks-parallelism.html","n":"Tasks and Parallelism","id":"parallel","h":"Parallel algorithms (C++17)","x":"Parallel algorithms (C++17) Most standard algorithms accept an execution policy as a first argument. The library decides how to split the work across threads. #include std::sort(std::execution::par, v.begin(), v.end()); long long sum = std::reduce(std::execution::par, v.begin(), v.end(), 0LL); // compiled: 500500 for 1..1000 std::transform(std::execution::par_unseq, a.begin(), a.end(), out.begin(), f); Policy Meaning seq sequential, as without a policy par may run on several threads par_unseq several threads and vectorised (SIMD): the function must not lock or allocate Your function object must be thread-safe : no unsynchronised writes to shared data. A data race inside a parallel algorithm is still undefined behaviour. std::reduce (not accumulate ) is the parallel fold: it may reassociate the operation, so the operation must be associative (floating-point sums may differ slightly). It only pays for big inputs and heavy work ; for small ranges the thread overhead dominates. With g++ you usually need Intel TBB installed and -ltbb for real parallelism; without a parallel backend the same code runs serially (as in the compiled example here). Exceptions escaping the callback call std::terminate . Choose Parallel algorithms for data parallelism over a container; a pool or std::async for independent tasks; a queue and a pinned thread for a latency-critical pipeline ( Performance & Memory ). Read more C++ Concurrency in Action (Williams) 10.1 to 10.3 Parallel algorithms: execution policies and the parallel standard algorithms (PDF pages 350 to 362)"},{"f":"17-tasks-parallelism.html","n":"Tasks and Parallelism","id":"cheat","h":"Cheat sheet","x":"Cheat sheet Say each answer aloud, then open it. std::async vs std::thread ? async returns a future carrying the result and any exception, and may use a pool or defer execution (use launch::async to force a thread). thread gives raw control with no result channel. A future from async blocks in its destructor. What are promise , future and packaged_task ? A promise is the write end of a one-shot channel, a future the read end. A packaged_task wraps a callable and fulfils a future with its result or exception, which is how a pool returns results. A shared_future is a copyable read end for several waiters. How would you signal several threads to start at the same moment? A promise<void> whose shared_future every thread waits on, or a std::latch / barrier . The promise is one-shot; for repeated signals use a condition variable. Why is the task run outside the lock in a thread-pool worker? Otherwise only one task could run at a time, and the queue would stay locked while a task works. The worker takes the task under the lock, releases it, then runs the task. How many threads should a pool have? About hardware_concurrency() for CPU-bound work (more means oversubscription and context switches), more for I/O-bound or blocking work. Use separate pools for mixed workloads. What is work stealing? Each worker has its own deque of tasks and pops from it; an idle worker steals from the back of another's deque. Owners and thieves work at opposite ends, so contention is low and load balances automatically. It removes the single shared queue's lock as a bottleneck. What is the difference between latch and barrier ? A latch is a one-shot countdown: threads count down and others wait until it reaches zero. A barrier is reusable: a fixed group of threads waits for each other at the end of each phase, then all continue together. What does a semaphore do that a mutex does not? A counting semaphore allows up to k threads in at once (a permit counter) and can be released by a different thread than the one that acquired it. A mutex is one owner at a time and must be unlocked by its owner. How do you stop a thread cleanly? Cooperatively: ask it to stop and let it return. C++20 std::jthread passes a std::stop_token that the loop checks, and its destructor requests the stop and joins. There is no safe forced kill. When is a parallel algorithm worth using, and what must you watch? For large inputs and non-trivial work per element. The callback must be thread-safe, reduce needs an associative operation, and small ranges are slower because of thread overhead. Without a parallel backend the call runs serially."},{"f":"18-atomics-memory-model.html","n":"Atomics and the Memory Model","id":"ops","h":"Atomic operations and compare_exchange","x":"Atomic operations and compare_exchange std::atomic<T> makes operations on one variable indivisible, usually without blocking. The increment is now one step: #include std::atomic counter{0}; counter++; // one indivisible read-modify-write counter.fetch_add(1); // same thing, explicit int v = counter.load(); // atomic read counter.store(5); // atomic write int old = counter.exchange(9); // write 9, get the previous value, all in one step std::atomic stop{false}; // a very common use: a shutdown flag Use an atomic for Do not use it for a counter, flag, swapped pointer, statistics an invariant over several variables: use a mutex Compare-and-swap (CAS) compare_exchange(expected, desired) : \"if the value still equals expected , set it to desired ; otherwise load the current value into expected and fail\". It is the building block of every lock-free update. int expected = x.load(); while (!x.compare_exchange_weak(expected, expected * 2)) { // someone changed x: `expected` now holds the current value, so recompute and retry } compare_exchange_weak compare_exchange_strong spurious failure may fail even when the value matched fails only if the value really differed use inside a retry loop (cheaper on some CPUs) a single attempt, no loop Compiled: a CAS with a stale expected returned false and refreshed expected to the current value (99). atomic_flag and a spinlock struct Spinlock { std::atomic_flag f = ATOMIC_FLAG_INIT; void lock() { while (f.test_and_set(std::memory_order_acquire)) { /* spin */ } } void unlock() { f.clear(std::memory_order_release); } }; atomic_flag is the one type guaranteed lock-free. Four threads each doing 50,000 increments under this spinlock reached exactly 200,000 (compiled). A spinlock burns a core while waiting: fine for tiny critical sections on dedicated cores, bad when the lock holder can be descheduled (use a mutex). volatile is NOT thread-safe It only stops the compiler optimising an access away. No atomicity, no ordering. Use std::atomic . is_lock_free() says whether the type is done with real atomic instructions; big structs fall back to a hidden lock. ABA problem: a CAS can succeed when the value went A to B and back to A, although the world changed meanwhile. Fix with a version counter next to the value, or hazard pointers. Read more Effective Modern C++ (Meyers) Item 40, Use std::atomic for concurrency, volatile for special memory (PDF pages 289 to 297) C++ Concurrency in Action (Williams) 5.2 Atomic operations and types in C++ (atomic_flag, atomic<bool>, pointers, integers, free functions) (PDF pages 151 to 164) 7.1 to 7.3 Lock-free data structures: definitions, a lock-free stack, memory reclamation, the ABA problem (PDF pages 229 to 274)"},{"f":"18-atomics-memory-model.html","n":"Atomics and the Memory Model","id":"ordering","h":"Memory orders and happens-before","x":"Memory orders and happens-before Compilers and CPUs reorder memory operations, and each core has buffers and caches, so one thread's write may become visible to another thread later , or after a write that came after it. A plain int gives no guarantee. Atomics let you say how much ordering you need. The one rule to learn: release and acquire int data = 0; std::atomic ready{false}; // producer thread data = 42; ready.store(true, std::memory_order_release); // consumer thread while (!ready.load(std::memory_order_acquire)) { } std::cout A release store publishes: every write the thread made before it becomes visible to any thread that reads the stored value with an acquire load . The release/acquire pair synchronizes-with , and that creates a happens-before edge: data = 42 happens before the consumer's read, so the read is not a data race and sees 42. happens-before is the formal \"must be visible\" relation. Within one thread it is program order; across threads it comes only from synchronization (atomics, mutexes, join , futures). Try it. Flush the producer's writes to memory in different orders and see what the consumer prints: The orders Order Guarantee Use relaxed only atomicity (and a single total order for that variable) counters and statistics nobody synchronizes on acquire (loads) nothing after it moves before it; sees what the matching release published reading a flag or pointer that publishes data release (stores) nothing before it moves after it; publishes earlier writes setting that flag or pointer acq_rel (read-modify-write) both at once fetch_sub on a reference count, a lock hand-over seq_cst (default) acquire/release plus one global order of all seq_cst operations that every thread agrees on when in doubt: it is the only order that is easy to reason about x86 hides bugs x86 already gives release/acquire for ordinary loads and stores, so wrong orderings often \"work\" on a developer's PC and fail on ARM (phones, Apple silicon, many servers). The simulator above shows what the C++ model allows , not what x86 does. Both sides must take part: a release store read by a relaxed load publishes nothing. A fence ( std::atomic_thread_fence ) gives the ordering without tying it to one variable. Know it exists; reach for acquire/release on the atomic first. Without any synchronization, two threads touching the same non-atomic variable (one writing) is a data race: undefined behaviour , not merely \"stale data\". Key point \"What does acquire/release guarantee?\" Everything written before the release is visible after the matching acquire. Say synchronizes-with and happens-before , and that seq_cst is the safe default. Read more C++ Concurrency in Action (Williams) 5.1 Memory model basics (objects, memory locations, modification orders) (PDF pages 148 to 150) 5.3 Synchronizing operations and enforcing ordering (synchronizes-with, happens-before, the memory orders, release sequences, fences) (PDF pages 165 to 196)"},{"f":"18-atomics-memory-model.html","n":"Atomics and the Memory Model","id":"patterns","h":"Ordering patterns that matter","x":"Ordering patterns that matter Pattern Orders Why Publish with a flag or pointer store release , load acquire the data written before the store is visible after the load Statistics counter relaxed fetch_add only the final total matters; no other data depends on it Reference count increment relaxed , decrement acq_rel adding a reference needs no ordering; the thread that drops the last one must see every other thread's writes before it deletes Spinlock lock acquire , unlock release a critical section must not leak out of the lock in either direction Shutdown flag relaxed if nothing else depends on it, else release / acquire a flag alone only needs to be seen eventually SPSC queue indices own index relaxed , other's acquire , publish release see the ring buffer in Performance & Memory struct Counted { // like shared_ptr's control block std::atomic refs{1}; void addRef() { refs.fetch_add(1, std::memory_order_relaxed); } void release() { if (refs.fetch_sub(1, std::memory_order_acq_rel) == 1) // the last one out sees everyone's writes... delete this; // ...before destroying the object } }; Compiled: five owners on four threads, the object was destroyed exactly once; a 2,000-round release/acquire publish test read a wrong value 0 times. Double-checked locking (the classic trap) std::atomic inst{nullptr}; std::mutex m; Widget* get() { Widget* p = inst.load(std::memory_order_acquire); // 1st check, no lock if (!p) { std::lock_guard lk(m); p = inst.load(std::memory_order_relaxed); // 2nd check, under the lock if (!p) { p = new Widget; inst.store(p, std::memory_order_release); } // publish a FULLY built object } return p; } Without the release/acquire pair, another thread could see the pointer before the constructor's writes. In practice use a function-local static or std::call_once and let the compiler do this. Be honest about what you know Start with a mutex or seq_cst , measure, and weaken orders only when you can say which happens-before edge each one provides. \"I use acquire/release for publication, relaxed for counters\" is a sound rule of thumb. Read more C++ Concurrency in Action (Williams) 5.3 Synchronizing operations and enforcing ordering (synchronizes-with, happens-before, the memory orders, release sequences, fences) (PDF pages 165 to 196)"},{"f":"18-atomics-memory-model.html","n":"Atomics and the Memory Model","id":"structures","h":"Thread-safe data structures","x":"Thread-safe data structures Three levels, in the order you should try them: Level How Example 1. One coarse lock a mutex around the whole structure a stack or map with a mutex (see the interface races in Multithreading ) 2. Fine-grained locks different mutexes for different parts, so threads that touch different parts do not wait a queue with a head mutex and a tail mutex; a hash table with one mutex per bucket 3. Lock-free atomics and CAS loops, no mutex a Treiber stack, an SPSC ring buffer Fine-grained: a queue with two locks template class TwoLockQueue { struct N { std::shared_ptr data; std::unique_ptr next; }; std::mutex headM_, tailM_; // producers take tailM_, consumers take headM_ std::unique_ptr head_; N* tail_; public: TwoLockQueue() : head_(new N), tail_(head_.get()) {} // a DUMMY node: head and tail never meet on the same node void push(T v); // locks tailM_ only std::optional tryPop(); // locks headM_ only (and tailM_ briefly to read the tail) }; The dummy node is the trick: with it, a push only touches the tail and a pop only the head, so one producer and one consumer never block each other. Compiled: a producer pushing 1 to 1000 and a consumer popping them summed to 500500. Read-mostly: a lookup table A price table read by many threads and updated rarely: std::shared_mutex with shared_lock for readers and unique_lock for writers ( Other mutex types ). Return copies or small values, never references into the table. Lock-free: a stack push struct Node { int value; Node* next; }; std::atomic head{nullptr}; void push(int v) { Node* n = new Node{v, head.load(std::memory_order_relaxed)}; while (!head.compare_exchange_weak(n->next, n, std::memory_order_release, std::memory_order_relaxed)) {} // on failure compare_exchange_weak reloads n->next with the current head: just retry } Compiled: four threads pushing 1000 nodes each gave 4000 nodes, no lock. Pop is the hard part : another thread may pop and delete the node you are reading ( memory reclamation ), and the ABA problem can corrupt the list. Real implementations need hazard pointers, epochs or a counted pointer. Lock-free is not faster by default A contended CAS loop can be slower than a mutex, and a bug shows up once a month on a customer machine. Use a mutex first, measure, and use a proven library (or the SPSC ring when one producer and one consumer is enough) before writing your own. Wait-free (every thread finishes in a bounded number of steps) is stronger than lock-free (some thread always makes progress) which is stronger than obstruction-free . Design the interface first: whole operations ( tryPop returning an optional ), not empty() then pop() . Read more C++ Concurrency in Action (Williams) 6.2 Lock-based concurrent data structures (a thread-safe stack and queue, fine-grained locks) (PDF pages 199 to 216) 7.1 to 7.3 Lock-free data structures: definitions, a lock-free stack, memory reclamation, the ABA problem (PDF pages 229 to 274)"},{"f":"18-atomics-memory-model.html","n":"Atomics and the Memory Model","id":"cheat","h":"Cheat sheet","x":"Cheat sheet Say each answer aloud, then open it. std::atomic vs mutex? An atomic makes operations on a single variable indivisible, usually lock-free and cheap. A mutex protects any critical section, including invariants over several variables, but may block. Use atomics for counters and flags, mutexes for compound state. Is volatile a substitute for atomic ? No. volatile prevents some compiler optimisations but gives no atomicity and no inter-thread ordering. Using it for synchronization is a data race. What are the memory orders? seq_cst (default, strongest, one global order), acquire / release (pair a release store with an acquire load to publish data), acq_rel for read-modify-write, and relaxed (atomicity only). Start with the default; weaken only with a reason and a clear happens-before argument. What does release/acquire guarantee? Everything the producer wrote before a release store is visible to a thread that reads the stored value with an acquire load. The pair synchronizes-with, which gives a happens-before edge, so the earlier plain writes are not a data race. What does happens-before mean? If A happens-before B, then A's effects are visible to B. Within a thread it is program order; between threads it exists only through synchronization: atomics with matching orders, mutex unlock/lock, thread start and join, futures. Two conflicting accesses without a happens-before relation are a data race. When is relaxed enough? When no other data depends on the atomic: a statistics counter, an id generator, a shutdown flag that carries no data. The final value is correct, but it orders nothing else. Why can code with a wrong memory order work on x86 and fail on ARM? x86's hardware already orders ordinary loads and stores like release/acquire, so missing orderings are hidden. ARM and POWER reorder far more, so the same code can read stale data there. The C++ model, not the PC you tested on, defines correctness. compare_exchange_weak vs strong ? Both set a value only if it still equals the expected one. weak may fail spuriously, so it belongs in a retry loop and is cheaper on some CPUs; strong fails only on a real mismatch and suits a single attempt. On failure they load the current value into expected . What is the ABA problem? A CAS succeeds because the value is A again, but it went A to B to A in between, and the structure around it changed (for example a node was freed and reused). Fix with a version counter beside the pointer, or hazard pointers or epoch reclamation. When is a spinlock appropriate? For very short critical sections on dedicated cores where waiting is shorter than a context switch. It burns CPU while waiting and is dangerous if the holder can be descheduled. Otherwise use a mutex. How is a reference count made thread-safe, and with which orders? With an atomic counter: increments can be relaxed, the decrement is acq_rel so the thread that drops it to zero sees all other threads' writes before it destroys the object. shared_ptr 's control block works this way. Mutex, fine-grained locks or lock-free: how do you choose? Start with one mutex and measure. Split locks if contention shows up and the structure has independent parts (head and tail of a queue, buckets of a table). Go lock-free only for a proven bottleneck and with a known-correct design, because memory reclamation and ABA make it hard; an SPSC ring buffer is the one case simple enough to write confidently."},{"f":"11-wire-formats.html","n":"Binary Data and Parsing","id":"s1","h":"From bytes to values","x":"From bytes to values bytes as they arrive (19-byte Sample message) 'T' length seq (4 bytes) amount (8 bytes) qty (4 bytes) copy out, fix byte order, check the length Sample { seq, valueUnits, qty } A buffer holds raw bytes; your program wants typed values. A network message arrives as bytes. The job: turn them into values quickly, correctly and without undefined behavior . Never cast the buffer to a struct pointer. Copy fields out instead. Check the length before every read. A truncated or corrupt packet must be rejected, not trusted. Convert byte order explicitly. The network and your CPU may disagree. The tempting wrong way auto* t = reinterpret_cast (buf); // reads fields straight out of the buffer std::cout seq; That one line hides four problems: aliasing (undefined behavior), alignment (the buffer address may not suit a Sample ), padding (the struct layout may not match the wire) and byte order . The next sections take them one by one."},{"f":"11-wire-formats.html","n":"Binary Data and Parsing","id":"s2","h":"Integers and bit manipulation","x":"Integers and bit manipulation flags = 0x17 = 0001 0111 0 0 0 1 0 1 1 1 bit 7 6 5 4 3 2 1 bit 0 unused channel = 101 = 5 urgent ack Read the channel: (flags >> 2) & 0x7 shift the field down, then mask its width. Protocols pack several fields into one byte; bit operations unpack them. Operation Expression set bit n x | (1u << n) clear bit n x & ~(1u << n) toggle bit n x ^ (1u << n) test bit n (x >> n) & 1u power of two? x != 0 && (x & (x - 1)) == 0 count set bits std::popcount(x) (C++20), or loop x &= x - 1 (clears the lowest set bit each time) constexpr std::uint8_t kAck = 1u > 2) & 0x7u; } std::uint8_t flags = kAck | kUrgent | (5u Rules that bite Use fixed-width types ( std::uint8_t , std::uint32_t , std::int64_t ) for anything on the wire; int and long change size between platforms ( long is 32-bit on Windows). Unsigned overflow wraps (well defined); signed overflow is undefined behavior . Shifting by the type's width or more is undefined: write 1u << n with n < 32 . Do bit work on unsigned types. Small types are promoted to int in arithmetic. A plain char may be signed, so widening char(0xFF) to int gives -1 : read bytes as unsigned char or std::uint8_t . Predict Promotion and wrap-around std::uint8_t a = 200, b = 100; std::cout 300 44 a + b is computed as an int (both promote), so it is 300 . Storing it back into a uint8_t keeps only the low 8 bits: 300 mod 256 = 44 . Read more C++ Primer (Lippman, Lajoie, Moo) 4.8 The bitwise operators (PDF pages 181 to 184) 17.2 The bitset type (PDF pages 752 to 756)"},{"f":"11-wire-formats.html","n":"Binary Data and Parsing","id":"s3","h":"Struct layout, alignment and padding","x":"Struct layout, alignment and padding struct { char a; int b; char c; } 12 bytes a padding b (4 bytes) c padding offset 0 4 8 12 struct { int b; char a; char c; } 8 bytes b (4 bytes) a c padding Same members, 33% smaller: put the largest members first. The compiler inserts padding so every member sits at a multiple of its alignment. Each type has an alignment ( alignof ): an int must start at a multiple of 4, a double or int64_t at a multiple of 8. The compiler pads between members to guarantee that, and pads the end so that arrays of the struct stay aligned. So sizeof is often bigger than the sum of the members. Step through the layout: struct Careless { char a; int b; char c; }; // 12 bytes struct Ordered { int b; char a; char c; }; // 8 bytes static_assert(sizeof(Careless) == 12 && alignof(Careless) == 4); static_assert(offsetof(Careless, b) == 4); // offsetof gives the byte offset of a member Packing, and why not to rely on it #pragma pack(push, 1) struct Packed { char a; int b; char c; }; // 6 bytes, no padding: matches a wire layout #pragma pack(pop) Packing is not free A packed member may be unaligned : slower on some CPUs, a fault on others, and taking a reference or pointer to it is unsafe. Do not read the wire into a packed struct and use it directly. Read the bytes with memcpy (next sections) and keep ordinary structs inside your program. static_assert(sizeof(...)) is a cheap guard against accidental layout changes."},{"f":"11-wire-formats.html","n":"Binary Data and Parsing","id":"s4","h":"Endianness","x":"Endianness value 0x12345678 stored at addresses +0 to +3 little-endian (x86, ARM) 78 56 34 12 big-endian = network byte order 12 34 56 78 +0 +1 +2 +3 Little-endian stores the least significant byte first, big-endian the most significant first. Same value, different byte order in memory. Endianness is the order of a multi-byte value's bytes in memory. Network protocols traditionally use big-endian (\"network byte order\"); most desktop and server CPUs are little-endian. Protocols differ, so read each protocol's specification. Convert at the boundary : when bytes enter the program, turn them into native values, and stay native inside. Tools: ntohl / htonl (sockets), std::endian::native (C++20) to test the CPU, std::byteswap (C++23). The portable way that works everywhere is to assemble the value from bytes with shifts : constexpr std::uint32_t readBe32(const unsigned char* p) { return (std::uint32_t{p[0]} Widen each byte to uint32_t before shifting, otherwise it promotes to a signed int and shifting a large value into the top bit is a trap. The same code works on little- and big-endian machines, and compilers turn it into a single load plus a byte swap."},{"f":"11-wire-formats.html","n":"Binary Data and Parsing","id":"s5","h":"Reading bytes safely: aliasing and memcpy","x":"Reading bytes safely: aliasing and memcpy Strict aliasing: you may access an object only through a pointer or reference of its own type (or a few related ones). Reading a uint32_t out of memory that holds something else is undefined behavior, and the optimizer is allowed to assume it never happens. Technique Defined? Notes *reinterpret_cast<const uint32_t*>(buf + 1) no aliasing violation, and buf + 1 is probably misaligned std::memcpy(&v, buf + 1, sizeof v) yes compilers turn it into a plain load; no alignment requirement on buf std::bit_cast<To>(from) (C++20) yes same size, trivially copyable types, e.g. float to its bits assemble with shifts ( readBe32 ) yes also fixes byte order write through a union and read another member no in C++ works as a GCC extension, but is not portable C++ view an object as unsigned char , char or std::byte yes the one exception: these may alias anything (but not the reverse) std::uint32_t v; std::memcpy(&v, buf + 1, sizeof v); // native byte order: add a swap if the wire is big-endian std::bit_cast (1.0f); // 0x3f800000: the bits of a float Key point \"Why is casting the receive buffer to a struct pointer wrong?\" Aliasing (UB), alignment, padding that may not match the wire, and byte order. \"What do you do instead?\" memcpy or shifts per field, after checking the length."},{"f":"11-wire-formats.html","n":"Binary Data and Parsing","id":"s6","h":"Zero-copy parsing","x":"Zero-copy parsing A zero-copy parser never copies the message: it keeps views ( std::string_view , std::span ) into the receive buffer and a cursor that advances. No allocation, no copying, no exceptions on the hot path. struct Field { int tag; std::string_view value; }; // \"1=D|2=north|3=12345|\" -> (1,\"D\") (2,\"north\") (3,\"12345\") std::optional nextField(std::string_view& rest) { auto bar = rest.find('|'); if (bar == std::string_view::npos) return std::nullopt; // incomplete: reject std::string_view item = rest.substr(0, bar); rest.remove_prefix(bar + 1); // advance the cursor auto eq = item.find('='); if (eq == std::string_view::npos) return std::nullopt; Field f{}; auto r = std::from_chars(item.data(), item.data() + eq, f.tag); if (r.ec != std::errc{}) return std::nullopt; f.value = item.substr(eq + 1); // a view: no copy return f; } std::from_chars (C++17) converts text to numbers with no allocation, no exceptions and no locale ; it returns an error code and the pointer where it stopped. Compare with stoi (throws, needs a std::string ) and stringstream (slow). Validate everything before trusting it: length fields, tags, digits. Corrupt input is normal; return std::nullopt or an error code instead of throwing. A view is valid only while the buffer is alive and unchanged . If the buffer is reused for the next packet, copy out what you need first. Step through the parse of a binary message (all integers big-endian):"},{"f":"11-wire-formats.html","n":"Binary Data and Parsing","id":"s7","h":"Money amounts: fixed-point versus double","x":"Money amounts: fixed-point versus double 0.1 + 0.2 == 0.3; // false 0.1 + 0.2; // 0.30000000000000004 double cannot represent most decimal fractions exactly, so sums and comparisons drift. For money and amounts use an integer count of units : 101.2500 with a unit of 0.0001 is 1012500 . class Money { std::int64_t units_; // 1 unit = 0.0001 public: static constexpr std::int64_t kScale = 10000; constexpr Money operator+(Money o) const { return Money(units_ + o.units_); } constexpr auto operator (const Money&) const = default; // exact comparison, no epsilon static std::optional parse(std::string_view s); // \"101.25\" -> 1012500, rejects a 5th decimal }; // 0.1 + 0.2 == 0.3 is true here: 1000 + 2000 == 3000 Exact and deterministic : the same inputs give the same bits on every machine, and equality needs no epsilon. Parse from text directly into units; never go through double . Watch overflow : int64_t holds about 9.2e18 units (9.2e14 amount units at 4 decimals). Multiplying amount by quantity can exceed that: check, or use a wider type ( __int128 in GCC and Clang). Decide the rounding rule (down, half-up, half-even) once, and apply it in one place."},{"f":"11-wire-formats.html","n":"Binary Data and Parsing","id":"s8","h":"Sequence numbers and gaps","x":"Sequence numbers and gaps seq 100 seq 101 102 missing seq 103 expected 102, got 103: GAP in order: expected = got + 1 Each message carries a sequence number; a jump means something was lost. Received versus expected Meaning Typical action equal in order process, expect seq + 1 higher gap : messages were lost buffer new messages, request a retransmission or a snapshot lower duplicate or old ignore enum class SeqResult { InOrder, Duplicate, Gap }; SeqResult classify(std::uint32_t expected, std::uint32_t received) { auto diff = static_cast (received - expected); // unsigned subtraction wraps, then read it as signed if (diff == 0) return SeqResult::InOrder; return diff Wrap-around: a 32-bit counter returns to 0 after 4294967295. Comparing with < breaks at that point; the unsigned subtraction followed by a signed reinterpretation keeps working as long as the two values are within 2 31 of each other. Expecting 4294967295 and receiving 0 is a gap of one , not a duplicate."},{"f":"11-wire-formats.html","n":"Binary Data and Parsing","id":"s9","h":"Cheat sheet","x":"Cheat sheet Answer aloud, then open. Why not cast the receive buffer to a struct pointer? Four problems at once: it violates strict aliasing (undefined behavior), the buffer address may not be aligned for the struct, struct padding may not match the wire layout, and the byte order may differ. Check the length, then copy each field out with memcpy or assemble it with shifts. What is struct padding and how do you reduce it? The compiler inserts unused bytes so each member sits at a multiple of its alignment, and pads the end so arrays stay aligned. sizeof can exceed the sum of the members. Order members from largest to smallest alignment; use static_assert(sizeof(...)) to guard the size. What does #pragma pack(1) do, and what does it cost? It removes padding so the struct matches a wire layout. The cost: members may be unaligned, which is slower or faults on some CPUs, and references or pointers to them are unsafe. Prefer reading fields with memcpy . What is endianness? Which does the network use? The order of a multi-byte value's bytes in memory. Little-endian stores the least significant byte first (x86, ARM), big-endian the most significant first. Network byte order is big-endian, though a given protocol may differ, so check the spec. Convert at the boundary. How do you read a big-endian 32-bit value portably? Assemble it from the bytes with shifts, widening each byte to uint32_t first: (uint32_t{p[0]} << 24) | (uint32_t{p[1]} << 16) | (uint32_t{p[2]} << 8) | uint32_t{p[3]} . It works on any CPU. What is strict aliasing? The rule that an object may only be accessed through an lvalue of its own type (or a few related types); the compiler assumes this when optimizing. Exceptions: char , unsigned char and std::byte may alias anything. Use memcpy or std::bit_cast to reinterpret bytes. memcpy , bit_cast or a union for type punning? memcpy and std::bit_cast (C++20) are well defined and compile to a plain load. Reading a different union member than was written is undefined behavior in C++, even though GCC allows it as an extension. How do you set, clear and test a bit, and check for a power of two? Set x | (1u << n) ; clear x & ~(1u << n) ; test (x >> n) & 1 . Power of two: x != 0 && (x & (x - 1)) == 0 . Use unsigned types and keep n below the width. Why should amounts not be double ? Binary floating point cannot represent most decimals exactly ( 0.1 + 0.2 != 0.3 ), so sums and comparisons drift. Store an integer count of units (a scaled int64_t ): exact, deterministic, and comparable with == . Parse text straight into units and watch for overflow when multiplying by quantity. Why std::from_chars in a parser? It converts text to numbers without allocating, throwing or consulting the locale, and reports errors through a return value plus the position where it stopped. stoi throws and needs a std::string ; stringstream is slow. How do you detect a gap in sequence numbers, and handle wrap-around? Compare the received number with the expected one: equal is in order, higher is a gap (lost messages: buffer and request a retransmission or snapshot), lower is a duplicate. For wrap-around compute static_cast<int32_t>(received - expected) and look at its sign."},{"f":"12-low-latency.html","n":"Performance and Memory","id":"s1","h":"Where latency comes from","x":"Where latency comes from Socket bytes arrive Reader thread parse the bytes SPSC queue Processor update the state Consumer consume results system calls, copies allocation, exceptions locks, false sharing cache misses, map lookups virtual calls, logging Every stage must be fast AND predictable. One occasional 50 microsecond stall anywhere shows up at the end of the pipeline. A hot path is a chain; the slowest rare event in any link is what you feel. Latency is the time for one message to get through; throughput is how many per second. A system can have great throughput and terrible latency. In a message pipeline the tail matters: the rare slow case (once in a thousand) is what causes a stale result or a missed deadline. Average time hides it. So the goal is not only \"fast\" but predictable : remove anything whose cost can suddenly jump. Source Rough cost (orders of magnitude) Remedy L1 cache hit versus main memory about 1 ns versus about 100 ns contiguous data, small hot structs Heap allocation ( new ) tens of ns, but unbounded in the worst case (locks, page faults) preallocate, pools, arenas Mutex contention, context switch microseconds single-writer queues, pinning System call (read, write, log) hundreds of ns to microseconds batch, keep off the hot path Page fault microseconds pre-touch memory at startup Throwing an exception microseconds error codes, optional The numbers vary a lot by CPU and system; treat them as ratios. The rule that matters: measure first , then remove the biggest source."},{"f":"12-low-latency.html","n":"Performance and Memory","id":"s2","h":"Measuring: percentiles and tail latency","x":"Measuring: percentiles and tail latency 99.9 % about 200 ns 0.1 % about 80 µs 400 times slower, but rare mean 281 ns looks healthy. p50 200 ns. p99 219 ns. p99.9 80,004 ns. The average hides the tail. (Numbers from the program in 12-low-latency.) Never judge by the average. Report percentiles : p50 (median), p99, p99.9, and the maximum. p99.9 means 1 in 1000 operations is at least that slow. Collect every sample (do not average on the fly), sort afterwards, read the percentile by rank. std::uint64_t percentile(const std::vector & sorted, double p) { std::size_t rank = static_cast (p / 100.0 * sorted.size()); return sorted[std::min(rank, sorted.size() - 1)]; } auto t0 = std::chrono::steady_clock::now(); // monotonic: never goes backwards doWork(); auto t1 = std::chrono::steady_clock::now(); samples.push_back(duration_cast (t1 - t0).count()); // reserve() the vector first Measuring pitfalls The compiler deletes unused work. If a benchmark's result is never used, the loop may be optimized away. Store results into a volatile sink, or print a checksum. Warm up. The first iterations pay for cold caches and page faults. Discard them, or report them separately. Do not disturb what you measure: no printing or allocation inside the timed region, and the clock call itself costs tens of nanoseconds. Measure in realistic conditions (real message rates, other threads running). A quiet benchmark hides the stalls that matter."},{"f":"12-low-latency.html","n":"Performance and Memory","id":"s3","h":"Memory on the hot path","x":"Memory on the hot path new and malloc can take a lock, walk free lists, call into the kernel for more memory and fragment the heap. Usually fast, but occasionally slow and unpredictable . The rule: allocate everything at startup; the hot path never calls new . Preallocate containers: reserve() a vector so it never reallocates (one allocation instead of several as it grows). Object pool: a fixed array of slots plus a free list . Allocation and release are a few instructions; if the pool is exhausted, you decide what to do (drop, count, wait). Arena ( std::pmr::monotonic_buffer_resource ): allocation is \"bump a pointer\" inside a buffer you provide; everything is released at once. Also: std::array and fixed buffers instead of std::string and std::vector for small data, and no std::function capturing large state (it may allocate). Step through a pool: template class Pool { union Slot { alignas(T) unsigned char storage[sizeof(T)]; std::size_t nextFree; }; std::array slots_; std::size_t freeHead_ = 0; // first free slot; N means none public: Pool() { for (std::size_t i = 0; i T* allocate(Args&&... args) { if (freeHead_ == N) return nullptr; // exhausted: the caller decides Slot& s = slots_[freeHead_]; freeHead_ = s.nextFree; // pop the free list return new (s.storage) T(std::forward (args)...); // placement new: construct in place } void deallocate(T* p) { p->~T(); auto* s = reinterpret_cast (p); s->nextFree = freeHead_; // push the slot back freeHead_ = s - slots_.data(); } }; Placement new constructs an object in memory that already exists, so no allocation happens. The program in 12-low-latency replaces operator new with a counter and shows 0 heap allocations on the pool path, 0 for a pmr::vector in an arena, and 1 for a vector with reserve(100) . alignas(Task) unsigned char storage[sizeof(Task)]; // raw, correctly aligned memory: no object yet Task* o = new (storage) Task(7); // placement new: construct INTO this memory, nothing is allocated // ... use *o ... o->~Task(); // you must call the destructor yourself: never delete it The address of o equals the address of storage (compiled). The memory must be aligned and big enough. This is how std::vector , std::optional and every pool build objects in storage they already own. C++20 std::construct_at does the same without the new syntax. Read more C++ Primer (Lippman, Lajoie, Moo) 19.1 Controlling memory allocation (PDF pages 849 to 853)"},{"f":"12-low-latency.html","n":"Performance and Memory","id":"s4","h":"Cache-friendly data","x":"Cache-friendly data Array of structs: 40-byte records, a loop reads only the low low high qty time name low high qty time name low high qty time name 1 record per 64-byte cache line: 8 useful bytes out of every 40 loaded. Struct of arrays: the lows sit together low low low low low low low low 8 lows per cache line: every loaded byte is used. The CPU loads whole 64-byte cache lines, not single fields. A cache line is 64 bytes. A cache miss to main memory costs about a hundred times a hit, so locality dominates: data used together should sit together. Contiguous beats linked. std::vector and std::array stream through memory; std::list and std::map chase pointers to nodes scattered across the heap (each node is also a separate allocation). AoS versus SoA. Array of structs keeps one record together (good when you use most fields); struct of arrays keeps each field together (good when a loop uses one or two fields). The program shows 40,000 bytes streamed to read all lows with AoS and 8,000 with SoA, with identical results. Hot/cold split: keep fields used on every message together and small; move rarely used fields elsewhere (struct size and padding: Binary Data and Parsing ). False sharing Two threads writing different variables on the same cache line make the line bounce between cores. Give each its own line: alignas(64) std::atomic<size_t> head_; alignas(64) std::atomic<size_t> tail_; ( sizeof grows from 16 to 128 bytes, which is the point). Introduced in Multithreading ."},{"f":"12-low-latency.html","n":"Performance and Memory","id":"s5","h":"Dense keys: an array instead of a map","x":"Dense keys: an array instead of a map count per bucket, index = key - base 120 0 340 75 500 0 0 index 0 1 2 3 4 highest non-empty bucket Update = one array write. When the highest bucket empties, scan down to the next one. A bounded key range turns a map into a flat array. When the keys are integers in a small, known range (histogram buckets, status codes, small scores, sensor ids), a plain array indexed by key - base beats a std::map or a hash map. The operations are always the same: update a bucket, find the highest non-empty one, look up an item by id . Structure Update Highest key Trade-offs std::map<key, count> O(log n) O(1) ( begin() or rbegin() ) simple and sorted, but a heap node per key (allocation, pointer chasing) sorted std::vector O(n) shift O(1) contiguous; fine when there are few keys array indexed by key O(1) O(1) tracked, rescan on empty no allocation, cache-friendly; needs a bounded key range and memory proportional to it hash map id -> (key, count) O(1) average n/a for lookup by id ; an open-addressing table avoids per-node allocation class BucketTable { static constexpr std::int64_t kBuckets = 4096; std::int64_t base_; // key of index 0 std::array count_{}; std::int64_t highest_ = -1; // -1 when empty public: void add(std::int64_t key, std::uint64_t n) { auto i = key - base_; count_[i] += n; if (i > highest_) highest_ = i; // O(1) } void remove(std::int64_t key, std::uint64_t n) { auto i = key - base_; count_[i] = n >= count_[i] ? 0 : count_[i] - n; if (count_[i] == 0 && i == highest_) // the highest bucket vanished: scan down while (highest_ >= 0 && count_[highest_] == 0) --highest_; } }; The program runs 200,000 random adds and removes against both this array and a std::map , and checks the highest key after every operation: 0 mismatches. A key outside the range needs a fallback (a re-centred range or an overflow structure)."},{"f":"12-low-latency.html","n":"Performance and Memory","id":"s6","h":"Queues between threads","x":"Queues between threads Pipeline stages run on separate threads and hand messages over a queue. With one producer and one consumer (SPSC) no lock is needed: each index is written by exactly one thread ( Multithreading ). template class SpscRing { static_assert((N & (N - 1)) == 0, \"capacity must be a power of two\"); T buf_[N]; alignas(64) std::atomic head_{0}; // consumer writes alignas(64) std::atomic tail_{0}; // producer writes public: bool tryPush(const T& v) { auto t = tail_.load(std::memory_order_relaxed); if (t - head_.load(std::memory_order_acquire) == N) return false; // full buf_[t & (N - 1)] = v; // mask instead of %: the counters just keep counting up tail_.store(t + 1, std::memory_order_release); return true; } bool tryPop(T& out) { auto h = head_.load(std::memory_order_relaxed); if (h == tail_.load(std::memory_order_acquire)) return false; // empty out = buf_[h & (N - 1)]; head_.store(h + 1, std::memory_order_release); return true; } }; Power-of-two capacity turns the wrap-around into index & (N - 1) , one instruction, with free-running counters. Padded indices ( alignas(64) ) so the two threads do not fight over one cache line. Always bounded. An unbounded queue turns a slow consumer into growing memory and growing delay. When the queue is full you choose a policy : Policy Behavior Use when block (back-pressure) the producer waits nothing may be lost, and the producer can afford to wait (not for a network reader that must keep up) drop newest tryPush returns false, count it simple; the consumer will need a recovery drop oldest overwrite the oldest entry only the latest data matters conflate keep only the latest update per key slow consumers of status updates The program runs two real threads over a 1024-slot ring with a drop-newest policy: received + dropped = 200000 and the consumer's sum equals the producer's, in 20 of 20 runs."},{"f":"12-low-latency.html","n":"Performance and Memory","id":"s7","h":"A predictable hot path","x":"A predictable hot path Besides memory and queues, a few language features add unpredictable cost. Keep them off the hot path: Avoid on the hot path Why Use instead exceptions ( throw ) a throw costs microseconds and its cost varies; unwinding tables add size error codes, std::optional , a small result struct; validate at the boundary virtual calls an indirect call: no inlining, harder for the CPU to predict templates, CRTP, std::variant with std::visit (decide the type at compile time) locks ( std::mutex ) contention means waiting and context switches single-writer data, SPSC queues, atomics logging and I/O system calls, formatting, locks inside the logger push a small record onto a queue; a background thread formats and writes std::function , shared_ptr possible allocation, an indirect call, an atomic reference count templates or function pointers, unique_ptr or plain ownership std::map , std::list , std::string building allocation per node or per string arrays, pools, fixed buffers, string_view enum class ParseError { None, Truncated, BadType, BadLength }; ParseError parseSample(const unsigned char* buf, std::size_t len, Sample& out) noexcept { if (len Mark functions that cannot throw noexcept ; it lets the compiler skip unwinding code. constexpr lookup tables move work to compile time; [[likely]] / [[unlikely]] (C++20) hint at the common path. These rules are for the hot path . Startup, configuration and error reporting can use exceptions and heap freely."},{"f":"12-low-latency.html","n":"Performance and Memory","id":"s8","h":"Threads, cores and busy-polling","x":"Threads, cores and busy-polling Pin each thread to a core (CPU affinity). The OS stops migrating it, so its caches stay warm and its timing steady. Give hot threads their own cores, and do not run more busy threads than cores ( oversubscription causes context switches). Busy-poll instead of blocking. A thread that sleeps on a condition variable pays a wake-up delay (microseconds) when work arrives. A thread that spins checking a flag reacts almost instantly but uses 100% of its core . Latency-critical threads spin; others block. Memory placement: on multi-socket machines keep a thread and its data on the same NUMA node. Pre-touch (page-fault in) buffers at startup so the first use on the hot path does not take a page fault. Isolating cores from the scheduler and tuning interrupts is an operating-system job, usually done by the platform team. Locks on the hot path Problem What happens Remedy Lock contention threads queue on one mutex, so the program is effectively serial (Amdahl) shorter critical sections, shard the data, per-thread data merged at the end Priority inversion a low-priority thread holds a lock a high-priority one needs, and a medium one keeps the low one from running priority inheritance, shorter locks, no locks shared across priorities Context switches blocking and waking a thread costs microseconds and cold caches brief spinning, pin threads to cores, keep waits off the hot path Allocation under contention malloc may lock; latency is unpredictable reserve, pools and ring buffers // busy-poll: spin on an atomic instead of sleeping (burns the core, minimal wake-up delay) while (!ready.load(std::memory_order_acquire)) { _mm_pause(); // x86 hint: be nice to the sibling hyperthread } // Linux only: pin the calling thread to core 3 cpu_set_t set; CPU_ZERO(&set); CPU_SET(3, &set); pthread_setaffinity_np(pthread_self(), sizeof(set), &set); Key point \"How would you design a program that processes a fast stream of messages?\" Reader thread pinned to a core, reading the socket and parsing with zero copy into preallocated structures; an SPSC queue to a processing thread, which updates array-based tables and publishes; no allocation, locks, exceptions or logging on the path; bounded queues with a stated drop or recovery policy; sequence-number gap detection; and p99.9 latency measured under realistic load. Read more C++ Concurrency in Action (Williams) 8.2 Factors affecting the performance of concurrent code (cache ping-pong, false sharing, oversubscription) (PDF pages 283 to 289) 8.3 Designing data structures for multithreaded performance (PDF pages 289 to 292)"},{"f":"12-low-latency.html","n":"Performance and Memory","id":"s9","h":"Cheat sheet","x":"Cheat sheet Answer aloud, then open. Why does tail latency matter more than the average in a message pipeline? The rare slow case (p99.9) is what produces a stale result or a missed deadline, and the average hides it: a mean of 281 ns can coexist with a p99.9 of 80 microseconds. Measure and report percentiles, and remove sources of unpredictable delay. Why avoid new on the hot path, and what do you do instead? The allocator can take a lock, walk free lists, fragment and call the kernel, so its cost is unpredictable. Allocate at startup: reserve containers, use an object pool or an arena ( std::pmr::monotonic_buffer_resource ), and use fixed buffers instead of strings and vectors for small data. How does an object pool work? A fixed array of slots, plus a free list threaded through the free slots. Allocation pops the head of the list and constructs the object there with placement new; deallocation destroys it and pushes the slot back. Both are a few instructions with no heap, and when the pool is empty the caller decides what to do. Array of structs versus struct of arrays? AoS keeps one record's fields together, good when you use most fields. SoA keeps each field in its own array, good when a loop uses one or two fields: it streams only the bytes it needs, so each cache line is fully useful. Same results, less memory traffic. What is false sharing and how do you fix it? Two threads writing different variables on the same 64-byte cache line force the line to bounce between cores. Give each variable its own line with alignas(64) or padding, as for the head and tail indices of an SPSC queue. Why is a vector or array usually faster than std::map or std::list ? Contiguous memory is cache-friendly and prefetchable; node-based containers chase pointers across the heap and allocate per node. Big-O hides these constants, especially for small and medium sizes. When would you use an array indexed by key instead of a map? When the keys are integers in a bounded range: updates are O(1) with no allocation and the data is contiguous, so it is cache-friendly. Track the highest or lowest non-empty bucket and rescan when it empties. A hash map from id to its bucket handles lookups by id. A std::map is simpler but allocates a node per key. Why is an SPSC ring capacity a power of two? The wrap-around becomes counter & (N - 1) instead of a modulo, one cheap instruction, and the head and tail counters can simply keep counting up while tail - head is the size. What do you do when a queue between threads is full? Never let it grow without bound. Choose a policy: block (back-pressure), drop the newest, drop the oldest, or conflate to the latest per key, count what was dropped, and recover from the gap if needed. A network reader normally cannot block. Why avoid exceptions, virtual calls, locks and logging on the hot path? Each adds cost that is large or unpredictable: a throw costs microseconds, a virtual call blocks inlining and is an indirect branch, a mutex can wait and context-switch, and logging means system calls and formatting. Use error codes, templates or CRTP, single-writer data, and an asynchronous logger fed by a queue. Busy-polling versus blocking, and why pin threads? Blocking saves CPU but a wake-up costs microseconds; busy-polling reacts almost immediately but burns a whole core, so only latency-critical threads spin. Pinning a thread to a core stops migration, which keeps its caches warm and its timing stable."}];
